{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war","id":1539224,"title":"Rethinking the Ascension War","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-07-25T10:22:44-07:00","id":1539224,"title":"Rethinking the Ascension War","content":"Okay, its time to reimagine Mage's history again. We played with this once before in <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1493716-alternative-traditions-origins\">Alternative Traditions Origins.</a> This time the idea is to throw out the current mix of Traditions and Conventions entirely. We are going to go back to the 1400's and figure out what the mystic world was like at the time, what kind of mages/mage equivalents existed at the time, the stakes and ideologies of the brewing Ascension War, etc.<br /> <br /> Unlike last time, I do not have a specific idea of who the major players are or which side (or sides) of the conflict they will fall on. Nor is there any need to make sure this ends up in the modern MtA we are familiar with. I'm more interested in who could be there and what their motivations are.<br /> <br /> Because this is such an open ended idea, though, I will bring in two ideas from the previous thread.<br /> <br /> 1) The Hermetic Schools - An loose association of scholars and professors piecing together mystical and non mystical insights from the vast and often contradictory libraries left behind by the Roman Empire. These guys look a lot like the typical academicians of the time because they are, there's just a much wider range of study than in our world.<br /> <br /> 2) The Wildlands - Also a product of the fall of Rome and the repeated invasions. Europe is filled with a mix of imported and often conflicting mythologies, monsters, and folkloric traditions and creatures. The Cities are largely untouched but the countryside has never quite stabilized.<br /> <br /> So, who and what would you expect to see?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T13:53:16-07:00","id":1539244,"title":"","content":"Just throwing something of a later date to the pot, today I was thinking about rainbows, and more specifically about the number of colors.<br /> <br /> Sir Isaac Newton is perhaps the ideal of the man of reason and science before the Industrial Revolution, developer of so many mathematical tools for use in physics, and...<br /> <br /> Decided a rainbow had seven colors because that was good numerology.<br /> <br /> My point is, the whole idea of a pure science based Technocracy is bullshit, so how could the Ascension War form itself in a world where the forefathers of Industrial Science are also &quot;Harry Potters&quot;?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T14:47:23-07:00","id":1539250,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539244#post1539244\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My point is, the whole idea of a pure science based Technocracy is bullshit, so how could the Ascension War form itself in a world where the forefathers of Industrial Science are also &quot;Harry Potters&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>Same way it worked in real life? Like, Newtonian became a byword for a kind of disenchanted mechanistic way of understanding the cosmos despite the man himself actually holding deeply mystical ideas about it. He just didn't make those public. Further, he's not the only one. Lots of scientists have held deeply weird and often idiosyncratic mystical ideas related to their work which they nevertheless keep to themselves.\u200b<br /> <br /> Now, in reality, that's probably no great lose. Like, I gather Newton's alchemical ideas were a bit dated for his time, and his religious stuff is basically just idiosyncratic radical Protestantism. But in Ascension, who knows? Maybe the inherent secrecy of metaphysical speculations is what's led to the Technocracy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T16:18:34-07:00","id":1539266,"title":"","content":"It seems to me that if we\u2019re looking at the 1400s, at least in Europe, a significant player has to be whatever Awakened factions align themselves with the hegemonic world religions (particularly Christianity &amp; Islam in the western context but also Buddhism as you start heading east)<br /> <br /> You might take various organizations that are posited by the canon WoD as precursors to the Celestial Chorus &amp; Batini and the like. Something I like to do with my WoD is take inspiration from irl esoteric and occult beliefs for use in my WoD setting, regardless of their real-world veracity (it\u2019s kinda like having sewer gators in NYC, it\u2019s unrealistic but too juicy to ignore). In that vein one might play around with the myriad hypotheses that have been posited for antique occult practices and their influence on the hegemonic churches of the Middle Ages (particularly with the real world occult revival that happened alongside the renaissance). <br /> <br /> Scattershot of possible ideas: the influence of Mediterranean mystery cults (filtered via Plato, Pythagoras, and the like) into the theology of the Church. The idea of sacred monotheists, perhaps tracing themselves back to the Egyptian cult of Aten, and their influence on the rise of the world religions. The concept of quasi-shamanic ecstatic religious groups present in Mediterranean antiquity whose ecstatic sacraments would become the partial basis for elements of baptism, Eucharist, etc. in later theology. The concept of mystery priesthoods dedicated to Mithras, Sol Invictus, etc. cross-pollinating with early Christians in Rome. The hypothesis that the black stone venerated by the Emperor Elegabalus may have had some connection with other stones used in the veneration of Canaanite gods, possibly making Elegabalus the first Roman Emperor to venerate \u2018the god of Abraham\u2019. Anything from the gnostic movements surrounding the development of the early Church tbh.<br /> <br /> Regardless of if one wants to make use of any of that, going later into history, one still finds plenty of juicy tidbits that one could mine for a hidden history of European Christianity. One could do something with the conceit of the crypto-Gnostic Cathars of southern France (beloved by occultists to this day) and the hypothesis they may have been influenced by the Bogomils of Eastern Europe and/or perhaps even a crypto-Islamic troubadour tradition which arose in southern France.<br /> <br /> Or one could do something more with the Knights Templar (who don\u2019t get nearly enough juice in canon imo) in all sorts of ways: maybe in the WoD the Knights Templar really did adopt some sort of crypto-Islamic and/or crypto-Gnostic beliefs after arriving in the Levant during the Crusade period; perhaps developed because of their contact with the legendary Assassins of Alamut, which did happen to some extent even irl. Maybe they did discover some great secrets under the Temple Mount (which one could relate to ideas given above or Dan Brown stuff of what have you). Either way one can also use their (irl) highly developed financial systems (for Europe at the time) and posit their Awakened counterparts as an important historically progressive group helping to usher in the modern age. In that vein, perhaps one adds a bent of Awakened politics to their suppression by Louis the Fair.<br /> <br /> Speaking of their suppression: perhaps, as various occultists have suggested, in the WoD the suppression of the Knights Templar was not so successful as it was in our world. Perhaps remnants of the Knights Templar really did escape their suppression on the continent and go underground in the Scottish Highlands, where their secrets would later re-emerge as (Scottish Rite) Freemasonry. Perhaps, as some have suggested, some Templar really did reach the New World pre-Columbus, and used their greater knowledge to further their plans for a new world (and maybe left some buried treasure at Oak Island, have some have suggested). Perhaps in the WoD, this Awakened Knights Templar, or at least an enlightened core cadre, plays a crucial role in the development of what we would come to know as the Order of Reason. <br /> <br /> If none of this stuff interests you, there\u2019s still plenty of irl cool stuff going on in pre-modern Christianity that one could mine for Awakened interest. Google the: \u2018Clerical Necromantic Underground\u2019 or watch the Dr. Justin Sledge/Esoterica YouTube video about it, it\u2019s pretty fascinating imo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T17:40:59-07:00","id":1539274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539250#post1539250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Same way it worked in real life?</div> </div> </div> </div>Last time I saw we didn't had an Ascension War irl, that's what I meant.<br /> <br /> We have arguably weirder things. But not Ascension War.<br /> <br /> I'd like to see an alternate history with more of a presence from African and American Mages, given that Mages have far more mobility than Sleepers (and while it wasn't reached by the Europeans, they did knew that the Americas existed, or rather that there was some land here).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-25T18:11:31-07:00","id":1539279,"title":"","content":"I've always been pretty curious about the Craftmasons. After all, they originally started as Hermetics, before Mistridge. And they were the backbone of the Order of Reason before everything went Ivory Tower. I've always been interested in a scenario where they left the OoR earlier, maybe even made peace with the Hermetics.<br /> <br /> Though that might require the Hermetics to be a lot less...Hermetic about a lot of things. (Which might also increase the likelihood of more non-European mages, since the Hermetics might manage not to antagonize them this time.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-26T08:35:08-07:00","id":1539317,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539266#post1539266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems to me that if we\u2019re looking at the 1400s, at least in Europe, a significant player has to be whatever Awakened factions align themselves with the hegemonic world religions (particularly Christianity &amp; Islam in the western context but also Buddhism as you start heading east)</div> </div> </div> </div>That's my general thought as well. Belief being such an important part of mage, it would be strange if the majority of Christian and Muslim mages weren't orthodox believers. To put it another way, I think that in the 1400s, the largest conglomeration of mages with an explicitly Christian paradigm will be found within the Catholic Church and they would not think of themselves as a separate entity from it. The Church as a whole wouldn't know about them, but neither would they be hidden heretics within it. Muslim paradigms would be in a similar situation with the Islamic power structures.<br /> <br /> For the same reasons, they wouldn't exist as monolithic factions but as associations of mages in common situations whose opinions and motivations vary as widely as the religious structures they are a part of.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539274#post1539274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd like to see an alternate history with more of a presence from African and American Mages, given that Mages have far more mobility than Sleepers (and while it wasn't reached by the Europeans, they did knew that the Americas existed, or rather that there was some land here).</div> </div> </div> </div>That's one of the things I'm hoping for as well. It's a big blank spot in Mage. For instance, it would strange if while Aztec empire was on the mages of the area weren't having some impact on the rest of the world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-26T08:35:40-07:00","id":1539318,"title":"","content":"That was supposed to be &quot;while the Aztec empire was on the rise, the mages of the area...&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-26T09:05:09-07:00","id":1539319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539318#post1539318\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That was supposed to be &quot;while the Aztec empire was on the rise, the mages of the area...&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Why would you assume the Aztec mages would know there is any more world than the two american continents?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-26T17:15:22-07:00","id":1539346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539319#post1539319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Why would you assume the Aztec mages would know there is any more world than the two american continents?</div> </div> </div> </div>They're Mages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-27T13:01:18-07:00","id":1539383,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war#post1539224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, its time to reimagine Mage's history again. We played with this once before in <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1493716-alternative-traditions-origins\">Alternative Traditions Origins.</a> This time the idea is to throw out the current mix of Traditions and Conventions entirely. We are going to go back to the 1400's and figure out what the mystic world was like at the time, what kind of mages/mage equivalents existed at the time, the stakes and ideologies of the brewing Ascension War, etc.<br /> <br /> Unlike last time, I do not have a specific idea of who the major players are or which side (or sides) of the conflict they will fall on. Nor is there any need to make sure this ends up in the modern MtA we are familiar with. I'm more interested in who could be there and what their motivations are.<br /> <br /> Because this is such an open ended idea, though, I will bring in two ideas from the previous thread.<br /> <br /> 1) The Hermetic Schools - An loose association of scholars and professors piecing together mystical and non mystical insights from the vast and often contradictory libraries left behind by the Roman Empire. These guys look a lot like the typical academicians of the time because they are, there's just a much wider range of study than in our world.<br /> <br /> 2) The Wildlands - Also a product of the fall of Rome and the repeated invasions. Europe is filled with a mix of imported and often conflicting mythologies, monsters, and folkloric traditions and creatures. The Cities are largely untouched but the countryside has never quite stabilized.<br /> <br /> So, who and what would you expect to see?</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, before reading the answers, I'd like to introduce a few points here.<br /> <br /> First of all, I dislike some of the stablished points for MtA. Beginning with the prevalence of &quot;Theosophical Societies&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, the first thing I would take into consideration is that all these secret Magic societies should be a reflection of the cultures from which they originated.<br /> <br /> And by the 1400, RELIGION IS CULTURE - there is, there was no such concept of &quot;I'm a celt and my religion is druidism&quot;. Both things were one and the same - in fact, it's only with the advent of Christianism that this changes somewhat, because until them, all the pantheons of religions were ethno-religions. But Christianism extended beyond its own group, but still created the concept of &quot;Christendom&quot;, which is still roughly similar.<br /> <br /> Only in the modern era can you be an American/European/Brazilian/Australian/Indian and be either a Christian or Muslim or Hindu or whatever.<br /> <br /> Now, it DOES make sense that the Mages, as the elitist pricks that they are, would keep the &quot;best stuff&quot; only for themselves while giving just a &quot;shallow version&quot; of &quot;how things work&quot; to the masses.<br /> <br /> What this means is that the Bhramin priests of India would be uncovering cosmic truths about the trial aspects of reality and using that to recreate the &quot;divine energies&quot; of the war between Gods described in the Mahabharata, while simply telling the peasants to &quot;pray to Shiva for health&quot;.<br /> <br /> But it doesn't make sense that those same Bhramin priests would instead be using Orphic Cults from Greece (which are the origin of what we know today as &quot;Gnosticism/Hermeticism/Theosophy) while telling the peasants to pray to Shiva.<br /> <br /> Therefore, I expected to see in Europe Mages that GENUINELY are Christians - they can operate under a &quot;higher form of Christianism&quot;, but is still CHRISTIANISM.<br /> <br /> Likewise, there would be Middle East Mages that know &quot;Higher Truths of Allah&quot; than the masses of Muslims, but they are STILL MUSLIMS.<br /> <br /> And so on, so forth.<br /> <br /> What's also important is to distinguish between the mainstream religions/philosophical views and the counterculture/subversive cults running beneath.<br /> <br /> In Europe that was particularly prevalent. European culture has been vastly influenced by those secret cultures, a LOT more than what most people are aware of.<br /> <br /> Beginning with ancient Greece and the Orphic Cults. Those are the origins of the Cults to Dyonisus - yes, the famous one.<br /> <br /> Dyonisus is actually OLDER than the entire Greek Pantheon - Dyonisus comes from a culture that existed BEFORE what we today call as &quot;Classic Greece&quot;. Those were called &quot;mystery cults&quot;, and held ideas pretty similar to modern Gnosticism/Hermeticism. Those were considered as &quot;subversive&quot; originally, but eventually they won over the decadent elites of ancient Greece and, eventually, the cult of Dyonisus became mainstream (to the point he was even incorporated into their Pantheon as one of the Dodekatheon, the prestigious rank of the 12 major Olympian Gods).<br /> <br /> Remember the idea of &quot;shadows in a cave&quot; from Plato?<br /> <br /> Well, these ideas come directly from these Orphic Cults from ancient Greece.<br /> <br /> That same general concept would form the base for the philosophy of &quot;Immaterialism&quot; from Emmanuel Kant almost 2.000 years later.<br /> <br /> And all of these form the basis for the entire Gnostic/Hermeticism &quot;pseudo-religion&quot; for the entire history of the West.<br /> <br /> The medieval Alchemists also followed those same precepts. The idea of Alchemy that we have today about medieval people trying to &quot;turn lead into gold&quot; is somewhat cartoonish, while in fact what they were actually trying to do was to find the &quot;reality beyond the shadows in the cave&quot;.<br /> <br /> Now, it is important to understand that all those secret Gnostics/Hermeticists were NOT Christians - even if they would occasionally use &quot;Christian jargon&quot;.<br /> <br /> That's because, Gnosticism is extremely syncretist in its dispositions. It sort of &quot;latches&quot; into real religions or philosophies and absorbs them and adapts with them. It is, therefore, not a &quot;real&quot; religion or philosophy. It is, instead, a collection of &quot;mystical ideas&quot;, a corp of &quot;esoteric beliefs&quot;. Anything today that is &quot;New Age&quot; or &quot;esoteric&quot; can trace its roots back into Gnosticism/Hermeticism. It is a sort of &quot;blender belief&quot;, where you pick and choose according to your likings. The modern &quot;denominations&quot; of this &quot;secret church&quot; are known as Gnosticism, Hermeticism, Theosophy, Illuminati, Masonry, Rosecruxes, Luciferianism, Satanism and several others. In those &quot;occult cults&quot;, you can take a bit of Hinduism, a bit of Buddhism, some pieces of Christianism, mixed with Kantian philosophy, Evolutionary Science and Quantum Physics, blend it all together and voil\u00e1! You have your brand new cult, ready for disseminating your weird insane ideas.<br /> <br /> The advent of Science in Europe is the result of BOTH Christian ideas and some of these &quot;Gnostic&quot; ideas.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28035,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","user":"Magus Maximus","date":". 07-27-2024, 04:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-27T13:15:36-07:00","id":1539384,"title":"","content":"It's just about impossible to come up with a playable culturally realistic magic users game representing historical Earth. Any attempt to make the game setting playable will automatically make it grossly unrealistic. We need to make the game reflect folklore, pop culture, and some literature. <br /> <br /> Having an historically accurate Tibetan Monk scolding an Irish witch, a French ritual occult practitioner, and an American Inventor for materialism would be painfully dull. Having an Arab occultist stab the lot of them in order to prevent their failures from threatening his bid for power would be creepy, borderline racist, and pretty much uninteresting. Then let's toss post-modernism into the mix.<br /> <br /> The 1990s M:tA is looking more and more inspired all the time.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-27T13:28:38-07:00","id":1539387,"title":"","content":"So, in resume, I would like to have at least one group representing each of the major cultures/religions in the world:<br /> <br /> - Christianism;<br /> - Islam;<br /> - Buddhism;<br /> - Hinduism;<br /> - Gnosticism/Hermeticism (counterculture in Europe)<br /> - Zoroastrianism (counterculture in Middle East)<br /> - Taoism + Confucianism<br /> - Paganism (nomadic and tribal groups almost exclusively)<br /> <br /> Now, that covers a good chunk of the world (Eurasia basically).<br /> <br /> It is important to notice that Buddhism is actually the COUNTERCULTURE movement in Asia, be it as in India, China or even Japan, that would eventually be fully integrated into mainstream as a sort of &quot;alternative but equally valid&quot; worldview, that would also suffer heavily from syncretism wherever it was adopted (to the point that Buddhists in India would still revere Shiva, while Buddhists in China would place Buddha as part of the Celestial Bureocracy, while originally Buddhism was a philosophy that was Agnostic in essence)<br /> <br /> Speaking of Japan... Some people may hate this, but Japan wasn't really important enough to matter. I'm sorry to break it up for all the sino-philes out there, but it is simply true. Japan only ever becomes important at the beginning of the 20th century. You can all add a Shintoist &quot;secret tradition&quot; if you will, but at the end of the day, that's just a minor group without any relevance to the rest of the world.<br /> <br /> There's still plenty of the world to go however. Oceania, Subsaharan Africa, Russian Steps and Central Asia and the Americas.<br /> <br /> Central Asia was just witnessing the last days of the once legendary Mongol Hordes. And, in terms of religion, that was one thing that the great Khan didn't care to impose in his vast Empire. Originally the Mongols followed a &quot;pagan&quot; (catchall terms to most politheistic religions in the world) which had a &quot;Father Sky* as main God. But amongst its many conquered populations, the diversity of creeds was immense.<br /> <br /> Each &quot;pagan&quot; group had their own unique, peculiar, pantheons and myths and believes and idiosyncrasies. The thing they all have in common is their politheism.<br /> <br /> Despite those differences, it does make somewhat sense to group them under &quot;paganism&quot;. Therefore, I wouldn't have a problem to group all those &quot;pagans&quot; from all over the world into a single &quot;Syncretic Tradition&quot; (such as the &quot;Verbena&quot;). Any one of these groups that would resort to keeping to their ethnocentric origins would be too niche to survive in a more &quot;globalized&quot; world.<br /> <br /> There is however some important distinctions to make between these so called &quot;pagans&quot;, that can easily justify grouping them in distinctive &quot;Traditions&quot;. And those distinctions are:<br /> <br /> - Politheism;<br /> - Animism;<br /> - Totemism;<br /> - Fetishism.<br /> <br /> Now, one could say for example that Hinduism could be grouped under Politheism, and that's true; however, Hinduism is a massive religion, therefore it cannot just simply be put in the same group. Likewise, Shintoism is a form of Animism, but given the isolation of Japan, it is highly un-syncretic, something that runs against how most animistic religions operate.<br /> <br /> Those divisions are also for the easier classification of those OUTSIDE of these groups, and are more like &quot;umbrella terms&quot;.<br /> <br /> Down the line, I do agree that these somewhat vague descriptions could form the basis for specific Traditions as political groups (if not philosophical/religious groups).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-27T13:34:36-07:00","id":1539388,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539384#post1539384\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's just about impossible to come up with a playable culturally realistic magic users game representing historical Earth. Any attempt to make the game setting playable will automatically make it grossly unrealistic. We need to make the game reflect folklore, pop culture, and some literature.<br /> <br /> Having an historically accurate Tibetan Monk scolding an Irish witch, a French ritual occult practitioner, and an American Inventor for materialism would be painfully dull. Having an Arab occultist stab the lot of them in order to prevent their failures from threatening his bid for power would be creepy, borderline racist, and pretty much uninteresting. Then let's toss post-modernism into the mix.<br /> <br /> The 1990s M:tA is looking more and more inspired all the time.</div> </div> </div> </div>My intent is not to create &quot;Mage: the Crusade/Jihad&quot;. But instead to not have an asinine unrealistic world based on sheer lunacy. As it stands, the Celestial Chorus, Hermetic Order, Ahl I Batin and Akashic Brotherhood are all practitioners of Theosophy &quot;before Theosophy was cool&quot;. And the only practical differences between them are about &quot;Theosophy of the East&quot; and &quot;Theosophy of the West&quot;.<br /> It is completely nonsensical.<br /> <br /> <br /> Edit: there was a post of mine before the one before that one, there I explain a bit better what my intent is... It has been flagged as spam... Sorry guys, my last answer will seem a bit disconnected without it. Let's wait for an admin to allow it, so that I may proceed","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28035,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","user":"Magus Maximus","date":". 07-27-2024, 04:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-27T19:46:45-07:00","id":1539405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539244#post1539244\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just throwing something of a later date to the pot, today I was thinking about rainbows, and more specifically about the number of colors.<br /> <br /> Sir Isaac Newton is perhaps the ideal of the man of reason and science before the Industrial Revolution, developer of so many mathematical tools for use in physics, and...<br /> <br /> Decided a rainbow had seven colors because that was good numerology.<br /> <br /> My point is, the whole idea of a pure science based Technocracy is bullshit, so how could the Ascension War form itself in a world where the forefathers of Industrial Science are also &quot;Harry Potters&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>He was also a &quot;Gnostic/Hermeticist&quot; Alchemist.<br /> <br /> This is a pretty deep habit hole to get into. The &quot;philosophy&quot; of alchemy alone is deep as hell and goes far far beyond the pure laboratory take of modern chemistry where you mix A with B in order to achieve Alpha result. There's a reason to why the Alchemists claimed that it was an art; Alchemy is much more akin to &quot;theology&quot; - under quotations because is not real theology, neither is it real philosophy nor real science, but rather something that needs its own classification, I call it &quot;gnosis&quot;, which is that which comes from &quot;divine revelation&quot; (but not really) and it is what drives all sorts of esoteric, mystical or magical thinking. The reason why it is not real theology is because it doesn't have dogmas, which is fundamental for any religion - which are based on &quot;faith&quot;, there is, believing in dogmatic truths. Those esoteric cults don't have dogmas, therefore they don't have faith, they change according to the surroundings, &quot;latching&quot; into other religions like parasites and syncretic evolving from them. You also &quot;discover&quot; said truths based on experience and experiment, but those are all pseudo-scientific; it is not, therefore, real science. And it is not philosophy either, because you don't use your reason to reach for the truth, but rather you can only discover the truth via &quot;divine revelation&quot; (which is exactly what the world &quot;Gnosis&quot; means).<br /> <br /> Hence why &quot;Gnosis&quot; is not theology, nor science and neither philosophy, but rather something on a category of its own. It does, however, use jargons from both theology, science and philosophy. Post Modernism could be claimed to be &quot;Gnosis&quot; disguised as &quot;science&quot;. Meanwhile, Kantian philosophy is entirely &quot;Gnostic&quot;. And any modern representations of Satan as the good guy (like in &quot;Paradise Lost&quot;) are &quot;Gnosis&quot; disguised as theology.<br /> <br /> Medieval Alchemy was entirely based on this &quot;Gnosis&quot; way of thinking. And Isaac Newton was an Alchemist. He was, therefore, a mystic, an occultist.<br /> <br /> The reason why he thought that 7 colours would be a good number is because Numerology is one of the &quot;sciences&quot; of esoteric beliefs.<br /> <br /> Astrology, numerology, &quot;quantum healing&quot; and all the rest of the New Age stuff, all of those are the innumerable branches of the esoteric (mystical) three of &quot;Gnosis&quot;.<br /> <br /> The main goal of the Alchemists to turn lead into gold was NOT to go brrrrrrrrrr into the printing money machine and create infinite inflation in medieval Europe (like modern economic Keynesian theory tells you to do, and all modern governments are doing at full steam). The goal was to turn things into their &quot;true self&quot; or their &quot;true form&quot;.<br /> <br /> Therefore, &quot;gold&quot; in their view was the &quot;true form&quot; of lead.<br /> <br /> Remember Plato? I've actually covered this in my previous post, but that has being rendered as spam because I accidentally double sent it. It will show in a while, but the important part is how Plato's ideas about our real world being just &quot;shadows in a cave&quot; and about how there's supposedly a real of ideas which is &quot;purer&quot; and &quot;more real&quot; than this material one is behind that. I also explained how all of this is connected to the ancient secret Orphic Cults, which were secret societies that praised Dyonisus in ancient Greece. Anyway, don't forget to check it out when the post arrives.<br /> <br /> So, the idea of the Alchemists is that lead isn't &quot;really&quot; lead, but rather is just a mere &quot;shadow&quot;, and gold was it's &quot;pure form&quot;, or &quot;real form&quot;.<br /> <br /> That's also not to be taken literally, but rather metaphorically. Therefore, the &quot;idea of gold&quot; would be the &quot;real form&quot; of the &quot;idea of lead&quot;.<br /> <br /> The goal of Alchemists was, therefore, not to simply transmutate the crude matter, but rather to unlock the secret of &quot;pure or real forms&quot;. They believed that, if they could be able to transmute lead into gold, that would unlock for them the secret for &quot;reaching the pure/real essence&quot; of all things.<br /> <br /> This explains the other great &quot;holy Graal&quot; of Alchemy, besides the Transmutation of lead into gold. And that is the idea of the &quot;Philosopher Stone&quot;.<br /> <br /> Many have heard of that, but very few have any idea what it means.<br /> <br /> The Philosopher Stone is, in fact, the very first idea about ARTIFICIAL LIFE. Ever heard about &quot;Synths&quot;? Androids, Biorroids (biological androids) and such Sci Fi tropes?<br /> <br /> Well, the Philosopher Stone was... Something like that.<br /> <br /> It was the idea of creating a &quot;Stone&quot; - therefore, an inert element - that had &quot;blood&quot;, that had a &quot;vital pulse&quot;.<br /> <br /> A.<br /> Stone.<br /> That.<br /> Was.<br /> Alive.<br /> <br /> That's right. That's the idea of a Philosopher Stone: to create a living rock. Something that blurs the lines between crude matter and life.<br /> <br /> Hell, but that's a freaking robot! Hehe.<br /> <br /> I once saw a brilliant video saying that our Smartphones are the culmination of the dreams of the Alchemists of old... We are, each one of us, carrying a &quot;Philosopher Stone&quot; constantly in our pockets; a piece of &quot;rock&quot; (made of several metals and silicates and plastics) that is nevertheless &quot;alive&quot; (it has vitality, pulse).<br /> <br /> Again, the idea was the same, to leave the world of platonic &quot;shadows&quot; and reach the &quot;real&quot; world of forms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-27T20:42:27-07:00","id":1539409,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539266#post1539266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems to me that if we\u2019re looking at the 1400s, at least in Europe, a significant player has to be whatever Awakened factions align themselves with the hegemonic world religions (particularly Christianity &amp; Islam in the western context but also Buddhism as you start heading east)<br /> <br /> You might take various organizations that are posited by the canon WoD as precursors to the Celestial Chorus &amp; Batini and the like. Something I like to do with my WoD is take inspiration from irl esoteric and occult beliefs for use in my WoD setting, regardless of their real-world veracity (it\u2019s kinda like having sewer gators in NYC, it\u2019s unrealistic but too juicy to ignore). In that vein one might play around with the myriad hypotheses that have been posited for antique occult practices and their influence on the hegemonic churches of the Middle Ages (particularly with the real world occult revival that happened alongside the renaissance).<br /> <br /> Scattershot of possible ideas: the influence of Mediterranean mystery cults (filtered via Plato, Pythagoras, and the like) into the theology of the Church. The idea of sacred monotheists, perhaps tracing themselves back to the Egyptian cult of Aten, and their influence on the rise of the world religions. The concept of quasi-shamanic ecstatic religious groups present in Mediterranean antiquity whose ecstatic sacraments would become the partial basis for elements of baptism, Eucharist, etc. in later theology. The concept of mystery priesthoods dedicated to Mithras, Sol Invictus, etc. cross-pollinating with early Christians in Rome. The hypothesis that the black stone venerated by the Emperor Elegabalus may have had some connection with other stones used in the veneration of Canaanite gods, possibly making Elegabalus the first Roman Emperor to venerate \u2018the god of Abraham\u2019. Anything from the gnostic movements surrounding the development of the early Church tbh.<br /> <br /> Regardless of if one wants to make use of any of that, going later into history, one still finds plenty of juicy tidbits that one could mine for a hidden history of European Christianity. One could do something with the conceit of the crypto-Gnostic Cathars of southern France (beloved by occultists to this day) and the hypothesis they may have been influenced by the Bogomils of Eastern Europe and/or perhaps even a crypto-Islamic troubadour tradition which arose in southern France.<br /> <br /> Or one could do something more with the Knights Templar (who don\u2019t get nearly enough juice in canon imo) in all sorts of ways: maybe in the WoD the Knights Templar really did adopt some sort of crypto-Islamic and/or crypto-Gnostic beliefs after arriving in the Levant during the Crusade period; perhaps developed because of their contact with the legendary Assassins of Alamut, which did happen to some extent even irl. Maybe they did discover some great secrets under the Temple Mount (which one could relate to ideas given above or Dan Brown stuff of what have you). Either way one can also use their (irl) highly developed financial systems (for Europe at the time) and posit their Awakened counterparts as an important historically progressive group helping to usher in the modern age. In that vein, perhaps one adds a bent of Awakened politics to their suppression by Louis the Fair.<br /> <br /> Speaking of their suppression: perhaps, as various occultists have suggested, in the WoD the suppression of the Knights Templar was not so successful as it was in our world. Perhaps remnants of the Knights Templar really did escape their suppression on the continent and go underground in the Scottish Highlands, where their secrets would later re-emerge as (Scottish Rite) Freemasonry. Perhaps, as some have suggested, some Templar really did reach the New World pre-Columbus, and used their greater knowledge to further their plans for a new world (and maybe left some buried treasure at Oak Island, have some have suggested). Perhaps in the WoD, this Awakened Knights Templar, or at least an enlightened core cadre, plays a crucial role in the development of what we would come to know as the Order of Reason.<br /> <br /> If none of this stuff interests you, there\u2019s still plenty of irl cool stuff going on in pre-modern Christianity that one could mine for Awakened interest. Google the: \u2018Clerical Necromantic Underground\u2019 or watch the Dr. Justin Sledge/Esoterica YouTube video about it, it\u2019s pretty fascinating imo.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh boy, yes... YES! I like the way you think.<br /> <br /> I love to study the influence of all those occult traditions in the West. And this is it.<br /> <br /> Now, I'll use &quot;Gnosticism&quot; as an umbrella term for all these secret cults - because there's too many of them to speak individually. And they all share a common root that comes from ancient Middle East, Balkans and Egypt, born out of insane syncretisms over that area of the Mediterranean.<br /> <br /> We are possibly talking about the oldest &quot;religion&quot; in the history of humanity (not exactly the first, but the oldest that still exists). Mithraism, the Orphic Cults in Greece and the Cults of Isis in Rome, those all share that same &quot;Gnosis&quot;.<br /> <br /> And so too are the first century Gnostics at the time of the early Christian church.<br /> <br /> And it is at this exact moment, at the century 1, that the first and most important &quot;schism&quot; of the magical society happens - even more important than the arise of the Order of Reason. And this is the Schism between the early Christians and the Gnostics.<br /> <br /> To put it simply, the Christians, as we all know, believe God came in a human material form, to redeem us humans from our sins.<br /> <br /> The Gnostics however, believe that the &quot;God of Abraham&quot; is actually the Demiurge - the DEVIL - that created the material world as an illusionary prison for our souls.<br /> <br /> Ever since that time, the Church has waged war against these &quot;Gnostics&quot; in any form or under any different names that they came from, and again, there are several.<br /> <br /> What's important is that this represents a very perceptible schism. Those that went for Christianism, we could call them &quot;Celestial Chorus&quot; or any other made up name, but what matters is that they remain Christian.<br /> <br /> The &quot;Gnostics&quot; however well...<br /> <br /> So, Mithraism, Orphic Cults and Cults of Isis or Sol Invictus or many many others, were not the only &quot;Gnostic Denominations&quot; in the world.<br /> <br /> We are actually VERY familiar with at least two others that are still present to this day in the real world. One everybody knows, and the other we know because we are a bunch of nerds.<br /> <br /> The one we know because we are nerds is, obviously, the cult to &quot;Hermes Trimegistus&quot;, or Hermes 3 times great, which is the fusion of the Greek God Hermes and the Egyptian God Thot (look at that wonderful syncretism right there).<br /> <br /> Also known as the famous ORDER OF HERMES. And let me tell you... Those guys were highly active from the shadows throughout all the time... And I'm talking about the real world history, not the fictional game one. The &quot;rebirth&quot; of esotericism in the Renaissance was only possible because the Order of Hermes have ever existed hidden in the shadows ever since the times of Rome (take that healthy dose of Illuminati conspiracy for you).<br /> <br /> The other famous &quot;Gnostic Denomination&quot; are non other than the Masons. Those are the most famous ones.<br /> <br /> Oh, I used to be a Mason too by the way, but I thought it was just a club of old people wishing to improve their Contacts on their sheets... But I was HUGELY mistaken. It is a cult. And I'm an atheist, and a skeptical, materialist, mechanicist materialist. All of their mambo jambo was too much for me.<br /> <br /> And for those curious yes, the Masons absolutely believe in the Demiurge, and that we are all &quot;God&quot; and we just need to &quot;remember that we are God&quot; and all the rest of the lunacy... The thing is thou, there are &quot;circles&quot; in it, and that's valid for ALL &quot;Gnostic Denominations&quot;, not just for Masons or Rosecruxes.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> What I mean by this is that there's an external circle: people in that area are &quot;prospects&quot;. The higher ups are looking them up to see if they hold any promises. People in that circle know NOTHING. Masons in that position will think that Masonry is indeed just a club of gentleman for the sole purpose of practicing philanthropy. All the weird simbles of compasses and eyes inside pyramids and what not, the ones above them will simply tell them things like &quot;oh, that eye? Oh, that eye is nothing, it's just an artistic representation of a metaphorical symbol. It's just to represent how you should always be watchful of every one of your actions&quot; and things like that.<br /> <br /> The Higher Ups will actually DIVERT promising acolites from any one of their secrets, even dissuading them from thinking that their cults might even HAVE secrets at all.<br /> <br /> Them comes the &quot;middle circle&quot;, and here is where a promissory &quot;applicant&quot; will actually be introduced to the inner mysteries. This is were they'll actually be induced inside of the cult. &quot;So, remember that eye? Well, actually, it's not just a metaphor...&quot;<br /> <br /> And finally, there's the Inner Circle. While those in the middle circle are introduced with the real face of the cult, the deeper mysteries are reserved for the Inner Circle.<br /> <br /> Now, those are not nomenclatures from Masonry - they actually have a system of 33 degrees, but it works in that same premisse, and this works for &quot;Gnostic Denominations&quot; outside of Masonry too. Therefore, this works in more or less the same way for Masons, Rosecruxes, Theosophians, Illuminatis, Luciferians and several others, including the (real world) Order of Hermes too.<br /> <br /> Also also, speaking of the fabled Knights Templar, the Masons claim to have their mythical foundation based from Knights Templar that escaped from their excommunication from the church. That is the legend, that the Templars founded the Masonry - I even enacted the persecution of Jacques Demolay, the last Grandmaster of the Templar order and absolute idol of the Masons, when I was a teen, in my local lodge. According to the legend, he was tortured to death, but never betrayed the order, and the location of the &quot;Templar treasures&quot; remained a secret that he took to the grave. Oh yes, and Jacques Demolay also cursed the king of France and the Pope, and both died horrible deaths at that same year. Or so the legend say. So, if you guys want that juicy &quot;sewer gators under NYC&quot; stuff, this is it... Mythical order of monastic warriors, excommunicated, persecuted and their last leader curses the Pope and the King, and their remnants create the mysterious illuminati-esc order of the Masons... No fiction will ever be able to topple that real world history.<br /> <br /> What's important to notice is that, the Order of Hermes was born in the ancient world, and they quickly departed from the Christians. The Templars however, were a Christian order, and perhaps they got &quot;infected&quot; by Gnostic cults? Perhaps like the Cults of Isis from ancient Egypt, which the Templars uncovered during the Crusades.<br /> <br /> My theory is that the Templars were indeed &quot;infected&quot; by Middle Eastern &quot;Gnostic&quot; ideas, and because of that they become to dissent from the church.<br /> <br /> Therefore, we would have 2 schisms:<br /> <br /> - Century 1, between Christians and Gnostics. From that are born the &quot;Christian Tradition&quot; (Celestial Chorus or whatever) and the Order of Hermes.<br /> - Century 14 with the excommunication of the Templars, who leave the church and found the secret Mason societies.<br /> <br /> The Masons and Order of Hermes are pretty similar in creed, but with very different origin stories.<br /> <br /> Now, what I think is that the Order of Reason actually comes from one of those. Either from a branch of the Order of Hermes, or from a branch of the Masons.<br /> <br /> Or perhaps... Why not from BOTH?<br /> <br /> Because it is undoubtful that those two groups ended up &quot;drinking from each other's cups&quot;. I've seen many many beliefs that were undeniably Hermetic within the Masonry with my very own eyes.<br /> <br /> So, it could be entirely possible that some rogue cells from both groups coalesced together to form the proto Order of Reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-27T21:31:52-07:00","id":1539410,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539317#post1539317\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's my general thought as well. Belief being such an important part of mage, it would be strange if the majority of Christian and Muslim mages weren't orthodox believers. To put it another way, I think that in the 1400s, the largest conglomeration of mages with an explicitly Christian paradigm will be found within the Catholic Church and they would not think of themselves as a separate entity from it. The Church as a whole wouldn't know about them, but neither would they be hidden heretics within it. Muslim paradigms would be in a similar situation with the Islamic power structures.<br /> <br /> For the same reasons, they wouldn't exist as monolithic factions but as associations of mages in common situations whose opinions and motivations vary as widely as the religious structures they are a part of.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely. Which is why, after all the history lessons, I claim that the &quot;Celestial Chorus&quot; (or perhaps it would be better to come up with a new name for them) is a secret society of Enlightened Christians operating inside the Church, and is the main hand guiding the Christendom in Europe, trying to purge the heretics and convert the heathens. They have 3 main enemies, 2 externals and 1 internal.<br /> <br /> The internal enemy will be no other than the Order of Hermes themselves - and oh boy, those are the bitterest of the enemies. The Hermetics are always hiding in the Shadows and always trying to subvert the church from within, and the Choristers are always trying to purge them. The power struggle between the two will shape the destinies of Europe, with times when the Chorister and church powers shrink enabling for Hermetic ideas to spread, such as in the Renaissance, having a dynamic upon which both groups influence would move in opposite waves, alternating between one or another on top.<br /> <br /> The two external enemies are the &quot;Janissaries&quot; (Islamic Mages) and the &quot;Pagans&quot;. The Pagans are fractured and disorganized, but Islam is the greatest threat to the Chorus (behind the Hermetics thou).<br /> <br /> North America is Totemism, South America, Central Asia and Russian Steps is Animist, India is Hinduist with syncretist Buddhism, China is dominated by the highly rigid, hierarchical and pretty solidified Wu Lung, that holds a meritocratic system that is integral to the Empire, leaving no room for other groups inside of the Middle Kingdom. The Wu Lung consider themselves as members of the &quot;Celestial Bureocracy&quot;, and practice Taoism, Confucianism and Syncretic Buddhism and care nothing about the many &quot;barbarians&quot; at the edges of the Middle Kingdom.<br /> <br /> In India, Bhramin Mages wage as many petty wars against one another as the many kingdoms upon which they reside.<br /> <br /> Subsaharan Africa is Fetishist.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's one of the things I'm hoping for as well. It's a big blank spot in Mage. For instance, it would strange if while Aztec empire was on the mages of the area weren't having some impact on the rest of the world. </div> </div> </div>I do propose to have a few more divisions between what is roughly called as the &quot;Verbena&quot; and &quot;Dream speakers&quot;. And that would be based on the religious concepts of Animism, Totemism, Fetishism and Polytheism.<br /> <br /> So, broadly speaking, all the &quot;pagans&quot; that don't belong to neither of the &quot;big boys&quot; - Christianism, Islam, Taoism + Confucianism, Buddhism and Hinduism, all the others are &quot;pagans&quot;.<br /> <br /> The natives in North America are Totemists<br /> The natives in South America, Central Asia and Russian Steps are Animists<br /> Subsaharan Africa is Fetishist<br /> And the &quot;pagans&quot; in Europe, Middle East and South East Asia are Polytheists<br /> <br /> So now we have all the groups that need one juicy secret group of Mages to play with:<br /> <br /> - Christians;<br /> - Muslims;<br /> - Taoists + Confucianists;<br /> - Hindus + Buddhists;<br /> - Animists;<br /> - Totemists;<br /> - Fetishists;<br /> - Polytheists<br /> <br /> Besides those, there are also the &quot;Heretics&quot; (aka the &quot;Order of Hermes&quot;). Those are the mystery cults, that were highly prevalent in both Europe and Middle East - what a surprise that 2 dogmatic religions that don't admit any theology other than monotheism to sprout secret cults of esoteric forbidden knowledge. That could never happen in India for instance because the entirety of Hinduism is like a mystery cults already...<br /> <br /> Coincidentally, the number of &quot;Traditions&quot; that formed from this is... Yeah, 9. I'm not a Cabalist Numerologist, that really was a coincidence. <br /> <br /> But that number could be 10, if the mystery cults in the Middle East (hidden from Islam) are NOT the same as those in Europe under the Christendom (and they probably aren't).<br /> <br /> But that's ok, because if it is indeed 10 Traditions, we could still keep the hole plot about the Ahl-I-Batin forming the Traditions but then defecting and what not.<br /> <br /> From this, you guys could even come up with &quot;seats for each Tradition&quot; and whatnot.<br /> <br /> However, I believe that the &quot;Christian Mages&quot; entry into the Traditions would only occur much later. At the 14th century, it is highly possible that they are even part of the Order of Reason - or maybe, even their FOUNDERS. So, the &quot;Choristers&quot; (or whatever their name), would be the enemies that the Traditions form to combat, only to be kicked out of the Technocratic Union (and losing all grip upon the church) later on.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539346#post1539346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> They're Mages.</div> </div> </div> </div> Just because they are Mages doesn't mean they are omniscient.<br /> <br /> &quot;Magic&quot; in the setting of Magic is limited by your imagination.<br /> <br /> If you can't imagine that there's anything beyond the vast (and infinite) ocean, than you cannot know that there are more lands on the &quot;other side&quot; - particularly if you don't even believe that there is an &quot;other side&quot;!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-28T04:06:13-07:00","id":1539416,"title":"","content":"Magus Maximus. The &quot;Satan is the Hero&quot; interpretation of Paradise Lost is a product of the Early Romantics and William Blake. Milton would have been appalled by the suggestion.<br /> <br /> Blake certainly had a radical take on many things however, although he would approve and appreciate &quot;gnosis&quot; an individual awareness of divine truth through mystical means. &quot;Gnosisticism&quot; was a very specific religious tradition that had a whole series of ideas he'd have loathed.<br /> <br /> As to &quot;Paradise Lost&quot; it's better interpreted politically. Milton was an admirer of the real Machivelli (as opposed to the weird construct most people think is Machivelli). Milton understood Machivelli's political thought clearly and was applying it to England through his poem. Heaven is run according to the <b>Discourses. </b>Hell is run according to the <b>\u200bPrince.</b><br /> <br /> Of course they're many more layers than that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 07-28-2024, 10:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-28T05:28:39-07:00","id":1539418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539346#post1539346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They're Mages.</div> </div> </div> </div>So what? Most of the world was oblivious that americas ever existed until columbus. I see no reason why just because someone is a mage will know there is another continent about a month (usually much more) worh of seatravel away. How would they even get the idea?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28439-quelsec","name":"quelsec","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"407"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-28T06:52:46-07:00","id":1539421,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539416#post1539416\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Magus Maximus. The &quot;Satan is the Hero&quot; interpretation of Paradise Lost is a product of the Early Romantics and William Blake. Milton would have been appalled by the suggestion.<br /> <br /> Blake certainly had a radical take on many things however, although he would approve and appreciate &quot;gnosis&quot; an individual awareness of divine truth through mystical means. &quot;Gnosisticism&quot; was a very specific religious tradition that had a whole series of ideas he'd have loathed.<br /> <br /> As to &quot;Paradise Lost&quot; it's better interpreted politically. Milton was an admirer of the real Machivelli (as opposed to the weird construct most people think is Machivelli). Milton understood Machivelli's politican thought clearly and was applying it to England through his poem. Heaven is run according to the <b>Discourses. </b>Hell is run according to the <b>\u200bPrince.</b><br /> <br /> Of course they're many more layers than that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yes, I'm aware. I failed to mention this. But it is how it works, those &quot;Gnostic Denominations&quot; are highly syncretic. I'm also using the word &quot;Gnostic&quot; as a rather loose umbrella term for all the different mystery cults that popped up throughout history, since they have some common elements (particularly the idea that God is the evil Demiurge, that the material world is an illusion created by this Demiurge (Devil) to trap our souls and that we are in fact, shards of the &quot;True God&quot;). From those ideas, every single one of these many branches erupted, and since it is a beast far to vast to tackle, and because we lack a better word for it, I use &quot;Gnosticism&quot; collectively for this... For this collection. Therefore, I'm not using it to designate (solely) the 1 century Gnostics.<br /> <br /> And as for Paradise Lost, yeah, absolutely. Totally coopted by the cult, and distorted to fit their views. It is what those &quot;Gnostic&quot; cults do, they pick a little here, a little there and breed their own thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-28T10:57:18-07:00","id":1539432,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>quelsec</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539418#post1539418\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So what? Most of the world was oblivious that americas ever existed until columbus. I see no reason why just because someone is a mage will know there is another continent about a month (usually much more) worh of seatravel away. How would they even get the idea?</div> </div> </div> </div>The idea that most people didn't had a clue about the Americas is a legend. People expected some landmass to be here since ancient times, just not this size. For most of history the only reason to not sail here was that it would be an exceedingly dangerous journey for the vessels they had.<br /> <br /> Other than that, Mages in the setting always knew and explored far more than Sleepers. In the Middle Ages they were already exploring other planets and the moon. The setting as is already mentions contact between Mages from Europe and the Americas, it just does nothing with it.<br /> <br /> I think bringing Mages from far away places to be a part of the story would make for a setting both more diverse and more interesting, and it is certainly more than possible with Mages being Mages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-28T14:46:29-07:00","id":1539442,"title":"","content":"I could see the overall Ascension War being a three-way conflict instead of the two-way Traditions vs. Technocracy we're familiar with. The third faction would cover Christianity, Islam, and Judaism: an Assembly of Faith. I would see four Customs (this faction's counterpart to Traditions and Conventions): <br /> <br /> \u2022 The Ahl-i-Batin, retooled to be more Islamic and not \u201cgnostic\u201d.<br /> \u2022 The Scions of God, a faction patterned after Catholicism which has influence in the Catholic Church, the Eastern Orthodox Church, the Anglican Church, and other Christian denominations that maintain a strong hierarchal structure among the clergy. <br /> \u2022 An as-yet unnamed group that collectively represents the various Protestant denominations that have largely abandoned the clergy/layman divide. <br /> \u2022 the Lions of Zion, a group introduced in the Sorcerers Crusade Companion who serve as guardians and spiritual leaders of Judaism. <br /> <br /> I see this as a separate faction in part because I don't see any of these groups being inclined to ally with \u201cgnostics\u201d who argue that God is an evil Demiurge, and in part because the Catholic Church has had about as much influence in Europe throughout most of it's history as the likes of the Wu Lung have had in China. In a similar way too how the Wu Lung never saw any need for allies until fairly recently, I don't see why the Catholic group would team up with anyone throughout most of its history. And while the Protestants would be largely at odds with the Catholics for most of their shared history, they'd nonetheless find far more common ground with the Catholics than with pretty much anyone else. <br /> <br /> Bringing in the Batini and/or the Lions of Zion would be more of a stretch, but still less of a stretch than bringing <i>anyone else </i>would be. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-28T15:40:55-07:00","id":1539444,"title":"","content":"In reply to some of the ideas in<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28035\" data-vbnamecard=\"28035\">Magus Maximus</a> \u2018s posts (with the disclaimer that I\u2019m following the \u2018Sewer Gator\u2019 principle of WoD brainstorming/setting writing)<br /> <br /> <b>re: Early Church-Gnostic Split</b><br /> <br /> Of the two major schisms you describe in post #16, it might be interesting to seed various other setting threads that one could run with in their WoD. One interesting hypothesis that could be relevant to storytellers with an interest in the gonzo is the concept that the Roman Empire rewrote the surviving gospels around the times of the various councils that established the early church\u2019s theological positions (the more radical version of this theory is the notion of Jesus of Nazareth being a wholesale invention of Rome) so as to make the gospels more amenable to co-existence with the social and political necessities of the empire. If such a thing was even partially true in one\u2019s WoD, this could provide fodder for a clique of axial age Roman Awakened who could be seen as pre-cursors of the Order of Reason/Technocrats (at least, in the form where they lost resemble the Exarchs of MtAw) in their aim to place limits upon the Gnostic currents. Or, alternatively, it could be a fun concept for the claimed history of a clique of neo-Roman philosopher-mages!<br /> <br /> Even if that particular story is untrue for your WoD it does draw inspiration imo from a real phenomena that was the process of socio-political conditioning the religious movements are drawn through if they are to harmoniously interweave with a historical polity. One could draw up analogous histories for the development of \u2018pagan\u2019, Islamic, Buddhist, Taoist, etc. beliefs which interwove with the socio-political/economic context of their polity. In fact the tensions which often exist within religious traditions as practiced by different segments of society (and reflecting the contradictions between the interests of those segments of society) is a rich vein for mining drama and narrative. Clearly, in the case of the Christian Church, a faction prevailed which would have to ensure that the Church could not only co-exist with the Empire (and later, the barbarian warlords that followed the empire), but synthesize with those political units.<br /> <br /> One might represent this dynamic by having a faction of Church-awakened (whether or not we want to call them the \u2018Celestial Chorus\u2019 proper, I\u2019ll call them the \u201cCuriae\u201d for now) whose interest was to create the world that aligned Empire &amp; Church and who were thus naturally opposed to the Gnostics, the (often Apocalyptic) \u2018Populares\u2019, Heretics (miscellaneous heterodox groups), and whatever other factions one wants to include within the Church as vessel for Awakened politics.<br /> <br /> <b>re: Order of Hermes</b><br /> <br /> I def agree that in the various crack ups of the renaissance and early modern period, Hermetic philosophers and the various philosophers who would align themselves with the Rosicrucians would be great fodder for depicting a shake up which would provide the material and ideological bases for something like the Order of Reason to form. <br /> <br /> The Rosicrucians as described in this early modern period as a secret brotherhood dedicated to aligning the spheres of True Philosophy and True Theology and bringing them together in a harmonious synthesis, which was of course indebted to the general trends of the Reformation, which as one strategy of its critique of the Church had spent a considerable amount of energy in criticizing the various superstitious, crypto-pagan practices which the pre-Reformation Church was accused of either having endorsed or tacitly overlooked. The Counter-Reformation of course swings back and perhaps the Jesuit Brotherhood are the best Catholic counterpart to the role and function of the Rosicrucians in this time period.<br /> <br /> Now irl the Rosicrucians as described in the classic pamphlets may never have actually existed outside of the idealized dreams of some Protestant German philosopher-theologians but this hasn\u2019t stopped the speculation of latter day occultists that various Protestant philosopher-theologians of the day were in fact involved in private (or even secret) reading and discussion societies which, if not inspired by Rosicrucianism, certainly rhymes with its stated values. Some have even proposed groups such as these as being the antecedent of such organizations as the British Royal Society and the ideological ferment behind such storied figures as Sir Francis Bacon, Sir Isaac Newton, &amp; John Dee. A prime recruiting pool for an Order of Reason type organization.<br /> <br /> As well as being of a similar background to individuals who would later become involved with Masonic societies after their proliferation (with the Rosicrucians perhaps as among the earliest \u201cPara-Masonic\u201d societies).<br /> <br /> <b>re: Masons</b><br /> <br /> The Masons are a very rich vein and one very useful imo for imagining the sort of influences on an Order of Reason type organization as well as how such an organization could eventually evolve into something like the Technocratic Union, because, as some of my favorite fringe historians have argued, a selected reading of the Brotherhood\u2019s history can in fact be used as mirror to chart the course of modern western society\u2019s development.<br /> <br /> One of the observations that fascinates me about the Masons, and even if one might quibble with its real world applications I think it\u2019s good for narrative reasons, and might also offer partial explanations for your experience MM, is this heuristic for Masonic history and the historical position of Masons relative to society at large: let us posit that the politically-active Masons are generally aligned with the ambitions of organized cadres of the (petty) bourgeoisie.<br /> <br /> According to the writer who I first heard this idea from, this can be shown by showcasing a few various tendencies of historical Masonic groups: in the 18th century Masonic Lodges provided means, opportunities, &amp; connections for the class of ideologically &amp; politically motivated bourgeois cadres which would play key roles in the American, French, &amp; Haitian revolutions; using lodges both as technology of social organization and a hot house for developing critiques of feudal and clerical order (for the ur-example of this, see Adam Weishaup\u2019s Bavarian Illuminati).<br /> <br /> In the 19th century Masonic Lodges could be split between those territories where the above hothouses of revolutionary liberalism were still relevant and thus Masonry could be historically progressive, such as in the Bolivarian revolutions of Latin America or with Garibaldi &amp; the Carbonari in Italy (which, as an aside, some mafia historians have posited the hypothesis that the organization of the Southern Italian mafias as para-Masonic organizations may have their origins in the contact between revolutionary carbonari &amp; rural banditti during imprisonment in 19th century Italy, which is a juicy notion); while in places where bourgeoise society was established Masonic groups took on a conservative or reactionary character (for the former consider British Masonry under Victoria or the proliferation of Masonry within the American political classes, for the latter consider the paramasonic nature of confederare &amp; post-confederate groups such as the Knights of the Golden Circle &amp; the first Ku Klux Klan; also note the curious political slipperiness of Popular Anti-Masonry in 19th century US politics).<br /> <br /> By the 20th century Masonic Lodges are so infamous for their capacity to harbor nodes of intelligence organization that most mass political movements, whether progressive or reactionary, suppress Masonry upon taking power so as to forestall restoration or counter-attack from the central Bourgeois core powers; despite this such mass political organizations, whether progressive or reactionary, often tend to also make use of many of the social technologies of Freemasonry and could perhaps even be called para-Masonic in their inner cadres. Meanwhile within a core bourgeois power such as the United States, Political Freemasonry remains a live current for the active political and economic classes for a time (my grandfather who was in government work was the Catholic equivalent, a Knight of Columbus); but by the end of Fordism, the Neoliberal turn, and the decline of classical Political Machines in the 1970s, Freemasonry has gradually lost its vitality and potency until today, in the 21st century, most US Masonic Lodges are probably indistinguishable from their local Elks Lodge or Rotary Club counterparts. <br /> <br /> Now of course there are some exceptions to this. There has always been a conservative, even reactionary, wing of Freemasonry. The Bavarian Illuminati famously caused a split in Continental freemasonry because many lodges were unwilling to tolerate and co-exist with the revolutionary implications of the illuminated lodges. This dynamic is depicted in an interesting fashion in Alan Moore\u2019s \u201cFrom Hell\u201d, where in a blink-and-you\u2019ll-miss-it plot point, the principal antagonist of the novel, a British Mason, invokes the threat of \u2018Illuminism\u201d to justify his extreme tactics to his reticent superiors. <br /> <br /> Anyhow, regardless of one\u2019s feelings on the heuristic given above and its relation to real world history and politics, I think that it offers an interesting vein to draw inspiration for the depiction of not only how the Masonic thread can be used in the presentation of the Order of Reason type group but also how it can be used to explain the development and transition of the Order of Reason into the Technocratic Union.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T07:58:20-07:00","id":1539472,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539432#post1539432\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The idea that most people didn't had a clue about the Americas is a legend. People expected some landmass to be here since ancient times, just not this size. For most of history the only reason to not sail here was that it would be an exceedingly dangerous journey for the vessels they had.<br /> <br /> Other than that, Mages in the setting always knew and explored far more than Sleepers. In the Middle Ages they were already exploring other planets and the moon. The setting as is already mentions contact between Mages from Europe and the Americas, it just does nothing with it.<br /> <br /> I think bringing Mages from far away places to be a part of the story would make for a setting both more diverse and more interesting, and it is certainly more than possible with Mages being Mages.</div> </div> </div> </div>It would also be... A bit nonsensical.<br /> <br /> Do you, for example, not think that both sides would immediately start to try to rally their people to conquer the other?<br /> <br /> Do you think that there wouldn't be native Americans shamans spying the European cities before the 1500's? <br /> <br /> I honestly dislike this. Why wouldn't the shamans give gunpowder to their tribes before the arrival of the Europeans?<br /> <br /> If it were up to me, all this &quot;exploring other worlds&quot; in a physical sense (as in, walking on the surface of Venus for example) would go away.<br /> <br /> Heck, ancient people didn't even know that those shiny dots in the sky are huge cosmic fireballs, and that a few of those are rocky planets like ours... How could they explore Mars if they thought that Mars was just shiney thing in the firmament?<br /> <br /> Don't we always say that &quot;Paradigm is king&quot;?<br /> <br /> So how could they do that if they didn't believe that to be the case?<br /> <br /> Now, if we were to talk about spiritual exploration then yes, absolutely. But physical exploration of outer space? Yeah, I don't like that at all.<br /> <br /> And I also don't like the idea of American Shamans and Bhramin priests from India and Taoist Monks from China plotting shenanigans with pointy hats from the OoH in Europe.<br /> <br /> This would make a globalized world of Mages ever since ever, and that makes no sense, and that also doesnt make any sense of why wouldn't the Order of Hermes try a power play in China, or why wouldn't the Wu Lung try to wage covert secret Magic war against the Choristers, and so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> I strongly believe that such concepts should be erased from the story","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T08:07:26-07:00","id":1539474,"title":"","content":"Not erased entirely, IMHO; but seriously curtailed. Awakened society can historically be ahead of the curve where exploration and travel are concerned; but I'm thinking mainly of Explorators landing on the shores of the American continents in small numbers a century or so before the New World entered the public awareness of Europe, not the notion that there's been a global Awakened society throughout recorded history.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T08:07:29-07:00","id":1539475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539442#post1539442\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I could see the overall Ascension War being a three-way conflict instead of the two-way Traditions vs. Technocracy we're familiar with. The third faction would cover Christianity, Islam, and Judaism: an Assembly of Faith. I would see four Customs (this faction's counterpart to Traditions and Conventions):<br /> <br /> \u2022 The Ahl-i-Batin, retooled to be more Islamic and not \u201cgnostic\u201d.<br /> \u2022 The Scions of God, a faction patterned after Catholicism which has influence in the Catholic Church, the Eastern Orthodox Church, the Anglican Church, and other Christian denominations that maintain a strong hierarchal structure among the clergy.<br /> \u2022 An as-yet unnamed group that collectively represents the various Protestant denominations that have largely abandoned the clergy/layman divide.<br /> \u2022 the Lions of Zion, a group introduced in the Sorcerers Crusade Companion who serve as guardians and spiritual leaders of Judaism.<br /> <br /> I see this as a separate faction in part because I don't see any of these groups being inclined to ally with \u201cgnostics\u201d who argue that God is an evil Demiurge, and in part because the Catholic Church has had about as much influence in Europe throughout most of it's history as the likes of the Wu Lung have had in China. In a similar way too how the Wu Lung never saw any need for allies until fairly recently, I don't see why the Catholic group would team up with anyone throughout most of its history. And while the Protestants would be largely at odds with the Catholics for most of their shared history, they'd nonetheless find far more common ground with the Catholics than with pretty much anyone else.<br /> <br /> Bringing in the Batini and/or the Lions of Zion would be more of a stretch, but still less of a stretch than bringing <i>anyone else </i>would be.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like that idea. In particular because I think that a so called &quot;Order of Reason&quot; should only ever truly arise after the Enlightenment (or come up to be a short bit before it, becoming its catalyst).<br /> <br /> Before that it would be the &quot;Church's Mages&quot; against everybody else, and &quot;da sCience&quot; was that which was sanctioned by the &quot;church Cabal&quot;, and represents the &quot;Divine Will&quot;.<br /> <br /> Thus, &quot;sCience&quot; as a Paradigm is born out of the &quot;Christian Cabal&quot;, as an attempt to &quot;understand divine will&quot;, much like in our own world, but at the Enlightenment, they break away from the church (to the point of embracing full atheism), and it is there that the Order of Reason is born.<br /> <br /> The Order of Reason then will quickly infiltrate the churches and try to bring it down.<br /> <br /> <br /> Oh, for Islam we cannot forget that there's several divisions too, but at least two antagonistic factions would need to exist, Shia and Sunni of course.<br /> <br /> And I doubt we would ever see those uniting with the christians and Jews.<br /> <br /> Those &quot;religious cabals&quot; would, funnily enough, work better as disparate crafts... Granted, they would be huge, but ultimately, they would be alone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T08:14:56-07:00","id":1539477,"title":"","content":"Agreed about the Order of Reason having a later start. But by the same token, unless the Traditions are revised so that they're no longer a reaction to the Order of Reason, they <i>too</i> would have a later start, with the Sorcerers Crusade taking place in the 1600s rather than the 1400s. Before that, you'd have proto-Traditions; but they'd mostly be no different than the disparate Crafts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T08:36:23-07:00","id":1539478,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539444#post1539444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Stuff</b></div> </div> </div> </div>That is absolutely brilliant! I loved it all.<br /> <br /> And hey, let's be honest here, what type of game would Mage be without some good old fashion secret hooded conspiracies in it?<br /> <br /> That's the core of the game, after all, what is the &quot;Technocratic Union&quot; if not the Illuminati conspiracy of the setting?<br /> <br /> I love this. That way, our beloved &quot;Illuminati&quot; (technocrats) not only have a presence in the game, but they have an actual, sensible, history.<br /> <br /> And it also creates an interesting dynamic, an interesting conflict. It all stars with theological conflict between Christians and Gnostics, Christians win, Gnostics go underground and for centuries work from the shadows, infiltrating and eroding all social institutions, until coming back with a vengeance.<br /> <br /> In that vein, the split between the Union and the Masons could be much later on - perhaps even at the 20th century.<br /> <br /> Hmm... Maybe the Masons could be the &quot;Political Convention&quot;, the NWO remains as the &quot;Information Convention&quot; and the Syndicate is the &quot;Money Convention&quot;. Then, because the Masons remain too reactionaries while the rest of the Union is more &quot;progressive&quot; (not exactly in &quot;social&quot; issues, but more in matters regarding the Time Table and whatnot), then they are expelled by the 19th or the beginning of the 20th century.<br /> <br /> I also always liked the idea that the Soviet Union (and Maoist China) were a byproduct of the Masons. So, my suggestion is that, after they are kicked, they start the Russian Revolution.<br /> <br /> Thus, the &quot;Cold War&quot; is actually a sort of &quot;Civil War&quot; between two factions of Technocrats - the &quot;regular&quot; Technocratic Union &quot;controls&quot; the West and the &quot;Rogue Masons&quot; controlling the Comminterm","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T09:04:59-07:00","id":1539480,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539477#post1539477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Agreed about the Order of Reason having a later start. But by the same token, unless the Traditions are revised so that they're no longer a reaction to the Order of Reason, they <i>too</i> would have a later start, with the Sorcerers Crusade taking place in the 1600s rather than the 1400s. Before that, you'd have proto-Traditions; but they'd mostly be no different than the disparate Crafts.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Now that I've come to think a bit about all discussed so far... I really like the direction of things.<br /> <br /> The most interesting thing is that this changes the Ascension War to actually be... A Spiritual War. And we could say that it begun 2000 years ago, except that it was covertly instead of overtly. It begins as a disagreement between early Christians and Gnostics, while they were both still being fed to the lions in Rome!<br /> <br /> A spiritual war between persecuted slaves that adhere to a new and subversive religion that threatens the stability of the Empire, those slaves being eviscerated in brutal games, and still they manage to find division and conflict due to theological issues.<br /> <br /> There, one of the groups wins, casts out and persecute the loser, and those losers fade into the shadows but never disappear, and for the next 15 centuries wage their Ascension War against the victors. Names change and ideas evolve, but the goal remains the same.<br /> <br /> Then, by the time of the Enlightenment, these &quot;sleeper cells&quot; emerge like the wrath of God, and the tables are finally turned... And the world shakes.<br /> <br /> Now, I would separate the Order of Hermes and the Masons as groups. First, I wouldn't exactly name them as such... They were more like &quot;individual cells&quot; rather than a large political group. By the time of the Enlightenment, Hermeticism was pretty much dead in the real world, but Masonry was on the rise.<br /> <br /> It is likely that they are the SAME group, Masons and Hermetics - or better saying, they are the same &quot;school of thought&quot;. There is, Mason Lodges and Hermetic Houses have pretty similar practices and philosophies.<br /> <br /> I would say that the Magi that nowadays call themselves as the &quot;Order of Hermes&quot; were the ones left out of the Order of Reason. At some point during the Enlightenment, the Masons form the Order of Reason; those that didn't receive or refused the invitation, become the Order of Hermes as we know it","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T10:23:55-07:00","id":1539482,"title":"","content":"What's more, by throwing the official formation of the Order of Reason to the 1600's, not only does it make more sense due to how society in the 1600's was more fit for the idea than in the 1400's (with the power of the church greatly diminished when they finally succeed in their power play during the Enlightenment), but it also makes more sense for the creation of the Traditions.<br /> <br /> Now, instead of some Shamans having visions of the future, their people is actually facing the effects of colonization.<br /> <br /> There's a few things thou:<br /> <br /> - The &quot;Curia&quot; (I LOVED that name!) will remain antagonistic towards the Traditions just as much as towards the Order of Reason at least until the 19th century. After the 19th century thou, their power over the church is all but spent, with the Order of Reason dominating most of it, and the church itself is but a husk of its former glory. That's when the Curia joins the Traditions - a gesture that, to them, means admitting defeat. <br /> * From that point forward, the Curia changes from the Inquisitors of old to proponents of religious freedom and ecumenist doctrines.<br /> * Hardcore Curian reactionaries go underground, refusing to side with the &quot;pagans and heretics&quot;, and form their own dwindling Craft of zealots<br /> <br /> - The Bhramins of India won't be in the Traditions until the British colonization. British Technocrats try to reform Indian culture. The Bhramins coalesce into a single group to join the Traditions and start the independence movements. India will be free from the Brits one day, but never from the Technocracy, that is present in their government.<br /> <br /> - The Hermetics and Masons use their influence to create the Jesuitical Order in order to protect the natives from the Curia - but they ultimately fail as the Curia uses the new order to convert the natives. <br /> <br /> - Groups of Masons and Hermetics form the Order of Reason in the 1600's. Those left out will coalesce into modern day Order of Reason, and become bitter enemies. The Order of Hermes forms the Traditions, calling the Order of Reason &quot;heretics&quot; and &quot;apostates&quot; that abandoned their sacred &quot;gnostic-ist&quot; worldview and embraced atheism. Animists, Totemists and Pagans from Europe the Americas and Central Asia are the first to join. The Ascension War gains new heights, as Curia, Order of Reason and Traditions fight an open (and covert) bloody war.<br /> <br /> - In Asia, the Wu Lung are unfazed by the troubles of barbarians. When British Technocrats get into China offering an alliance and several of their trinkets and an open sit amongst equals, the Wu Lung reply arrogantly in LATIM (the last language they had contact with from those &quot;European barbarians) for them to get lost.<br /> <br /> - The Technocracy answers the Wu Lung with the Opium War. The century of humiliation for China comes next, and the Technocrats will posit themselves in the courts of all the Warlords that carve out China afterwards, forever pushing the Wu Lung away from power. Defeated, they finally join the Traditions, and are the last guardians of the ancient Chinese culture. China will later on descent into civil war between the two factions of Technocrats, the Capitalist and Communist Technocrats, which the Wu Lung will be powerless to stop. By the end of the 19th century they finally join the Traditions.<br /> <br /> - In Japan, the Shintoists adamantly refuse contact with barbarians, until American Technocrats force them with cannons. The Japanese Mages, rather than trying to fight the Technocracy, EMBRACE IT. And thus begins the Meiji Restoration. The few traditional Shinto become rogues and go into hiding.<br /> <br /> - The African Fetishists don't care about the rest of the world, being happy to trade slaves with forces from Order of Reason. That is, until the scramble for Africa. The Technocracy absolutely destroys them, and in shatters, they pledge allegiance to the Traditions by the 19th century.<br /> <br /> - There are Fetishists in the Americas that join the Traditions earlier thou, coming from groups of slaves. <br /> <br /> - By the end of the 19th century, the Order of Reason, now officially &quot;The Union of the Technocratic Conventions&quot;, arrogantly declares officially its victory. The Technocracy won, the world is theirs. Their new and more fit name is just a reflection of that victory. The last bastion of resistance is the Middle East, but even the decadent Ottoman government is already technocratic.<br /> <br /> - Following their victory, the Technocracy becomes complacent and corrupt. While they still fight the superstitious and the unnatural and while they still follow the Time Table, the Technocrats now care more about power than about ideology. Their Amalgams will soon adhere to national identities more than to the ideals of the Union. Imperialism will swiftly follow, with Technocrats fighting against one another for the sake of their Imperial power base just as much as they fight the Traditions or other forces.<br /> <br /> - As a consequence of the internal power struggle of the Union, WW1 happens. A group of disillusioned Technocrats tired of the state of it, will coalesce in Russia and will change the world forever...<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b- Russia: the Masons of Russia adhere to the call of the Order of Reason but... They have their own agendas. Instead of the progressives in France, the Russians are a reactionary lot. In time, the Technocracy (Order of Reason at the Time) will turn more and more Reactionary. Funnily enough, in time, the Masons will be kicked out of the Order; but in Russia, they will rally against it, this time however with the roles reversed: the rest of the Order turns reactionary, while the rebel Masons turn Progressive (the elites in Russia will embrace the reactionary side of the Technocracy, while those Mages bellow them turn revolutionary). In the communist block, the Masons hunt down the Syndicate to extinction; in the Capitalist block, the Syndicate returns the favor. The other Conventions simply split between the two. The NWO in Russia creates the NKVD and then the KGB. The Void Engineers gain great prestige between the Comminterm, being the first ones to put Space Travel into the Time Table, prompting the Syndicate and the NWO counterpart in the Capitalist block to increase the fundings for the VE of the West to reach the Moon. Meanwhile, the Progenitors of the Comminterm commit to atrocious human experiments.<br /> <br /> - The Traditions and Disparate Crafts try to capitalize in the fractured Technocratic world, but are largely unsuccessful. In the Comminterm, they are brutally crushed by the Masonic project of a perfect society.<br /> <br /> - Under the &quot;Red Fear&quot;, several Rogue agents coalesce into a new ideology, coming from rogue agents from both the Traditions and the &quot;Classical&quot; (Liberal-Capitalist) Technocracy, in what will later be called as the &quot;Unholy Alliance&quot;. Yes, it is Nazi-Fascism. Pagans, Christians, even Muslims and Technocrats alike join forces and work together in Germany, Italy and Japan. Technological wonders create grotesque engines of war blessed by Shamans and Priests.<br /> That alliance unleashes WW2, which causes an uneasy alliance between both the 2 rival Technocratic factions and the Traditions against them.<br /> After the war is over, it gets evident the heavy Nefandic influence that lead to such an abomination. The nefarious ideology of Nazi-Fascism will be defeated but not destroyed, remaining as a spectre lurking in the shadows rallying Nefandic forces to it.<br /> WW2 serves as a wake up call to all sides in the Ascension War. Never before were the Nefandi considered as serious players in the game. Now, all sides would be wary to be vigilant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T11:03:25-07:00","id":1539483,"title":"","content":"I'd be inclined to keep the Curia distinct from the Traditions until much later: even in the 20th century, the influence of the Church was a force to be reckoned with, and I wouldn't see the Curia admitting defeat and joining a power bloc until the middle to late 20th century at the earliest. Even then, the Traditions would need to be considerably less gnostic for the Curia to join with them: you'd need to re-envision the Traditions more along the lines of M20's Disparate Alliance, where there is no overall \u201cwe're all doing the same thing in different ways\u201d glue holding them together, before the Curia would be willing to join: they'd be more inclined to side with <i>Technocrats</i> than with pagans. Whether the Technocrats would reciprocate is a different story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-29T11:17:25-07:00","id":1539485,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539483#post1539483\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd be inclined to keep the Curia distinct from the Traditions until much later: even in the 20th century, the influence of the Church was a force to be reckoned with, and I wouldn't see the Curia admitting defeat and joining a power bloc until the middle to late 20th century at the earliest. Even then, the Traditions would need to be considerably less gnostic for the Curia to join with them: you'd need to re-envision the Traditions more along the lines of M20's Disparate Alliance, where there is no overall \u201cwe're all doing the same thing in different ways\u201d glue holding them together, before the Curia would be willing to join: they'd be more inclined to side with <i>Technocrats</i> than with pagans. Whether the Technocrats would reciprocate is a different story.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I agree. Not until the middle-late 20th century would they do it. AND there would be a rebel faction that would go rogue and hidden (everybody goes underground to plot revenge centuries later, I'm absolutely loving this lol)<br /> <br /> And yes, perhaps the Traditions could work better in the likes of the Disparate Alliance","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T08:46:42-07:00","id":1539528,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure the Curia would ever &quot;admit defeat&quot; any more than the Church itself would because they are largely intertwined with it. Most likely they are part of the Church in a way not dissimilar to Franciscans or Jesuits are, wielding political power within it but ultimately subservient to it. Factions within the Curia might rise and fall and even schism as the Church does, but if the group itself is playing the role of orthodox Catholic mages, that position will be around as long as the Church is.<br /> <br /> Also, because I suspect that they would be a diverse lot within the bounds of that orthodoxy, they wouldn't change from Inquisitors to ecumenists as a group. Rather there would be both within the faction with one or the other (or neither) in ascendency at various times. I mean, its not like the Inquisition itself is even gone, it just changed its methods and its name. It's currently the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith and it is quite active. <br /> <br /> All that said, they would still be willing to work with other factions depending on who, why, and how that work is handled. I think <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> has the right of it. They are much more likely to be a part of a Disparate alliance than under a Council of Traditions.<br /> <br /> <br /> Going back to the 1400s, lets say that the idea of the Consensus is still discovered at around the same time. If we are diverging from Mage history here are three important questions: Who discovers it? Who is interested in using it? How fast do they move?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T09:09:49-07:00","id":1539530,"title":"","content":"I actually think the Church might be the ones who would fire the opening shots of the Ascension War, and I think they'd move quick. Understand that throughout history, most people have been terribly interested in ensuring correct orthodoxy. The ruling classes don't give much of a shit so long as you pay lip service to the imperial religion, whatever it is, and the lower classes have bigger problems to worry about. I won't say that Christian interest in conversion and obedience to orthodoxy is unique, but it's certainly unusual. As a large organization stretching across Europe, with many scholars and agents, they are well-positioned to make use of Consensus.<br /> <br /> This may not actually scuttle the Technocracy's formation--many of the earliest people imagining scientific utopias were also committed Christians, and in canon the Technocracy was already intertwined with the Church for a long time.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539410#post1539410\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The two external enemies are the &quot;Janissaries&quot; (Islamic Mages) and the &quot;Pagans&quot;. The Pagans are fractured and disorganized, but Islam is the greatest threat to the Chorus (behind the Hermetics thou).<br /> <br /> North America is Totemism, South America, Central Asia and Russian Steps is Animist, India is Hinduist with syncretist Buddhism, China is dominated by the highly rigid, hierarchical and pretty solidified Wu Lung, that holds a meritocratic system that is integral to the Empire, leaving no room for other groups inside of the Middle Kingdom. The Wu Lung consider themselves as members of the &quot;Celestial Bureocracy&quot;, and practice Taoism, Confucianism and Syncretic Buddhism and care nothing about the many &quot;barbarians&quot; at the edges of the Middle Kingdom.<br /> <br /> In India, Bhramin Mages wage as many petty wars against one another as the many kingdoms upon which they reside.<br /> <br /> Subsaharan Africa is Fetishist.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a really horrible understanding of religion and anthropology that I would suggest no one bother using. You cannot just refer to entire regions as &quot;totemist,&quot; &quot;animist,&quot; or &quot;fetishist,&quot; which are terms without any actual meaning to boot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T09:46:07-07:00","id":1539531,"title":"","content":"As an addendum, if there really is better worldwide communication between mages, I would see something like the Disparate Alliance emerging earlier than the Traditions. Why? Because the Traditions are primarily a reaction to the Technocracy specifically while I've always interpreted the Disparates as emerging as a reaction to imperialism. In real life, there wasn't really a widespread anti-imperial alliance, but that was partly due to political divisions and lack of communication and transport. The latter isn't an issue for mages, and the former is less of an issue because mages are less attached to existing political institutions. It would struggle to get off the ground at first, but eventually people are going to notice that the Curia is bulldozing their way through much of the world and doing the occasional genocide. Combining forces is going to seem like the only realistic option to a lot of people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T10:17:38-07:00","id":1539534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539530#post1539530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I actually think the Church might be the ones who would fire the opening shots of the Ascension War, and I think they'd move quick. Understand that throughout history, most people have been terribly interested in ensuring correct orthodoxy. The ruling classes don't give much of a shit so long as you pay lip service to the imperial religion, whatever it is, and the lower classes have bigger problems to worry about. I won't say that Christian interest in conversion and obedience to orthodoxy is unique, but it's certainly unusual. As a large organization stretching across Europe, with many scholars and agents, they are well-positioned to make use of Consensus.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's possible, but I'm inclined against the idea that they'd be early users of the Consensus. They were only well positioned to use it because they were already spreading orthodoxy for their own reasons, the same ones they already had before the 1400s. I don't think it would motivate the Curia much more than they were already motivated, and they'd continue at the same pace they already were. <br /> <br /> I suppose it really depends on what the initial conflict and why the first shots are fired and the first moves made.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T10:41:46-07:00","id":1539537,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539485#post1539485\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I agree. Not until the middle-late 20th century would they do it. AND there would be a rebel faction that would go rogue and hidden (everybody goes underground to plot revenge centuries later, I'm absolutely loving this lol)</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, I've already posited a \u201crebel faction\u201d going rogue. But not hidden; and not splintering off in the 1900s. There's a reason why \u201cProtestant\u201d includes the word \u201cprotest\u201d. <br /> <br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> said, the Curia wouldn't \u201cadmit defeat\u201d; nor would it ever join a Council of Magick that's pushing the idea that pagans, spiritualists, necromancers, and the like are all ultimately doing the same thing that they are. That's why They'd only join something more along the lines of the Disparate Alliance, where there's no pressure to be ecumenical. They'd <i>rather</i> be part of the Technocratic Union; and the main reason they aren't is because the Union doesn't want them. In many ways, the Curia is very similar to the Gabrielites, save only that they weren't destroyed when they were kicked out of the nascent Union: they have enough of a power base in the various organized Churches to withstand efforts by the Technocrats to destroy them. They're a Craft, albeit a very powerful one. <br /> <br /> The Protestant breakaway faction, on the other hand, needs an ecumenical philosophy to hold together as a coherent faction. Whether or not they'd be willing to extend that to non-Christian factions to the extend needed to join the Traditions is a separate issue; but I <i>could</i> see them being more open to joining the Traditions than the Curia would be, even if they make an effort to not get <i>too</i> sucked into what the Traditions are pushing. \u201cBe <i>in</i> the world, but not <i>of</i> the world.\u201d<br /> <br /> Let's say that <i>that's</i> this setting's version of the Celestial Chorus. Mostly-Protestant Christian ecumenists rather than pseudo-Theosophists, who have joined the Traditions but don't feel terribly comfortable with their membership. In part because they fear that their membership will eventually lead to heresies springing up amongst them. Which has, in fact, happened: there are fringes of the Chorus who in fact <i>have</i> bought into Gnosticism and who resemble the published Celestial Chorus with its \u201cwe are all fragments of the One, and just need to be reminded of that\u201d mindset. It's not a <i>popular</i> opinion amongst this Chorus, and those Choristers who hold such a view tend not to voice it too loudly; but it's there. <br /> <br /> Conceptually, you'll mainly find bishops, priests, and nuns among the Curia. You mostly <i>won't</i> find them among the Celestial Chorus, though there are exceptions; instead, the Chorus tends to be decentralized (often with each congregation having no Earthly authority over it), and mainly features ministers and deacons. Given their lack of a central authority, it's a minor miracle that the Chorus has remained as harmonious as it has.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T10:46:17-07:00","id":1539538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539534#post1539534\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's possible, but I'm inclined against the idea that they'd be early users of the Consensus. They were only well positioned to use it because they were already spreading orthodoxy for their own reasons, the same ones they already had before the 1400s. I don't think it would motivate the Curia much more than they were already motivated, and they'd continue at the same pace they already were.<br /> <br /> I suppose it really depends on what the initial conflict and why the first shots are fired and the first moves made.</div> </div> </div> </div>The notion of a subjective reality is something I'd see the Hermetics push. Not the Masons, and not the Curia. It very definitely fits the Hermetic notion that \u201creality is whatever I Will it to be.\u201d","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T11:04:18-07:00","id":1539540,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539538#post1539538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The notion of a subjective reality is something I'd see the Hermetics push. Not the Masons, and not the Curia. It very definitely fits the Hermetic notion that \u201creality is whatever I Will it to be.\u201d</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed, and I think a syncretic faction, one that already deals with a lot of merged paradigms, is more likely to even notice the Consensus in the first place. It's sounding like the Hermetic Schools, (if we keep that idea) are a good source for the initial idea. <br /> <br /> Not the only one, though. It doesn't have to start in Europe. I only have a passing familiarity with the history of Africa or the Americas in this time period. Is there a place on those continents where a lot of different cultures were mingling in one place in the 1400s?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T11:10:23-07:00","id":1539541,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539531#post1539531\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As an addendum, if there really is better worldwide communication between mages, I would see something like the Disparate Alliance emerging earlier than the Traditions. Why? Because the Traditions are primarily a reaction to the Technocracy specifically while I've always interpreted the Disparates as emerging as a reaction to imperialism. In real life, there wasn't really a widespread anti-imperial alliance, but that was partly due to political divisions and lack of communication and transport. The latter isn't an issue for mages, and the former is less of an issue because mages are less attached to existing political institutions. It would struggle to get off the ground at first, but eventually people are going to notice that the Curia is bulldozing their way through much of the world and doing the occasional genocide. Combining forces is going to seem like the only realistic option to a lot of people.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing that makes the Traditions unique is their shared philosophy that \u201cwe're all doing the same thing; we're just going about it in different ways\u201d. That's not a \u201creaction to the Technocracy\u201d, especially since the Technocracy is an in-game representative of imperialism: there's a reason why the Order of Reason was reforged as the Technocratic Union in the British Empire. <br /> <br /> That said, I like the suggestion made earlier that there are (or at least <i>were</i>, in the late 20th century) <i>two</i> Technocracies in this setting, one heavily featuring the Syndicate and representing the Cold War's First World (the US and it's allies), and the other heavily featuring the Masons and representing the Cold War's Second World (Russia and its allies). Whether they've remained distinct or have been merging with each other in the 21st century I'd a separate question. <br /> <br /> But I <i>could</i> see the Curia being part of the \u201cFirst World Technocracy\u201d as late as the 1980s. <br /> <br /> Finally, I also like the idea that WWII was when the Technephandi first made themselves known. I'd still present the Nephandi as an \u201cinfection\u201d rather than as a separate faction, with (for example) Mammonites being what you get when the Nephandi corrupt members of the Syndicate; but WWII was when it became apparent just how far the Nephandic influence had spread.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T11:57:22-07:00","id":1539544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539540#post1539540\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not the only one, though. It doesn't have to start in Europe. I only have a passing familiarity with the history of Africa or the Americas in this time period. Is there a place on those continents where a lot of different cultures were mingling in one place in the 1400s?</div> </div> </div> </div>For Africa I would probably point to Timbuktu as a center of knowledge for intra-African cultural exchange, and while Europeans were just starting to travel to sub-Saharan Africa they had well established exchange with any of the major North-African ports. The Swahili coast (Zanzibar, Mombasa) was very active with Middle Eastern, Indian, and Asian trade and cultural exchange.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T12:08:52-07:00","id":1539545,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a>: I don't think the Curia would see their use of the Consensus as doing something fundamentally different from what they were already doing. Though IRL some Church figures held that the very existence of magic was mere superstition, the dominant opinion in the Curia must surely be that rival sources of magic come from demons and other malign beings. They actually encounter magic, after all. Now imagine that they start to notice that in areas that have been converted, the magic stemming from these beings is weaker and in areas that haven't been, their own miracles are weaker. This is the beginnings of starting to understand and weaponize Consensus as a way to improve conversion efforts (and make imperialism easier). It's simply an outgrowth of what they were already doing.<br /> <br /> I don't think the Curia declares that reality is the product of Consensus. This would go against their theology. Instead, they likely say something more along the lines that through faith, the community can strengthen the miracles of the holy men and through wickedness others can weaken your link to God.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a>: The Traditions <i>are</i> a reaction to the Technocracy, though--they formed after the Order of Reason did and one of the First Cabal's tasks was to fight the Order of Reason. Not all of them believe they're doing the same thing either; they might believe it's all forms of magic but they have very different paradigms and I don't think the average Verbena looks at an Etherite waving around a raygun and thinks, &quot;Ah yes, they must actually be a witch who's deluded themselves into thinking they need to make a mechanism to shoot lightning.&quot;<br /> <br /> As far as the idea of a Masonic Technocracy that is involved with Russia... I think that's ridiculous, and as tying the USSR and Masonry together was actually a fascist conspiracy theory I think that idea is better thrown in the dustbin. Also the actual USSR outlawed Freemasonry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T12:19:39-07:00","id":1539546,"title":"","content":"As far as mingling in the Americas: I would suggest the Triple Alliance's tributary system, which had subjugated a large swath of Mexico and was trading with what is now currently Arizona. There were also a ton of ethnic groups in the area, and the various Maya groups had some level of contact with them too.<br /> <br /> For further up in North America... unfortunately, by the 1400s Cahokia has declined pretty badly. I don't know of any big trading centers that had risen to replace it, but it would work if you messed with the timeline of Mage a little.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T12:32:06-07:00","id":1539547,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539545#post1539545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a>: The Traditions <i>are</i> a reaction to the Technocracy, though--they formed after the Order of Reason did and one of the First Cabal's tasks was to fight the Order of Reason.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Traditions are a consequence of <i>The Seer</i> predicting something along the lines of mystical traditions becoming lost as iron and machines take over the world.<br /> <br /> I always read that as a reaction to the future encroaching of the Technocracy, and I think that was the canon interpretation too.<br /> <br /> But, now that I think about it, it <i>should </i>have been a reaction to Imperialism, since the traditions that the founder of the Extatics would see &quot;weakening&quot; would more than likely be the cultures that were victim to the cultural expansionism of &quot;the west&quot; (and not, say, the christianity that was very intently exported alongside European Imperialism, that, as said, it's represented by the OOR - unless we want to say that the forming of the Traditions caused that somehow).<br /> <br /> So, in the context of this general idea I think it could be OK if the Disparate Alliance formed <i>first</i> as an effort of many taditions to survive, and then the Traditions formed latter as an offshoot of syncretists that was formed within the Alliance itself as consequence of the exchanges of so many disparate traditions of mages figthing for a roughly common cause","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-30-2024, 03:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T13:04:38-07:00","id":1539553,"title":"","content":"I was actually thinking, given the Traditions European bias, that they might be a consequence of the Curia/Technocracy attempting to suppress political and religious dissidents in Europe. The Technocracy has a habit of purging large parts of its organization pretty often, and often these groups survive in some form and join the Technocracy's enemies. In this case, they would have a falling out with various occult groups <i>after</i> the Disparate Alliance had already formed. The occultists are widespread and powerful enough that they're able to flee and regroup, but the Disparate Alliance isn't comfortable with them joining because not too long ago the occultists were pretty happy to be wearing the boot stamping on their faces.<br /> <br /> Fast forward a couple of decades, and another group gets purged. These ones end up joining up with the occultists. Eventually you have a large and surprisingly powerful organization made up of the people ejected from the Curia/Technocracy/whatever it's called that it failed to kill. They're in an uneasy alliance of convenience with the Disparates that probably grows closer over time. Some of the groups we know and love won't be in there--I doubt the Kha'vadi or the Verbena would end up joining--but they might get additional defectors from the Technocracy. Look in the history of science and religion for approaches that lost out at the time; these could be the seeds of entirely new traditions. The Levellers, Humboldtian science, some of the earlier utopians before notions of eternal growth really took off; all of these are potential sources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T13:07:23-07:00","id":1539554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539545#post1539545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As far as the idea of a Masonic Technocracy that is involved with Russia... I think that's ridiculous, and as tying the USSR and Masonry together was actually a fascist conspiracy theory I think that idea is better thrown in the dustbin. Also the actual USSR outlawed Freemasonry.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. The important point to me is the idea that throughout most of the 20th century, there was a First World Technocracy and a Second World Technocracy, and that the former embraced the Syndicate and possibly the Curia while the latter rejected both. I had been thinking Masonry because Mage ties the Craftmasons heavily into socialism, and the Russian Technocracy attempted to leapfrog Marx's ideas about the natural course of history, skipping right past the Capitalist stage and straight to the post-Capitalist socialism stage and the subsequent Communist endgame. <br /> <br /> But you're right; that's not the Masons. It's more like the New World Order attempting to do economics in its own \u201cBig Brother\u201d approach, as they've purged the Syndicate and <i>someone </i>has to fill their role. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T13:20:05-07:00","id":1539556,"title":"","content":"Yeah, Marx was a social scientist anyway, and a lot of figures in the USSR were political theorists. Granted, some were also economists, but it makes perfect sense to say that some of them were Technocrats of the New World Order. I'd also have H. G. Wells in it, given that he <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_World_Order_(Wells_book)\" target=\"_blank\">literally wrote the book on it</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T14:11:52-07:00","id":1539558,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539545#post1539545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a>: I don't think the Curia would see their use of the Consensus as doing something fundamentally different from what they were already doing. Though IRL some Church figures held that the very existence of magic was mere superstition, the dominant opinion in the Curia must surely be that rival sources of magic come from demons and other malign beings. They actually encounter magic, after all. Now imagine that they start to notice that in areas that have been converted, the magic stemming from these beings is weaker and in areas that haven't been, their own miracles are weaker. This is the beginnings of starting to understand and weaponize Consensus as a way to improve conversion efforts (and make imperialism easier). It's simply an outgrowth of what they were already doing.<br /> <br /> I don't think the Curia declares that reality is the product of Consensus. This would go against their theology. Instead, they likely say something more along the lines that through faith, the community can strengthen the miracles of the holy men and through wickedness others can weaken your link to God.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. They wouldn't see changing the Consensus as changing truth and they would very easily describe it as strengthening faith and righteousness, something that they already consider to be a way of spreading God's message and miracles. That's really why I don't see them capitalizing on the Consensus revelation, because it only indicates that they should do what they were already doing. At best it is just encouragement that they were already on the right path.<br /> <br /> Side note: The Curia would call magic that comes from demons and other malign beings superstition even when it works. The Catholic definition of superstition is not an unjustified belief in mystical things that don't work. Superstition is an excess of religion; it's actually putting faith in the wrong things, whether they work or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T17:07:56-07:00","id":1539562,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I wouldn\u2019t use Freemasonry as a core part of any sort of Socialist-faction/Second World Union either. The point I was trying to make in my original post regarding the Freemasons is that by the 20th century they were pretty much already in decline as a useful vessel for the organization of politically active cadres (although I do still think one could argue that elements of para-Masonic social technology has outlived the Brotherhood proper as useful means of organization). IMO by the 20th century the Masons proper are more a vestigial part of the organizational methods of the Capitalist-faction/First World Union and even then pretty much defunct by the 1970s/80s. Any true Illuminists (radical liberals dreaming of a universal world-republic) within the ranks of the Union are probably already sidelined by the time of the Victorian reconstruction. <br /> <br /> Yeah I can easily see Awakened like the Curiae as well as the Celestial Chorus and like groups not seeing the world as governed by Consensus but something more along the idea that perhaps there are Princes of the World which govern certain zones and that the interference of these powers would be what limits the power of the evangelizing Awakened in such areas and why spreading the Word, thereby weakening the grasp of the Worldly Powers, allows the faithful Awakened to more readily make use of miracle, prayer, ritual, etc. Such a concept isn\u2019t too far from ideas which have been proposed irl by religious groups, particularly the ones most interested in \u2018spiritual warfare\u2019, a concept which I\u2019ve always found ripe for narrative potential (one of the reasons I like \u2018In Nomine\u2019).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T17:31:01-07:00","id":1539563,"title":"","content":"The problem with the <i>Curia</i> preaching that magic comes from demons is that this has never been the orthodoxy for a reason. The Church deemed witches either heretics or apostates, but otherwise said their powers were either a hoax, a delusion, or simply natural phenomena.<br /> <br /> And this interpretation is important because it comes from the idea that the god of the church was all powerful, and therefore no real power could exist outside his.<br /> <br /> So the <i>Curia</i> does need a naturalistic explanation for the supernatural powers they witness, and the <i>Consensus</i>, or a similar notion, may be exactly what they need.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T17:49:03-07:00","id":1539564,"title":"","content":"Actually, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11637_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11637\" data-vbnamecard=\"11637\">Gryffon15</a> has it right: there are significant Biblical references to Satan and his followers having dominion over the Earth for the time being, and these Princes of the Earth are a major source of power that those not in the Church draw from. It's not the <i>only </i>source; there are, for example, the Longevi (faeries) who are neither Divine nor Infernal. <br /> <br /> Not only is this in keeping with some real-world Church beliefs (albeit beliefs held by the more scholarly, whereas the laymen tend to go with either God v demons or God v fakery), but it also avoids the trap that early edition portrayals of Technocrats fell into with their apparent insistence that Magick isn't real. Like the most recent depiction of the Technocracy, the Curia should acknowledge that there <i>are </i>sources of supernatural power other than theirs; but their Divine power is superior.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T18:23:03-07:00","id":1539566,"title":"","content":"The reason the Catholic Church disliked Freemasonry was that Freemasons wanted to have a subcommunity outside of State control and communicate freely. In the Early Modern period (1450-1790) the Catholic Church, like many European churches, was an arm of the State. Freemasonry defied the State and thus threatened Church authority. Further, conspiracy theorists proclaimed the Freemasons caused both the American and French revolutions in a bid to take over the world. (The Bavarian Illuminati were a minor freemason-like group that got a wildly overblown reputation as masterminds. They were more of a joke really.) <br /> <br /> By the 1920s it was fashionable to blame Freemasonry for any and all resistance to the elites of Europe from the masses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T18:34:51-07:00","id":1539567,"title":"","content":"Freemasons (or Craftmasons, if we view them as a reasonable standin for the former) would work quite well as a Tradition or Craft, actually. <br /> <br /> I'd view the French Revolution as the beginning of the Masons' \u201cfall from grace\u201d, in that it didn't play out at all the way they wanted it to \u2014 though at the same time, I'd have the French Revolution be the birth of the Convention that will eventually become the New World Order. By the early 1800s, the Masons are out of the Order of Reason, and the Lightkeepers are beginning to reorganize it into the Technocratic Union.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T18:58:01-07:00","id":1539569,"title":"","content":"Yeah of course there are huge differences between the extent of influence that irl Freemasons had and what has later been purported by occultists, but like my Sewer Gator principle, the ideas of the latter can be just as interesting for storytelling as the former.<br /> <br /> One can quibble on the extent to which Freemasonry had an impact on the revolutions of the 17th-19th century. I agree that any suggestions outside of fiction of a grand Masonic conspiracy orchestrating affairs is wildly overblown - the American, French, &amp; Haitian Revolutions would most likely have occurred even if Masonry did not exist; though in WoD it can be fun to propose that organizations such as the Craftmasons did have a large influence, if only as a personification/embodiment of the larger social &amp; ideological forces concurrent with such historic developments.<br /> <br /> However I do think one could argue that Freemasonry did provide a certain amount of cover and social technology to the men of the social circumstances who were most likely to be at the heart of these political ruptures when they occurred. These lodges would have provided a means of developing group-solidarity (akin to how a country club or chamber of commerce might do the same) as well as the requisite anonymity needed for discussion of heterodox ideas. The ubiquity of para-Masonic organizations in this period showcases imo the validity of the social tech, regardless of the purported ideology of any particular organization.<br /> <br /> That same ubiquity, imo, showcases one important element of Freemasonry that makes it valuable for storytelling in Mage. One didn\u2019t need to share Adam Weishuap\u2019s visions of a universal world-republic to find merit in his suggestion that Masonic lodges could be useful places for ambitious men to gather. The men who joined the first KKK didn\u2019t need to know or care about Albert Pike\u2019s elaborate quasi-theosophical para-Masonic occult schema. These organizations did have beliefs and ideologies that were the object of immense interest for certain members, some of whom would become revolutionary thinkers within their ideological sphere; even if others, perhaps even a majority, cared far more for the practical effects of such an organization\u2019s values. I find this phenomena interesting in how it speaks to a larger aspect of Mage: the image of the Mage-as-ideological-juggernaut who becomes a gravitational center for a number of other individuals, who may or may not agree with or understand the Mage, but who, for their own reasons, help to remake the world in the Mage\u2019s own image.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T19:10:40-07:00","id":1539570,"title":"","content":"Sorry, got a little away from the topic at hand in the above post and ended up moving into a more general philosophizing.<br /> <br /> To Dataweaver\u2019s point above, I\u2019d agree that by the early 1800s the Craftmasons (in so far as, imo, they represent a certain petit-bourgeoisie ideological current most relevant in the core nations of Europe around the time of the French Revolution) would have reached the zenith of their usefulness and the Order of Reason/proto-Technocratic Union would be in the midst of moving them towards the back burner as more pragmatic-managerial types take over the administration of the gains which the Craftmasons helped achieve.<br /> <br /> I don\u2019t think you necessarily see them entirely fall out of the OoR/TU\u2019s orbit then though, depending on how you want to characterize those factions. I see the Craftmasons still being a relevant factor during the periods of bourgeoise revolutionary activity in the 19th century (from Bolivar in Latin America to Garibaldi to Latin America to the Greek Expeditions to the Revolutions of 1848 etc). I do however you see the Craftmasons drifting away from the center of the OoR/TU\u2019s orbit in that time period - as it becomes increasingly clear that the predominant interests at the core of the nascent TU (here coinciding with Victoria\u2019s British Empire) would not necessarily encourage the sort of liberal revolutions the Craftmasons envisioned (at least not in the short-term of the Time Table) if the rise of such newly enlightened nations could present difficulties for the maintenance of the world-system surrounding Victoria\u2019s Empire (imagined by the core cadres of Awakened of this era as, if they were sincere, most likely the best means of carrying out a global \u2018civilizing mission\u2019). I\u2019d say that if there is a clean break it happens either in the aftermath of the revolutions of 1848, in the latter 19th century (sometime around 1870, likely in the wake of the suppression of the Paris Commune), or around the outbreak of WWI in the early 20th century.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11637,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","user":"Gryffon15","date":". 07-30-2024, 10:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T19:18:39-07:00","id":1539571,"title":"","content":"Bear in mind that in the original timeline, the Electrodyne Engineers left the Union at the turn of the century, and the Virtual Adepts broke ranks after World War II. Unless we choose to change that up, I'd be inclined to have the Masons break from the Union sooner rather than later; so 1848 sounds good to me. <br /> <br /> The more I'm thinking about it, the more I'm liking the notion of the Craftmasons as the Traditions' counterpart to the NWO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T20:37:53-07:00","id":1539572,"title":"","content":"Change the initial conditions of the Ascension War even slightly, and not only will that breakup probably not happen at the same time, the Electrodyne Engineers and Virtual Adepts might not exist. So I wouldn't be concerned about that detail.<br /> <br /> As far as the initial factions of the Disparate Alliance, potential candidates would be based in northern Scandinavia (Scandinavian colonial violence against the Sami goes back pretty far), <i>possibly</i> Lithuania (Northern Crusades), Siberia (Russian colonization), and naturally the Andes, Mesoamerica, and the Caribbean. European colonialism hadn't spread to all these places by the 1400s but by the early 16th century it had started to hit all of them, and in all these cases it was tied enough to the Church that if the Curia's activities are a driving force behind the formation of the Disparate Alliance, member groups from these areas make sense.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, the only region I know a good deal about is Mesoamerica.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T20:44:02-07:00","id":1539573,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539572#post1539572\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Change the initial conditions of the Ascension War even slightly, and not only will that breakup probably not happen at the same time, the Electrodyne Engineers and Virtual Adepts might not exist. So I wouldn't be concerned about that detail.</div> </div> </div> </div>Somehow, \u201cthe Electrodyne Engineers and the Virtual Adepts might not even exist\u201d doesn't strike me as something not to be concerned about. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-30T20:54:04-07:00","id":1539574,"title":"","content":"What I mean is if you're reworking Mage history from the ground up &quot;when did these two conventions defect in the original timeline&quot; seems relatively minor because the expected changes are much grander in scale than changing those dates.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T11:48:19-07:00","id":1539598,"title":"","content":"Well that ties into a larger problem of alt-histories in general and one of those things I always have to just make myself ignore about settings like WoD or superhero settings - that the existence of these things def changes how history shakes out, so one either embraces that and writes a radically altered setting or you have it that a relatively equal distribution of the X-factor is given to irl historical forces so that they more or less reach the same balances of power they did irl.<br /> <br /> If one posits a history of the world that plays out in a recognizable fashion, which this thread seems to, then I think it\u2019s fair to imagine that Awakened will pop up around the same forces which generated the Electrodyne Engineers/Virtual Adepts in canon, although their circumstances might be radically different then in other timelines. The Etherites are going to have to be purged or defect whatever form they take, almost by definition, as their paradigms fall away from Consensus and the Time Table. The Virtual Adepts however are interesting to me in that I don\u2019t think one necessarily has to have such a defection, rather it\u2019s causes are almost entirely ideological, inspired by a certain cypherphreak/hacker/california-ideology ethos which the VA writers were tapping into. I\u2019ve always felt that the ties between the Union and the VA should be a lot messier then usually depicted, particularly between Iteration-X and the Virtual Adepts origins.<br /> <br /> That aside I def think that the use of internal exploited groups and their history is good inspiration for the material which would come to form the basis of any Disparate Alliance/Council type org, as well as the results of colonial and imperial ventures outside the bounds of the peninsula of northwest Asia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T12:38:58-07:00","id":1539599,"title":"","content":"The Electrodyne Engineers weren't created to represent a historical group; they were created to give an origin for the Sons of Ether, who in turn were created to give the Traditions a Mad Scientist archetype, and to provide a home for practitioners of fringe and outdated science. <br /> <br /> I don't think we need the Electrodyne Engineers for that. Just say that the attempt to reforge the Order of Reason as the Technocratic Union left a lot of discontented Scientists in every Convention, and they chose to defect rather than have an authoritarian hierarchy dictate to them what they ought to research.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T14:01:00-07:00","id":1539602,"title":"","content":"A contradiction in the Technocracy has always been that they claim to pursue science, yet their modus operandi is to change reality to suit their theories rather than change their theories to suit reality. This is completely against the spirit of science. I would like to see an organized group of scholars who reject Consensus entirely--not like the Society of Ether does when they say that ether theory is true, damn what the Sleepers think, but people who say that Consensus just distorts an objective reality <i>and that's bad</i>. Rather than the Technocracy's goal of getting rid of magic through the use of Consensus or the Traditionalist's goal of a Consensus which allows all paradigms, their goal is to get rid of Consensus entirely so that the underlying truth of reality can be seen.<br /> <br /> I actually think it's a more natural opinion for a lot of early natural historians to come to. If you're a deist, using Consensus to make a truth of the world false--by shunting a region into the Umbra--is something close to blasphemy, concerns over other living beings aside.<br /> <br /> Mechanically, members of this group (call it the Enlightened Society of Natural Philosophers or something) would be represented as sorcerers. A lot of discontented scientists might end up falling into their ranks, with the ones who don't want to get rid of Consensus ending up with the Traditions or Disparates (who really need an alternative name for this).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T19:08:28-07:00","id":1539609,"title":"","content":"You don't need a new faction to represent this; you just need the existing Dissidents in the Sons of Ether. The extreme versions of this would likely succumb to Quiet, and possibly go so far as to become Marauders. <br /> <br /> As for the original contradiction that you pointed out: my own take on that is to lean into Earthly Foundations, which were originally presented as \u201cthose boundaries between Coincidental and Vulgar that the Consensus finds nearly impossible to change\u201d and later got recontextualized as \u201cthe default nature of Reality, and what it reverts to whenever the influence of the Consensus is removed\u201d. To an Enlightened Scientist, Effects that are consistent with Earthly Foundations are in a sense \u201cmore true\u201d than Effects that aren't; and as a result, they try to <i>build on </i>those Foundations, preferring to craft Effects that are Coincidental no matter what the Consensus says because they're in accord with the Earthly Foundations. When M20 talks about Technocrats having the Coincidental Edge, that's what they're talking about; and when Technocrats talk about discovering objective truths, that's <i>also </i>what they're talking about<b></b>: an \u201cobjective truth\u201d is something consistent with the Earthly Foundations and therefore always Coincidental. <br /> <br /> Likewise, something is \u201cobjectively false\u201d when it's always Vulgar no matter what the Consensus believes; and Enlightened Scientists try to avoid those kinds of Effects. <br /> <br /> Where things get messy if that most Effects are neither: whether they're Vulgar or Coincidental depends entirely on the local Consensus. But even here, Enlightened Scientists have a working theory that not all possible Reality Zones are created equal: even when a particular Effect <i>can </i>be rendered Coincidental or Vulgar by the Consensus, it may be easier to get the Consensus to accept some Effects than others; and part of what makes Enlightened Scientists \u201cscientists\u201d is that they seek to discover what Effects the Consensus is prone to accept and which ones the Consensus is prone to reject; and then they try to develop theories to <i>predict </i>whether or not a given Effect is more likely to be accepted by the Consensus. Technocratic Reality Zones are the result of centuries of this admittedly weird application of the Scientific Method to mold the Consensus into a form that's as fully compatible with the Earthly Foundations as possible <i>and</i> as accepting of as wide a range of Effects as possible at the same time. Essentially, these tendencies of what the Consensus will accept or reject are put on a spectrum between the extremes defined by the Earthly Foundations, with things that the Consensus easily accepts being \u201cobjectively mostly true\u201d and thought of as being \u201cnear\u201d the ideal of full compatibility with an Earthly Foundation, and vice versa with things that it's nearly impossible to get the Consensus to accept. <br /> <br /> (This is actually something that belongs in my Technocratic Fields thread; but I'm bringing it up here to give you a sense of how I attempt to resolve the \u201cTechnocrats are doing Science in a world where Reality is flexible\u201d dilemma.)<br /> <br /> For the purpose of this thread, I'm thinking that there's should probably be a faction of Enlightened Scientists in the same sense as the Technocrats are, but who reject the Technocracy idea that technical experts should decide how everyone lives their lives. Setting aside the more mystical takes on the VAs (who I'll refer to as the Mercurial Elite from now on, to distinguish them from their more Scientifically grounded brothers), the VAs are actually a solid example of this: they strive to keep their Science grounded in mathematical \u201cEarthly Foundations\u201d as per my rant above; but they're anti-authoritarian, about as far from \u201cTechnocrat\u201d as you get. To the extent that the surviving Craftmasons are still Scientists, they may also fall into this group: their American Experiment was an attempt to create a governmental system that's run bottom-up rather than top-down, making it largely anti-Technocratic. (Or maybe I'm misunderstanding the Masons?)<br /> <br /> This \u201canti-Technocracy\u201d would be the go-to for those who want to play Enlightened Scientists but <i>don't </i>want to play Technocrats. In that regard, they're very similar to M20's Navalon, but without the Camelot imagery or the British roots.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T20:04:19-07:00","id":1539610,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539528#post1539528\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure the Curia would ever &quot;admit defeat&quot; any more than the Church itself would because they are largely intertwined with it. Most likely they are part of the Church in a way not dissimilar to Franciscans or Jesuits are, wielding political power within it but ultimately subservient to it. Factions within the Curia might rise and fall and even schism as the Church does, but if the group itself is playing the role of orthodox Catholic mages, that position will be around as long as the Church is.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I didn't thought of them as Catholics exclusively but... Yeah, it could do. I was thinking about them being an umbrella christian organization but... Yeah, doesn't make sense. Too much blood. There would have to be another group of Orthodox. And protestants later on too.<br /> <br /> Anyway, the reason why I said that they would &quot;claim defeat&quot; is because there's absolutely zero chance that they keep power within the Church. The Technocracy kicks them out and take over the Church for their own purposes - and not only the Catholic church, but ALL churches. Religion is too big of a tool for the Technocracy not to be in. I can easily see the NWO controlling both the Catholic church and their equivalent rebel ones in Russia controlling the Orthodox Russian church while the Syndicate controls the evangelicals churches.<br /> <br /> The way I see it, the &quot;Curia&quot; and other similar groups for the other christian denominations would envision themselves as the &quot;last true christians&quot;, but their official status within the hierarchical structures of organized Christian churches would be of EXCOMMUNICATED. There's absolutely zero chance that they would remain at the heart of the hierarchical structures of Christendom. Except perhaps as hidden agents, still trying to guide things but with several restrictions and under constant threat of being exposed.[/quote]<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, because I suspect that they would be a diverse lot within the bounds of that orthodoxy, they wouldn't change from Inquisitors to ecumenists as a group. Rather there would be both within the faction with one or the other (or neither) in ascendency at various times. I mean, its not like the Inquisition itself is even gone, it just changed its methods and its name. It's currently the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith and it is quite active.<br /> <br /> All that said, they would still be willing to work with other factions depending on who, why, and how that work is handled. I think <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> has the right of it. They are much more likely to be a part of a Disparate alliance than under a Council of Traditions. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, everything that I considered was in the context that they lose power within the Church, which I believe is the most realistic outcome, and given that fact they decide to change posture and adopt a policy of, if not &quot;the enemy of my enemy&quot;, than at least &quot;I need to survive today. I can kill you all tomorrow&quot;.<br /> <br /> Now, what I envisioned is that, under these dire conditions, they decide to &quot;seek external help&quot; for pragmatic reasons, which causes a faction to become true believers of ecumenism, a second faction to just PRETEND to adhere to it, but in fact just want to use &quot;all these damn heathens&quot; to defeat technocrats &quot;unbelievers&quot; but who are sure to turn into their new &quot;allies&quot; as soon as the deed is done and a third faction that refuses to &quot;make a pact with the devil&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Going back to the 1400s, lets say that the idea of the Consensus is still discovered at around the same time. If we are diverging from Mage history here are three important questions: Who discovers it? Who is interested in using it? How fast do they move? </div> </div> </div>Probably the Order of Hermes, or the Curia. It must be one of the two. <br /> <br /> But I think it would be interesting if the Muslim Mages discovered that too more or less at the same time and independently. (I'm just gonna call them &quot;Janissaries&quot;)<br /> <br /> And that actually makes sense. Monotheistic religions don't admit &quot;divergence of though&quot;. In India, you can worship whatever God. They'll just add them to their pantheon.<br /> <br /> In Christendom and the Caliphate, there's only one God.<br /> <br /> This is the perfection condition for the rise of an homogeneous Reality Zone. And one that greatly benefits these groups of Mages.<br /> <br /> Soon enough, the effects would become noticeable.<br /> <br /> That's also a great theme to explain the Crusades and Jihad. In that vein, those religious wars aren't just about religion - they are actually the first moves at the Ascension War. Therefore, the Ascension War is kicked in earnest as theological wars to control the world.<br /> <br /> Eventually however, the Order of Hermes + Masons would get the wind of it, and they would work towards becoming the masters of Consensus, creating the Order of Reason to expunge all others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T20:16:03-07:00","id":1539611,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539530#post1539530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is a really horrible understanding of religion and anthropology that I would suggest no one bother using. You cannot just refer to entire regions as &quot;totemist,&quot; &quot;animist,&quot; or &quot;fetishist,&quot; which are terms without any actual meaning to boot.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry but, there won't be 5000+ micro groups made of a single Mage (if that much) that are completely irrelevant to the game. There's a reason why &quot;Dreamspeakers&quot; is a thing. You can customize your Mage if you will, but no, there won't be a single unique group for each individual human tribe in History. <br /> <br /> Let's also remind that the number of Mages is miniscule. Official source is &quot;1 Awaken per 1.000.000 sleepers&quot;, which in the 1400's would mean a mere 500 Mages globally. Granted, I firmly believe this number should be summarily ignored, otherwise we wouldn't have an &quot;Ascension War&quot;, but rather a few sages hidden in mountains in half a dozen of sacred grounds, but still, that number can't be so high to the point that we get 5M Mages (1% of the population), which would mean that every single noble and his dog are Awaken.<br /> <br /> This means that many groups actually won't have a single Mage to count within their ranks, so it's not too crazy to think that not all cultures are or even SHOULD be represented. And again, nobody would take it seriously to have &quot;2000 Traditions&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, Animists, Totemists, Fetishists, Politheists. That division sticks, those are obviously umbrella terms for things that share SOME similarities, even if not perfect and if doesn't account for every bit of idiosyncrasies within them, but it is already more than just &quot;Dreamspeakers&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T20:49:20-07:00","id":1539613,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539541#post1539541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> That said, I like the suggestion made earlier that there are (or at least <i>were</i>, in the late 20th century) <i>two</i> Technocracies in this setting, one heavily featuring the Syndicate and representing the Cold War's First World (the US and it's allies), and the other heavily featuring the Masons and representing the Cold War's Second World (Russia and its allies). Whether they've remained distinct or have been merging with each other in the 21st century I'd a separate question.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks! It's nice to be appreciated.<br /> <br /> You see, the reason as to why have these changes is because we have a round hole, which is the real world history, which we then have to fit the secret &quot;illuminati&quot; game history into. It will be no good if we try to fit a square game story into our circle real world history.<br /> <br /> I always felt that there are many things that are poorly designed in the game. I mean ok, the Technocracy has conquered Consensus. They are the big dogs, the final bosses and stuff.<br /> <br /> So why did WWI happen?<br /> <br /> I mean, why the hell would the Technocracy ever allow the &quot;Great War&quot; to take place?<br /> <br /> &quot;Oh but it was serving their interests, and they were playing both sides&quot;.<br /> <br /> Yeah right. How exactly were they doing this?<br /> <br /> No, it doesn't make any sense. If an organization such as the Technocracy ever existed, they would do everything in their power to prevent WWI to happen. Because number 1, there's no sense in it; the Union already had control over the empires. And if the Union just sticks to using said Empires, they can crush all the remaining resistance to their Pogrom.<br /> <br /> Second, WWI was just a massive waste of resources, which they would do best to invest elsewhere.<br /> <br /> Honestly, if the Technocracy ever existed, they would have murdered Kaiser Wilhelm III with smallpox as a teenager, as soon as his &quot;warmonger proclivities&quot; were evident.<br /> <br /> AND the Technocracy would do everything in their power to keep both Prussia and Russia down.<br /> <br /> Let's think rationally: what would be the best course of action for the Techies?<br /> <br /> And the simple answer: to have the British Empire conquer EVERYTHING and become a true &quot;global government&quot;.<br /> <br /> Therefore, the Napoleonic Wars make no sense, the German unification makes no sense and WW1 makes no sense.<br /> <br /> How then do we make a circle that fits?<br /> <br /> Simple, the Technocracy becomes so powerful and so successful at defeating their enemies, that soon enough they become corrupt and start fighting themselves.<br /> <br /> Therefore, the &quot;Union&quot;, while still sharing in purpose and ideology and while having factions of &quot;Integralists&quot;, by and large decides itself along national identities.<br /> <br /> Hence why you have a clear Technocratic symbol such as Otto von Bismarck unifying Germany, and waging war against another Technocrat, but a French one, Napoleon III.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bWW2 can be explained by Nephandi, sure, but what about the Cold War?<br /> <br /> So, the easiest and even most logical answer is to have the Technocracy split in 2, between one group that believers in Capitalism and led by the Syndicate, and another that believes in a world driven by authority and with central planned economy, in the Masons - with the remaining Conventions splitting between the two (but remaining largely the same).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I <i>could</i> see the Curia being part of the \u201cFirst World Technocracy\u201d as late as the 1980s. </div> </div> </div>Hell yeah! I liked your idea even better!<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Finally, I also like the idea that WWII was when the Technephandi first made themselves known. I'd still present the Nephandi as an \u201cinfection\u201d rather than as a separate faction, with (for example) Mammonites being what you get when the Nephandi corrupt members of the Syndicate; but WWII was when it became apparent just how far the Nephandic influence had spread. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I like that idea too. Yes, absolutely, they are an infection, not a &quot;their own faction&quot;. They were there the whole time, and when the opportunity came, they seized it.<br /> <br /> By the way, &quot;Mammonites&quot;? Lol I loved that<br /> <br /> It's important to also notice that it weren't just the Technonephandi that were behind WW2 thou, the &quot;Thule Society&quot; could be a group of mystic nephandi that joined forces with them, and in Japan... How about &quot;Mikakabosheans&quot;? Mikakaboshe is of course, a Kami of darkness, an evil entity of Shintoism, so priests of Mikakaboshe joining forces with Imperial Technonephandi in Japan, while the Thule Society in Germany does the same, and they drag many non Nephandi Mages along too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T21:00:55-07:00","id":1539614,"title":"","content":"Note: \u201cMammonites\u201d wasn't my invention. It came directly out of the <b>Book of the Fallen</b>, as one of the new factions introduced there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T21:08:03-07:00","id":1539615,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539545#post1539545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a>: I don't think the Curia would see their use of the Consensus as doing something fundamentally different from what they were already doing. Though IRL some Church figures held that the very existence of magic was mere superstition, the dominant opinion in the Curia must surely be that rival sources of magic come from demons and other malign beings. They actually encounter magic, after all. Now imagine that they start to notice that in areas that have been converted, the magic stemming from these beings is weaker and in areas that haven't been, their own miracles are weaker. This is the beginnings of starting to understand and weaponize Consensus as a way to improve conversion efforts (and make imperialism easier). It's simply an outgrowth of what they were already doing.<br /> <br /> I don't think the Curia declares that reality is the product of Consensus. This would go against their theology. Instead, they likely say something more along the lines that through faith, the community can strengthen the miracles of the holy men and through wickedness others can weaken your link to God.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. I see both the Curia and the Janissaries as the pioneers of &quot;weaponizing Consensus&quot;, but not in understanding it. To them, their faith and the Lord's work strength the divine presence of Deus/Allah, while witchcraft weakens their holy works while strengthening the powers of the Devil/Shaitan.<br /> <br /> Therefore, both the Curia AND the Janissaries are the pioneers, but they have a distorted understanding of it.<br /> <br /> It would actually be the Order of Hermes + Masons that understand what Consensus actually is, exactly by being able to observe their effects brought up by the efforts of the Curia and Janissaries. While the Curia and Janissaries use it ostensibly, they don't actually understanding what is that they use.<br /> <br /> But the Hermetics, the inheritors of the Gnostic heritage of syncretism, belief in the Immaterialism of the world and adherents to the idea that &quot;we are all shards of the One&quot; and believers that the Material World is a creation of &quot;God's Mind&quot;, which ultimately, since &quot;we are all shards of God&quot;... Well, let's just say that, when the Hermetics begin to notice the trend, they quickly understand what it means. And that's when they begin to win, and form the Order of Reason.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As far as the idea of a Masonic Technocracy that is involved with Russia... I think that's ridiculous, and as tying the USSR and Masonry together was actually a fascist conspiracy theory I think that idea is better thrown in the dustbin. Also the actual USSR outlawed Freemasonry. </div> </div> </div>You sure have some pretty strong words for someone who contributes so little. <br /> <br /> To begin with, this is NOT &quot;Freemasonry&quot;. If you think that the hidden group of REAL MAGES we are calling as &quot;Masons&quot; are the same as the old guys at the lodge in the corner, they are not.<br /> <br /> Secondly, Freemasonry was verboten everywhere all the time. And yet, they still remained for some centuries.<br /> <br /> This is a secret group of people with Magic abilities. They aren't just lawyers and politicians and doctors meeting in secret. The game &quot;Masons&quot; may influence Lodges of Sleepers, but they are the &quot;illuminati within the Illuminati&quot;.<br /> <br /> And third, this is a silly game about preposterous Illuminati conspiracies. Go crazy or go home.<br /> <br /> You don't need to like it. But how about you giving a better alternative and actually contributing in some way to the debate?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T21:27:15-07:00","id":1539616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539562#post1539562\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, I wouldn\u2019t use Freemasonry as a core part of any sort of Socialist-faction/Second World Union either. The point I was trying to make in my original post regarding the Freemasons is that by the 20th century they were pretty much already in decline as a useful vessel for the organization of politically active cadres (although I do still think one could argue that elements of para-Masonic social technology has outlived the Brotherhood proper as useful means of organization). IMO by the 20th century the Masons proper are more a vestigial part of the organizational methods of the Capitalist-faction/First World Union and even then pretty much defunct by the 1970s/80s. Any true Illuminists (radical liberals dreaming of a universal world-republic) within the ranks of the Union are probably already sidelined by the time of the Victorian reconstruction.<br /> <br /> Yeah I can easily see Awakened like the Curiae as well as the Celestial Chorus and like groups not seeing the world as governed by Consensus but something more along the idea that perhaps there are Princes of the World which govern certain zones and that the interference of these powers would be what limits the power of the evangelizing Awakened in such areas and why spreading the Word, thereby weakening the grasp of the Worldly Powers, allows the faithful Awakened to more readily make use of miracle, prayer, ritual, etc. Such a concept isn\u2019t too far from ideas which have been proposed irl by religious groups, particularly the ones most interested in \u2018spiritual warfare\u2019, a concept which I\u2019ve always found ripe for narrative potential (one of the reasons I like \u2018In Nomine\u2019).</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm. Ok, when I said &quot;the Masons&quot; I never meant the &quot;Freemasons&quot;.<br /> <br /> The Freemasons are Sleepers. The &quot;Masons&quot; are awaken. Sure, the &quot;Masons&quot; probably hold the Freemasons under a short leash, but they are not the same.<br /> <br /> By the time of the Russian Revolution, the revolutionaries are NOT the Freemasons. But they are the &quot;Masons&quot;.<br /> <br /> Or rather, whatever decentralized group comes from the Masons - an evolution of them. I thought about remaining them, but it's a bit confuse already, which is why I've been saying &quot;Masons, Masons, Masons&quot;... I just didn't want to have to rename them all the time every single century.<br /> <br /> <i>Any true Illuminists (radical liberals dreaming of a universal world-republic) within the ranks of the Union are probably already sidelined by the time of the Victorian reconstruction.</i><br /> <br /> Yes, and that's exactly why this disaffected group starts the Technocratic Civil War which will translate into the Russian Revolution, because they have been sidelined and have witnessed both the masses and the Union fall into Imperialism. They are inspired by the Romanticism of the 19th century and still dream about the one world government.<br /> <br /> Sure, technically those wouldn't be the same &quot;Masons&quot; as before, but they hold many of the same ideas. Which is why I still call them &quot;Masons&quot; for simplicity, but I see that only caused more confusion.<br /> <br /> The Revolutionaries would be &quot;disorganized Masons&quot;. Now, it is evident that this &quot;Socialist Convention&quot; will need a name of their own (and no, just calling them &quot;Bolsheviks&quot; won't do, even if there's no name more perfect than that one)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T21:28:06-07:00","id":1539617,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539614#post1539614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Note: \u201cMammonites\u201d wasn't my invention. It came directly out of the <b>Book of the Fallen</b>, as one of the new factions introduced there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh really? Oh boy, I did not remember that. My memory is deceiving me","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T21:57:45-07:00","id":1539618,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539566#post1539566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The reason the Catholic Church disliked Freemasonry was that Freemasons wanted to have a subcommunity outside of State control and communicate freely. In the Early Modern period (1450-1790) the Catholic Church, like many European churches, was an arm of the State. Freemasonry defied the State and thus threatened Church authority. Further, conspiracy theorists proclaimed the Freemasons caused both the American and French revolutions in a bid to take over the world. (The Bavarian Illuminati were a minor freemason-like group that got a wildly overblown reputation as masterminds. They were more of a joke really.) <br /> By the 1920s it was fashionable to blame Freemasonry for any and all resistance to the elites of Europe from the masses.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539569#post1539569\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah of course there are huge differences between the extent of influence that irl Freemasons had and what has later been purported by occultists, but like my Sewer Gator principle, the ideas of the latter can be just as interesting for storytelling as the former.<br /> <br /> One can quibble on the extent to which Freemasonry had an impact on the revolutions of the 17th-19th century. I agree that any suggestions outside of fiction of a grand Masonic conspiracy orchestrating affairs is wildly overblown - the American, French, &amp; Haitian Revolutions would most likely have occurred even if Masonry did not exist; though in WoD it can be fun to propose that organizations such as the Craftmasons did have a large influence, if only as a personification/embodiment of the larger social &amp; ideological forces concurrent with such historic developments.<br /> <br /> However I do think one could argue that Freemasonry did provide a certain amount of cover and social technology to the men of the social circumstances who were most likely to be at the heart of these political ruptures when they occurred. These lodges would have provided a means of developing group-solidarity (akin to how a country club or chamber of commerce might do the same) as well as the requisite anonymity needed for discussion of heterodox ideas. The ubiquity of para-Masonic organizations in this period showcases imo the validity of the social tech, regardless of the purported ideology of any particular organization.<br /> <br /> That same ubiquity, imo, showcases one important element of Freemasonry that makes it valuable for storytelling in Mage. One didn\u2019t need to share Adam Weishuap\u2019s visions of a universal world-republic to find merit in his suggestion that Masonic lodges could be useful places for ambitious men to gather. The men who joined the first KKK didn\u2019t need to know or care about Albert Pike\u2019s elaborate quasi-theosophical para-Masonic occult schema. These organizations did have beliefs and ideologies that were the object of immense interest for certain members, some of whom would become revolutionary thinkers within their ideological sphere; even if others, perhaps even a majority, cared far more for the practical effects of such an organization\u2019s values. I find this phenomena interesting in how it speaks to a larger aspect of Mage: the image of the Mage-as-ideological-juggernaut who becomes a gravitational center for a number of other individuals, who may or may not agree with or understand the Mage, but who, for their own reasons, help to remake the world in the Mage\u2019s own image.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's my point in here. Mage is THE giant albino extraterrestrial reptilian gator at the sewer. This is a game that actually makes satire out of conspiracy theory and blows it out of proportion. What is the Technocracy if not the most schizophrenic conspiracy theory ever? The Technocracy are the Illuminati on steroids, it's a conspiracy inside a conspiracy inside of a conspiracy. <br /> <br /> And there's no better tool to play with other than the Masons - it's like the Masons only existed in rl to become a parody in the MtA. They are practically begging for it!<br /> <br /> Which is why I'll said it again: go big or go home.<br /> <br /> Now, if something like the Order of Reason were to be formed, it would be by them (perhaps with some - but not much - Hermetic aid). But then, the OoR becomes corrupt and decadent - you know, the tale as old as history - and now only cares about power.<br /> <br /> Those idealists of old become disgusted by what the OoR turns into, and begin to stirr up trouble again.<br /> <br /> I believe that the best solution would be to have the &quot;Masons&quot;, the group of Awakened ones, not the Sleeper Freemasons who are, at best, just their pawns, is actually sort of &quot;diluted&quot; after the formation of the OoR.<br /> <br /> I'll explain: they don't call themselves &quot;Masons&quot; anymore because they now simply call themselves as &quot;Order of Reason&quot;. And thus, these &quot;Masons&quot; divide themselves between the different Conventions, which is why there's no single Convention called &quot;Masons&quot; - they are, in a sense, all &quot;Masons&quot;.<br /> <br /> But the division between Conventions is an organizational and pragmatic one, not a political division. It is a division based on specialized labor.<br /> <br /> Politically, the OoR will begin to fracture between Reactionaries and Liberals, which was non existent when it formed, but as it gains more power and as it shifts from &quot;fighting the system&quot; to &quot;being the system&quot;, Reactionaries start to sprout.<br /> <br /> Then the French Revolution happens, which is the culmination of the dream of the Liberal wings of the Order of Reason, but that also culminates with their final act. Immediately, the Reactionary groups within the Order raise to fight them, and after their imposed regime of &quot;Terror&quot; and after the Napoleonic Wars (all of which sponsored by them), the Liberal Wing of the Order is all but dead.<br /> <br /> Or is it thou? Well, that's the thing, you can kill a flower but you cannot stop spring. Or, you can't kill ideas.<br /> <br /> And.... Once again... Just like the Gnostics come back with revenge in the form of the Order of Hermes + Masons creating the OoR, so too does the &quot;Masons&quot; - not the organized group that exists as a political identity, but rather the &quot;concept&quot; of them, or in other words, the romanticized idea that the Liberal Wings within the now Technocracy have about their past Order of Reason, those ideas resurface during the Russian Revolution.<br /> <br /> (Seriously now, we need a cool name for the Convention that is all about &quot;Central Planning&quot;, something that is the opposite of the Syndicate and its antithesis)<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-07-31T22:22:39-07:00","id":1539619,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539598#post1539598\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well that ties into a larger problem of alt-histories in general and one of those things I always have to just make myself ignore about settings like WoD or superhero settings - that the existence of these things def changes how history shakes out, so one either embraces that and writes a radically altered setting or you have it that a relatively equal distribution of the X-factor is given to irl historical forces so that they more or less reach the same balances of power they did irl.<br /> <br /> If one posits a history of the world that plays out in a recognizable fashion, which this thread seems to, then I think it\u2019s fair to imagine that Awakened will pop up around the same forces which generated the Electrodyne Engineers/Virtual Adepts in canon, although their circumstances might be radically different then in other timelines. The Etherites are going to have to be purged or defect whatever form they take, almost by definition, as their paradigms fall away from Consensus and the Time Table. The Virtual Adepts however are interesting to me in that I don\u2019t think one necessarily has to have such a defection, rather it\u2019s causes are almost entirely ideological, inspired by a certain cypherphreak/hacker/california-ideology ethos which the VA writers were tapping into. I\u2019ve always felt that the ties between the Union and the VA should be a lot messier then usually depicted, particularly between Iteration-X and the Virtual Adepts origins.<br /> <br /> That aside I def think that the use of internal exploited groups and their history is good inspiration for the material which would come to form the basis of any Disparate Alliance/Council type org, as well as the results of colonial and imperial ventures outside the bounds of the peninsula of northwest Asia.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, in the case of the Etherites and VA, I still believe that their defection is not only entirely possible, but almost mandatory, no matter which alt history we create.<br /> <br /> First of all, they are too freaking cool to eliminate.<br /> <br /> And secondly, the reasons for their defections is different from the Masons. The Masons are politically and ideologically driven; think about the Masons as a political liberal movement against an ever more reactionary Union. The &quot;Masons&quot; represent the initial ideals of the Order of Reason which got lost due to the corruption of power. And no matter how much the Union tries, there will always be new idealists.<br /> <br /> I actually like this scenario better than the canon Mage. In here, the Union is kind of doomed to always having to face rebellious elements. Some new idealists revolutionaries will rise within the Union, the Union will beat them down, turn even more repressive, and that in turn will ignite the flames of further rebellion.<br /> <br /> With this, the Technocratic Union is posited to fail, all the time. They'll always fail, because they are trapped in a vicious cycle out of their own hubris. Huh. That sounds poetic. <br /> <br /> That's also a Technocracy that is much more dynamic. Now, when someone says &quot;oh no, they don't control everything&quot; - yeah, they don't, and there's a reason why they don't! And the reason is that, when they become too powerful, and when they beat all their enemies into submission, they turn into each other and create the conditions for revolution, which fracture them, over and over again.<br /> <br /> So, in the end, the Technocracy ends up being its own worse enemy, like a dragon that devours itself.<br /> <br /> It is also fitting: we must not forget that the Order of Reason begun as a revolutionary movement itself, but as we all know, &quot;the revolution devours itself&quot;. Poetic <br /> <br /> Now, the VAs would probably represent the last exponents of that revolutionary impetus from the Masons of old and therefore they can stay, although I would place them in the 1960's for defection, but the Etherites is absolutely different, and it has nothing to do with politics or political ideology. The Etherites deal is strictly about Paradigm, thus it can always happen and have no regards about the political situation of the world at all.<br /> <br /> In fact, I would put the Etherites defection at some time to cause maximum impact. And although their defection is not politically driven, they could use the chaos from political situations to make the jump. So, perhaps during the Franco Prussian War would be a good time, when Imperialist Technocrats reactionaries from France and Germany are fighting each other for petty power, could be a good time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T07:33:18-07:00","id":1539636,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539610#post1539610\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, I didn't thought of them as Catholics exclusively but... Yeah, it could do. I was thinking about them being an umbrella christian organization but... Yeah, doesn't make sense. Too much blood. There would have to be another group of Orthodox. And protestants later on too.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. I could maybe... maybe... see the Curia as crossing the Catholic-Orthodox divide, since a lot of the early split was caused by miscommunication issues that mages don't necessarily share. For the most part, though, there wouldn't be an umbrella group.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Anyway, the reason why I said that they would &quot;claim defeat&quot; is because there's absolutely zero chance that they keep power within the Church. The Technocracy kicks them out and take over the Church for their own purposes - and not only the Catholic church, but ALL churches. Religion is too big of a tool for the Technocracy not to be in. I can easily see the NWO controlling both the Catholic church and their equivalent rebel ones in Russia controlling the Orthodox Russian church while the Syndicate controls the evangelicals churches.<br /> <br /> The way I see it, the &quot;Curia&quot; and other similar groups for the other christian denominations would envision themselves as the &quot;last true christians&quot;, but their official status within the hierarchical structures of organized Christian churches would be of EXCOMMUNICATED. There's absolutely zero chance that they would remain at the heart of the hierarchical structures of Christendom. Except perhaps as hidden agents, still trying to guide things but with several restrictions and under constant threat of being exposed. </div> </div> </div><br /> I really don't get why you think this is the case. By demographic there should be a lot of mages with catholic paradigms out there and the natural place for them is still within the Catholic Church. That's as true in modern times as it was in the 1400s.<br /> <br /> Also, the Catholic Church being successfully taken over by the Technocracy is much worse plot element than it sounds. It's essentially saying that the Church that Catholics base their faith around is wrong, all of the sleeper followers are duped, and only a select group of mages know the truth. It's the whole &quot;mages are the true practitioners and sleepers are just wrong&quot; all over again.<br /> <br /> Don't get me wrong, the Technocracy <i>trying</i> to take over the Church works. It certainly makes sense that they would want to. They can even get a foothold and wield political influence within it. None of that is a problem. They simply can't take it over, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T07:37:56-07:00","id":1539637,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539611#post1539611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm sorry but, there won't be 5000+ micro groups made of a single Mage (if that much) that are completely irrelevant to the game. There's a reason why &quot;Dreamspeakers&quot; is a thing. You can customize your Mage if you will, but no, there won't be a single unique group for each individual human tribe in History.<br /> <br /> Let's also remind that the number of Mages is miniscule. Official source is &quot;1 Awaken per 1.000.000 sleepers&quot;, which in the 1400's would mean a mere 500 Mages globally. Granted, I firmly believe this number should be summarily ignored, otherwise we wouldn't have an &quot;Ascension War&quot;, but rather a few sages hidden in mountains in half a dozen of sacred grounds, but still, that number can't be so high to the point that we get 5M Mages (1% of the population), which would mean that every single noble and his dog are Awaken.<br /> <br /> This means that many groups actually won't have a single Mage to count within their ranks, so it's not too crazy to think that not all cultures are or even SHOULD be represented. And again, nobody would take it seriously to have &quot;2000 Traditions&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, Animists, Totemists, Fetishists, Politheists. That division sticks, those are obviously umbrella terms for things that share SOME similarities, even if not perfect and if doesn't account for every bit of idiosyncrasies within them, but it is already more than just &quot;Dreamspeakers&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not about there being 5000 micro groupings, it's about it's about your framework not making any sense and being based on anthropology that was discarded for being racist, as heard through a bad telephone. If you're going to conflate all those groups there's no reason to separate them into those broad categories to begin with\u2014especially since those broad categories are meaningless outside of vaguely racist, antiquated anthropology or Paradox games. Seriously, read the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fetishism\" target=\"_blank\">Wikipedia article</a> on the subject and tell me anything there is worth salvaging. To the extent that there's anything real there, it could just as easily apply to the Syndicate.<br /> <br /> <i>That's</i> why no one should bother using it. Not because you need different Traditions for every tribe in North America, but because your idea is nonsensical. For instance, you referred to most of a continent as &quot;fetishist&quot; including Muslims, Christians, and an array of different polytheistic cultures. This is shockingly bad anthropology.<br /> <br /> Practically, you will have regional associations, but they're going to be based around common interests and political philosophy more than belonging to the same religious grouping. There's probably not an umbrella term that could describe the religious practices of all the Pacific Northwest's indigenous groups but that doesn't matter because the reason they're one Craft is a shared history in the region and common ideological goals. Individual mages may exchange some knowledge but the bulk of their training would be handled by sorcerers\u2026 who are also going to be the majority of the craft.<br /> <br /> In some places, there will be some overlap. Mesoamerica uses a lot of the same motifs and some beliefs are shared across cultures. However, these regions of similarity are far smaller than you may them out to be, they include elements that Ye Olde Shitty Anthroplogists would put in multiple categories of the ones you list, and none of the terms you give are appropriate. Mesoamerica wasn't polytheist/animist/totemist, it was\u2026 Mesoamerican. If you want an umbrella term you can just use that.<br /> <br /> Belief in a subtle energy that makes up all of reality (be it called teotl, chu'lel, or anything else), use of mirrors for divination, and the belief in a sort of animal companion inextricably tied to a human's lifeforce are, while not necessarily <i>universal</i> in Mesoamerica, common enough that if you wanted to create a Mesoamerican Craft that spans the region you could use that as a starting place. But if you pop over to the American Southwest or to the Caribbean there's very little in common.<br /> <br /> Yes, this means the whole globe can't be covered in a write-up, but I don't think we need to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539615#post1539615\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You don't need to like it. But how about you giving a better alternative and actually contributing in some way to the debate?</div> </div> </div> </div>I did, you just ignored it. The founders of the USSR were political theorists, they were inspired by a social scientist, and prior to Stalinization a lot of futurists supported them--socialism promised a more peaceful, egalitarian, rationally ordered world. H. G. Wells isn't the only one who favored the USSR, at least early on. J. B. S. Haldane was another.<br /> <br /> Having the Second World Technocracy be born from the New World Order's attempts to rationally organize society and create a world government to prevent another World War works quite nicely. But the nature of the New World Order is still a strongly hierarchical one, so they end up purging their own dissidents and centralizing power by taking it away from the actual worker councils and investing it in the state.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T08:25:24-07:00","id":1539641,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539637#post1539637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having the Second World Technocracy be born from the New World Order's attempts to rationally organize society and create a world government to prevent another World War works quite nicely. But the nature of the New World Order is still a strongly hierarchical one, so they end up purging their own dissidents and centralizing power by taking it away from the actual worker councils and investing it in the state.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u2026which is why I don't see a need to create a \u201cSecond World counterpart\u201d to the Syndicate: the \u201ccentral planning\u201d nature of Soviet economics makes the Second World \u201cmoney mages\u201d so intimately connected to the political masters as to be one and the same. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T10:45:36-07:00","id":1539646,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539636#post1539636\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I really don't get why you think this is the case. By demographic there should be a lot of mages with catholic paradigms out there and the natural place for them is still within the Catholic Church. That's as true in modern times as it was in the 1400s.<br /> <br /> Also, the Catholic Church being successfully taken over by the Technocracy is much worse plot element than it sounds. It's essentially saying that the Church that Catholics base their faith around is wrong, all of the sleeper followers are duped, and only a select group of mages know the truth. It's the whole &quot;mages are the true practitioners and sleepers are just wrong&quot; all over again.<br /> <br /> Don't get me wrong, the Technocracy <i>trying</i> to take over the Church works. It certainly makes sense that they would want to. They can even get a foothold and wield political influence within it. None of that is a problem. They simply can't take it over, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>It then would mean that the Church, ALL Christian denominations basically, are completely at odds with the Status quo of the world?<br /> <br /> This would mean that all organized religions - Islam, Christianism, Hinduism, Buddhism and so on - are at odds with the Status quo, and that they remain within &quot;Tradition&quot; (or whatever similar mystics Mages) control?<br /> <br /> Then yeah, the Techies would try to infiltrate, and the Mystics would use religion as a tool against the Men in Suits.<br /> <br /> That somehow... Makes a lot of sense.<br /> <br /> There would need to be a case by case analysis here thou. For instance, the Russian Orthodox clearly aligned themselves with the Reactionary Technocracy of Russia (yes, I am talking about Putin and his cronies).<br /> <br /> In some places, the Techies would go soft on these theological mystics, some times even making arrangements with them - in Japan for example, the Shinto priests absolutely entered the Union - and some other times they go medieval on their skin. <br /> <br /> And some times the Techies just push the mystics out altogether. In the case of the Catholic Church yeah, I can see the Curia and the Techies trying to control it internally, while for the Protestant churchs, given their decentralization, there are those controlled by &quot;true believers&quot; and some others controlled by the Syndicate (the &quot;Theology of Liberation&quot; in particular is very fit for being thrown into the Syndicate).<br /> <br /> Heck, the whole reformation might even have been kicked as a result of Masonic/Proto-OoR infiltration... It is crazy conspiracy of course, but a cool idea. The OoR infiltrates the Church because the Curia are their main enemies after all, and they use this to corrupt it from within, while at the same time fanning the flames of revolt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T11:26:17-07:00","id":1539647,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539637#post1539637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's not about there being 5000 micro groupings, it's about it's about your framework not making any sense and being based on anthropology that was discarded for being racist, as heard through a bad telephone. If you're going to conflate all those groups there's no reason to separate them into those broad categories to begin with\u2014especially since those broad categories are meaningless outside of vaguely racist, antiquated anthropology or Paradox games. Seriously, read the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fetishism\" target=\"_blank\">Wikipedia article</a> on the subject and tell me anything there is worth salvaging. To the extent that there's anything real there, it could just as easily apply to the Syndicate.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm aware that the term &quot;Fetishism&quot; was used almost as saying that they practice &quot;ooga booga&quot;. The problem however is that there is no other word for it, unfortunately your politically correct progressive anthropologists decided to just discard it altogether because it doesn't &quot;feel well&quot;. <br /> <br /> I don't care enough about Newspeak language that doesn't ruffle any feathers thou. I'm not so fragile to be at pain due to words. And again, since there's no other word for it, that's the one I have to use.<br /> <br /> Also, the point is not to have some highly specific definition, but rather a broad concept. It's like in biological taxonomy. To say that a fish and a cow are both vertebrates, say very little about them, except that they have one trait, one common theme that binds them in a group more similar than a fish and a worm.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>That's</i> why no one should bother using it. Not because you need different Traditions for every tribe in North America, but because your idea is nonsensical. For instance, you referred to most of a continent as &quot;fetishist&quot; including Muslims, Christians, and an array of different polytheistic cultures. This is shockingly bad anthropology. </div> </div> </div>Errr.... I never said that the African Christians, Muslims or polytheists were Fetishists. Those CLEARLY are Christians, Muslims or Polytheists. Ethiopia has the oldest denomination of Christianism. The Patriarchy of Ethiopia is their own thing, just like the Patriarchy of the Coptic church in Egypt. And the East African Muslims are East African Muslims.<br /> <br /> The &quot;Fetishists&quot; are clearly the ones that aren't in any of the other groups. Claiming otherwise would be like saying that Europe is &quot;Pagan&quot;, since the European countryside and the Norse remained as pagan for quite a while.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Practically, you will have regional associations, but they're going to be based around common interests and political philosophy more than belonging to the same religious grouping. There's probably not an umbrella term that could describe the religious practices of all the Pacific Northwest's indigenous groups but that doesn't matter because the reason they're one Craft is a shared history in the region and common ideological goals. Individual mages may exchange some knowledge but the bulk of their training would be handled by sorcerers\u2026 who are also going to be the majority of the craft. </div> </div> </div>That I can agree on.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In some places, there will be some overlap. Mesoamerica uses a lot of the same motifs and some beliefs are shared across cultures. However, these regions of similarity are far smaller than you may them out to be, they include elements that Ye Olde Shitty Anthroplogists would put in multiple categories of the ones you list, and none of the terms you give are appropriate. Mesoamerica wasn't polytheist/animist/totemist, it was\u2026 Mesoamerican. If you want an umbrella term you can just use that.<br /> <br /> Belief in a subtle energy that makes up all of reality (be it called teotl, chu'lel, or anything else), use of mirrors for divination, and the belief in a sort of animal companion inextricably tied to a human's lifeforce are, while not necessarily <i>universal</i> in Mesoamerica, common enough that if you wanted to create a Mesoamerican Craft that spans the region you could use that as a starting place. But if you pop over to the American Southwest or to the Caribbean there's very little in common. </div> </div> </div>So, Totemist? Yeah, Totemist. All I heard was &quot;bla bla bla Totemist, bla bla bla unique, bla bla bla Totemist&quot;. So yeah, Totemist.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, this means the whole globe can't be covered in a write-up, but I don't think we need to. </div> </div> </div>The other thing you're forgetting is that the Traditions - or Disparate Alliance, since it seems we are leading towards having it come before the Traditions - is formed by the Order of Hermes that was left out of the Order of Reason Party.<br /> <br /> We are talking here about the Hermetic Order, the guys that believe themselves to be GOD and that view all others are idiotic fools. They really don't care about the &quot;correct Anthropology&quot; of things. That's why in the canon game they took a good look into everybody that looked like &quot;savages&quot; and said &quot;yeah, Dreamspeakers&quot;. They didn't care about arguments regarding uniqueness. For them, the &quot;world&quot; was Europe, North Africa, Middle East, India and China, and the rest was lucky that the Hermetics did them the favor to even allow them to join at all (because after all, they are oh! so magnanimus. And yes, this is sarcasm).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I did, you just ignored it. The founders of the USSR were political theorists, they were inspired by a social scientist, and prior to Stalinization a lot of futurists supported them--socialism promised a more peaceful, egalitarian, rationally ordered world. H. G. Wells isn't the only one who favored the USSR, at least early on. J. B. S. Haldane was another.<br /> <br /> Having the Second World Technocracy be born from the New World Order's attempts to rationally organize society and create a world government to prevent another World War works quite nicely. But the nature of the New World Order is still a strongly hierarchical one, so they end up purging their own dissidents and centralizing power by taking it away from the actual worker councils and investing it in the state. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, but the problem is that the NWO will still exist in the First World. The Syndicate however won't exist in the Second World. It wouldn't be possible to reconcile a rogue faction of the NWO that solely takes the brunt of antagonizing the Syndicate and still having it at the First World. The presence of the NWO in the First World as potential turncoats would be unbearable.<br /> <br /> It's different if there's another actor holding the role of antagonistic towards the Syndicate. The &quot;Central Planners&quot;.. Hmmm... How about &quot;Utopians&quot;? A new Convention born out initially as just a vague romantic ideology at the 19th century and formalized as an entity in the Second World after the Russian Revolution. The Convention of the Utopians strive for totalitarian control and ultimate social engineering of the human nature &quot;for the greater good&quot;, and their entire thing revolves around political power. Those would be the &quot;New Masons&quot;.<br /> <br /> Then you would have the Syndicate + NWO holding power in the First World and the Utopians + (the other) NWO holding power in the Second World. The remaining Conventions likewise are divided between the two, therefore the Second World would also have their own counterparts to them - and basically, each &quot;Technocratic Union&quot; considers itself the only &quot;true one&quot;, and the other one are &quot;traitorous rogues (heretics)&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T11:32:13-07:00","id":1539649,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539641#post1539641\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2026which is why I don't see a need to create a \u201cSecond World counterpart\u201d to the Syndicate: the \u201ccentral planning\u201d nature of Soviet economics makes the Second World \u201cmoney mages\u201d so intimately connected to the political masters as to be one and the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't like the idea of having the NWO as the ones behind the Second World. And the reason is because their presence in the First World would become unbearable. The NWO would just simply be viewed as dangerous dissidents and the Syndicate would have no qualms into purging them in a &quot;Red Fear&quot; frenzy.<br /> <br /> I personally don't see that as possible, but I will adhere to the majority opinion on the matter.<br /> <br /> I personally would introduce the &quot;Utopians&quot; as a new and Second World exclusive Convention, just as the Syndicate would then become First World exclusive, and the NWO would be the Illuminati equivalents of both the CIA and the KGB. But that's my opinion. If most people don't like it, I'll stick with what the majority thinks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T11:37:14-07:00","id":1539650,"title":"","content":"What I meant was the Second World counterpart to the NWO. Call them the Utopians; that's certainly what I see their end-goal being. But they'd be a merger of politics, espionage, and economics, all with a central planning ethos.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":".  Minor typos 08-01-2024, 02:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T11:44:07-07:00","id":1539651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539650#post1539650\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I meant was the Second World version of the NWO. Call them the Utopians; that's certainly what I see their end-goal being. But they'd be a merger of politics, espionage, and economics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm... It's just that I like NWO... And I would like a CIA vs KGB specific thing, then the &quot;Utopians&quot; would be more akin to the &quot;illuminati-esc&quot; Politburo while the NWO would be the secret polices like NKVD, KGB, Stasi and so, just like their First World counterpart is the CIA, Mi6 and so on.<br /> <br /> But having the Utopians take the role of both Syndicate and NWO works too, although I still would prefer them being separated (just because I like the NWO so damn much)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T11:51:05-07:00","id":1539652,"title":"","content":"You still have the NWO \u2014 but solely as a First World Convention. <br /> <br /> This split may not be permanent, though: I'm thinking that after the Cold War ended, it's possible that the two Technocrats remerged, and that from the early 90s to today you have a single Technocracy. Or perhaps there are still two, but the Second World one shifted its HQ to China and Russia has fallen under the sway of the First World one (with the Syndicate rushing in to create the Oligarchs and the Curia reclaiming and revitalizing the Eastern Orthodox Church).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T11:58:02-07:00","id":1539653,"title":"","content":"That separation would also perhaps work better to justify the internal rivalries in the USSR. The 3 main axis of power within the USSR were the Military, the KGB and the Politburo, and they mostly hated each other and we're always playing power grabs - with the intelligence sector finally emerging victorious after Nikita Khrushchev. This could be represented as the Second World NWO struggling internally against the Utopians, and then finally kicking them out for good. <br /> Also, by the end of it, the rogue NWO manages to cut a deal with the First World Technocracy, and push all of the Utopians ideas out for good after 1991, and the last Utopians are utterly purged by them at the end of it, and the ones cynical Utopians that agree to play ball get their club membership card with the Syndicate (the famous &quot;Russian Oligarchs&quot; we have today), until the Putin faction (a puppet or member of the old school NWO of the USSR?) kicks those new Syndics out power, allowing them to keep their resources, and takes reign while redistributing the power to his NWO buddies. So, basically, Russia now is ruled by a semi independent NWO faction that is the legacy of the old Second World NWO (and now they are &quot;independent&quot; from the First World Technocracy yet again... Probably not gonna last much thou).<br /> <br /> Then we also have China. For a while, there were 2 &quot;true&quot; &quot;Technocracies&quot;... But then the Soviet-Sino split becomes a thing, and the Chinese Technocrats also split from the rest of the Second World... And cut a deal with the &quot;main&quot; First World Technocracy, that allows them to retain semi independence. But now it seems they are once again trying to become fully independent and antagonistic one more time...<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T12:13:19-07:00","id":1539654,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539652#post1539652\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You still have the NWO \u2014 but solely as a First World Convention.<br /> <br /> This split may not be permanent, though: I'm thinking that after the Cold War ended, it's possible that the two Technocrats remerged, and that from the early 90s to today you have a single Technocracy. Or perhaps there are still two, but the Second World one shifted its HQ to China and Russia has fallen under the sway of the First World one (with the Syndicate rushing in to create the Oligarchs and the Curia reclaiming and revitalizing the Eastern Orthodox Church).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, after the end of the Cold War, I think that the Russian and Chinese Technocrats are able to cut a deal that allows them to retain a somewhat level of independence, and they also allow the Syndicate to enter their domains, albeit under some level of internal control.<br /> <br /> I think we can then go with the Utopians as the counterpart of both Syndicate+NWO, then the Utopians split from the rest of the Second World in China and cut a deal with the First World Technocracy, allowing the Syndicate to enter under their control, and allegedly unite again with the &quot;main&quot; Technocracy, and then after 1991 the same goes for the entire Second World, and from 1991 up until the 2020's there was officially only one single Technocracy, but nowadays the Technocrats of both China and Russia, who retained some level of independence, are moving towards a new type of &quot;rebellion&quot;, but this time not ideologically driven, but more like the internal fights that the Technocracy had during the times of the Empires - this is their &quot;Second Technocratic Civil War&quot;, but based solely on a desire for power within national borders.<br /> <br /> Unlike their first &quot;Technocratic Civil War&quot;, which was based on ideological disputes between the Reactionary and Liberal wings of the Technocracy, this new, Second Technocratic Civil War, is a war between BOTH reactionary Technocrats fighting for power and the expansion of their respective Empires (not unlike when they would sponsor their European Empires against one another).<br /> <br /> Man... I gotta say, I'm liking this scenario more and more over the official one... These Technocrats are absolutely cursed, now we actually have a Technocracy that is a blight upon the world - as it should be! And at the end, despite all their talk of &quot;protect the Masses, create a better tomorrow&quot;, their legacy is endless war, misery and an incapability to compromise towards their higher (and still noble) goals. <br /> <br /> In the end, all that this Technocracy can offer is hubristic failure.<br /> <br /> It also ties up all the lose ends - because like I said, things like WW1, Imperial Wars or the Cold War don't make sense if you truly have just a single one Technocracy. So, that address it all","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T13:30:53-07:00","id":1539658,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539647#post1539647\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm aware that the term &quot;Fetishism&quot; was used almost as saying that they practice &quot;ooga booga&quot;. The problem however is that there is no other word for it, unfortunately your politically correct progressive anthropologists decided to just discard it altogether because it doesn't &quot;feel well&quot;.<br /> <br /> I don't care enough about Newspeak language that doesn't ruffle any feathers thou. I'm not so fragile to be at pain due to words. And again, since there's no other word for it, that's the one I have to use.<br /> <br /> Also, the point is not to have some highly specific definition, but rather a broad concept. It's like in biological taxonomy. To say that a fish and a cow are both vertebrates, say very little about them, except that they have one trait, one common theme that binds them in a group more similar than a fish and a worm.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are no commonalities that unite all non-Abrahamic religions in sub-Saharan Africa except &quot;they were African.&quot; This is the reason the &quot;politically correct progressive anthropologists&quot; discarded it--it is useless and explains nothing. I would compare it to the taxon Bestiae, but at least Linneaus referred to characteristics that actually existed--the cultures you're terming &quot;fetishist&quot; actually worshiped multiple gods, for one thing. The term &quot;traditional African religions&quot; is sometimes used for calculating demographic data, but is implicitly an admission that the only thing those religions share is being from Africa.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539647#post1539647\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, Totemist? Yeah, Totemist. All I heard was &quot;bla bla bla Totemist, bla bla bla unique, bla bla bla Totemist&quot;. So yeah, Totemist.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, because the animal isn't an emblem, there is no claim of descent or kinship to them, and they're linked to a single person rather than a group of people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539647#post1539647\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The other thing you're forgetting is that the Traditions - or Disparate Alliance, since it seems we are leading towards having it come before the Traditions - is formed by the Order of Hermes that was left out of the Order of Reason Party.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, I'm not--because the Order of Hermes wouldn't form them. The Disparate Alliance would predate the Order of Reason; the events that trigger the Disparate Alliance's formation are brought about by colonial efforts being backed by the Curia. It's their activities in Mesoamerica, the Caribbean, northern Europe, and (possibly) the Baltic region that forces people from a variety of societies together in order to survive. The most likely candidates to form the Disparate Alliance are all in Mesoamerica, and while the Mesoamericans could be pretty ethnocentric I don't see them dictating terms like the Hermetics did... partly because they aren't in the position to, partly because IIRC the Hermetics did that because of their obsession with having nine seats on the Council, one for each sphere, and the Mesoamericans won't give a shit.<br /> <br /> The Order of Hermes only starts going against the Curia/Technocracy much later, after Renaissance magic has peaked and declined (yes, this means that the Traditions are only a few centuries old), and they probably don't join the Disparate Alliance. Instead, the Order of Hermes goes underground, the Order of Reason purges more of its membership but fails to kill them all off (because they do that all the time), and the Hermetics join with these to form the Traditions.<br /> <br /> This affects group makeup. The Dreamspeakers probably <i>don't exist</i> as a Tradition. The Wu Lung probably are one--they've got overlap, the Hermetics would be interested in them (yes, even if they're also dicks; this was true IRL of occultists), and as we exit the early modern period they've probably started to spot potential trouble coming from the Technocracy/Curia.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539647#post1539647\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's different if there's another actor holding the role of antagonistic towards the Syndicate. The &quot;Central Planners&quot;.. Hmmm... How about &quot;Utopians&quot;? A new Convention born out initially as just a vague romantic ideology at the 19th century and formalized as an entity in the Second World after the Russian Revolution. The Convention of the Utopians strive for totalitarian control and ultimate social engineering of the human nature &quot;for the greater good&quot;, and their entire thing revolves around political power. Those would be the &quot;New Masons&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not going to be a romantic ideology, it's going to be an enlightenment one. If you look at the more unpleasant, more totalitarian utopias, they're rationalist enlightenment projects rather than romantic ones.<br /> <br /> I also don't see why you need to have another convention when you can just have the NWO split. They're not going to be a truly separate thing; it's just that the NWO has pre-existing tensions over whether to embrace Marxist-Leninism or capitalism and after the Allies intervene in the Russian Civil War it splits down the middle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-01T13:33:18-07:00","id":1539660,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539654#post1539654\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It also ties up all the lose ends - because like I said, things like WW1, Imperial Wars or the Cold War don't make sense if you truly have just a single one Technocracy. So, that address it all</div> </div> </div> </div>No, WWI does make sense if you have one Technocracy. Futurism held that war and violence were renewing forces; their manifesto had lines like this: &quot;We will glorify war\u2014the world's only hygiene\u2014militarism, patriotism, the destructive gesture of freedom-bringers, beautiful ideas worth dying for, and scorn for woman.&quot;<br /> <br /> Just say that they had enough influence in the Technocracy to ensure WWI happened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-02T10:02:45-07:00","id":1539709,"title":"","content":"Getting back to the 1400s, what would the world actually look like? Before we look at how everyone is acting, we should have an idea of why they are reacting. <br /> <br /> The world should be somewhat different from the history books with a lot more magic, mysticism, and other things that are not common in the present age. I've already put forward the idea that much of the European and Middle Eastern countryside is dealing with a chaotic mix of creatures and folklores of the cultures that Rome dragged across the continent, as well as those brought in by the various invasions in prior centuries. What is going on in Asia at this time? Or America? Or Africa?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-02T11:12:42-07:00","id":1539713,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539709#post1539709\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Getting back to the 1400s, what would the world actually look like? Before we look at how everyone is acting, we should have an idea of why they are reacting.<br /> <br /> The world should be somewhat different from the history books with a lot more magic, mysticism, and other things that are not common in the present age. I've already put forward the idea that much of the European and Middle Eastern countryside is dealing with a chaotic mix of creatures and folklores of the cultures that Rome dragged across the continent, as well as those brought in by the various invasions in prior centuries. What is going on in Asia at this time? Or America? Or Africa?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>To Europeans the world would look scary. The Ottoman State looked to be ready to conquer Europe and enslave the entire population. The Barbary Corsairs (called pirates by the Europeans) were conducting slave raids as far north as the British Isles (by the 17th century the Barbary Corsairs had raided Ireland and Iceland and had planned attacks on the Caribbean, North America, and Norway). Before 1650 the numbers of Europeans taken by the Barbary Corsairs in slave raids would have outnumbered African slaves purchased as slaves (the African kingdoms never allowed Europeans to raid their lands for slaves, they sold prisoners of war to Europeans) by at least three to one.<br /> <br /> Europe looked fragile, weak, and doomed. The Order of Hermes would have been aggressively guarding Europeans against threats that would seem obvious to them and obscure to us.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-02T11:32:09-07:00","id":1539715,"title":"","content":"I think in East Asia, the supernatural nations are heavily intertwined with the Ming Dynasty to a degree that they aren't other places. The Beast Courts, the Quincunx, Wu Lung, and the Celestial Bureaucracy (I prefer <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/changeling-the-dreaming-in-the-realm-of-gods-and-dreams-live-drafting-a-hsien-reboot.911145/\" target=\"_blank\">In the Realm of Gods and Dream's</a> take on the hsein/changelings) are, if not quite arms of the Ming state, so linked to it as to not be really separable. Co-existence is very fragile, but there's not open warfare and some of the supernatural nations have polite formal relations with each other (the Beast Courts and Celestial Bureaucracy, for instance, or the Celestial Bureaucracy and the Wu Lung). The acquiescence of supernaturals to the status quo is bought by doling out the fruits of the Ming's imperial largesse and establishing favorable laws (bans on tiger hunting in certain areas, for instance).<br /> <br /> But I also don't see this as stable in the long-term. There's clashing ideologies and material interests--mages and Fera compete over caerns/nodes, Fera occasionally purge a hungry dead the Quincunx feel could be dealt with other ways, mages and members of the Celestial Bureaucracy dispute over ownership of certain artifacts. The wheels are coming off of the Ming Dynasty, the Ming <i>know it</i>. That's why they start the treasure voyages--in addition to the reasons they had IRL, it's an attempt to show the various supernatural nations that the Ming are still worthy of backing. Valuable books and artifacts are brought back to the Wu Lung, the Beast Courts bring additional caerns under their influence and destroy nests of Wyrm creatures, the Celestial Bureaucracy sends off some of their troublemakers, et cetera.<br /> <br /> China was also invading Southeast Asia and facing trouble in its north at this time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-02T11:47:32-07:00","id":1539717,"title":"","content":"This period is when Majapahit was at its height and an important empire in Indonesia. Look at YouTube for Extra History Majapahit. That will give you an overview of the history and a hint or two about what an Indonesian Mage tradition might value. Superficial, but far better than nothing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-02T13:08:04-07:00","id":1539718,"title":"","content":"As far as Mesoamerica, the 1400s were when the Triple Alliance was rising, the Pur\u00e9pecha Empire was occasionally warring with them, and the League of Mayapan collapsed partway through. I would suggest having Mesoamerica be home to a variety of sophisticated, wealthy states--European visitors to the Triple Alliance were amazed by the state of their cities, after all; they were well-maintained, clean, and expansive. Materially, these empires' inhabitants are well-off and enjoy the fruits of widely available ritual magic. But like all empires, the successes of these empires are built on blood. Most bloodletting isn't fatal, but some is. Some die so that others may prosper. Sometimes it's new subject peoples, sometimes it's the underclasses, sometimes it's political dissidents. Mesoamerica's empires are just more literal than most.<br /> <br /> Most of Mesoamerica's mages are either folk magicians without broader organization or attached to temples. There is one exception, though, and that's the Fifth Sun Society. Dating back to the 14th century, it crosses cultural and political boundaries (though their membership is mostly Nahuatl) and is dedicated towards ensuring that the Fifth Sun never ends and that, if it does, something survives. In addition to fighting Nephandi and various other horrors, the Fifth Sun Society has a variety of Umbral realm boltholes, stockpiles quintessence and magical artifacts, and maintains wards over some of Mesoamerica's cities.<br /> <br /> Eventually, they'll be one of the seeds of the Disparate Alliance--they were formed to fight a very different threat, but they're already primed to form alliances and they've been prepping for the apocalypse for a while.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-02T14:14:25-07:00","id":1539720,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539658#post1539658\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are no commonalities that unite all non-Abrahamic religions in sub-Saharan Africa except &quot;they were African.&quot; This is the reason the &quot;politically correct progressive anthropologists&quot; discarded it--it is useless and explains nothing. I would compare it to the taxon Bestiae, but at least Linneaus referred to characteristics that actually existed--the cultures you're terming &quot;fetishist&quot; actually worshiped multiple gods, for one thing. The term &quot;traditional African religions&quot; is sometimes used for calculating demographic data, but is implicitly an admission that the only thing those religions share is being from Africa.<br /> <br /> <br /> No, because the animal isn't an emblem, there is no claim of descent or kinship to them, and they're linked to a single person rather than a group of people.<br /> <br /> <br /> No, I'm not--because the Order of Hermes wouldn't form them. The Disparate Alliance would predate the Order of Reason; the events that trigger the Disparate Alliance's formation are brought about by colonial efforts being backed by the Curia. It's their activities in Mesoamerica, the Caribbean, northern Europe, and (possibly) the Baltic region that forces people from a variety of societies together in order to survive. The most likely candidates to form the Disparate Alliance are all in Mesoamerica, and while the Mesoamericans could be pretty ethnocentric I don't see them dictating terms like the Hermetics did... partly because they aren't in the position to, partly because IIRC the Hermetics did that because of their obsession with having nine seats on the Council, one for each sphere, and the Mesoamericans won't give a shit.<br /> <br /> The Order of Hermes only starts going against the Curia/Technocracy much later, after Renaissance magic has peaked and declined (yes, this means that the Traditions are only a few centuries old), and they probably don't join the Disparate Alliance. Instead, the Order of Hermes goes underground, the Order of Reason purges more of its membership but fails to kill them all off (because they do that all the time), and the Hermetics join with these to form the Traditions.<br /> <br /> This affects group makeup. The Dreamspeakers probably <i>don't exist</i> as a Tradition. The Wu Lung probably are one--they've got overlap, the Hermetics would be interested in them (yes, even if they're also dicks; this was true IRL of occultists), and as we exit the early modern period they've probably started to spot potential trouble coming from the Technocracy/Curia.<br /> <br /> <br /> It's not going to be a romantic ideology, it's going to be an enlightenment one. If you look at the more unpleasant, more totalitarian utopias, they're rationalist enlightenment projects rather than romantic ones.<br /> <br /> I also don't see why you need to have another convention when you can just have the NWO split. They're not going to be a truly separate thing; it's just that the NWO has pre-existing tensions over whether to embrace Marxist-Leninism or capitalism and after the Allies intervene in the Russian Civil War it splits down the middle.</div> </div> </div> </div>I actually liked the idea of having the Disparate Alliance being created in the Mesoamerica. Perhaps an alliance of several different priests after the Aztecs and Inca fall. Then it begins to spread throughout the world.<br /> <br /> The Mesoamerican Mages would have to adapt to NOT perform human sacrifices however...<br /> <br /> Anyway, their Traditions however would soon die out, so perhaps some sort of syncretism for the future? Like Voodoo or Santeria and Umbanda here in Brazil? Sure, those are not native religions, those are African, but the native cultures will be basically dead not long after","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-02T15:35:52-07:00","id":1539723,"title":"","content":"Human sacrifice, I envision, will gradually decrease over time. It might not disappear entirely--I'm pretty sure the Verbena still do it, and from what I understand of Mexica metaphysics sacrifice is supposed to work by turning one sort of energy into another, so if you've got someone who's tainted up the wazoo a sacrificial ritual could get you quintessence from their death while purifying it. But its use would be limited to Nephandi and similarly tainted people. Blood sacrifice is probably still common, possibly done <i>en masse</i> to get as much power as possible while minimizing the cost.<br /> <br /> Syncretism will probably happen (it did IRL, after all), but one of the reason I mentioned Umbral boltholes and warded cities is that I see them trying to prevent the loss of native culture in remote communities the Spanish can't reach. Some of these places would have sizable Sleeper populations. So it won't be universal, and eventually they will undoubtedly back some of the revivalist movements you see in the region today.<br /> <br /> I agree that people from the Inca Empire are also likely early members. If it's a mythic history, rather than priests, though, why not road-builders? The Inca are justly famous for their road network, and having the mages responsible for supporting the empire's transportation, logistics, and communications infrastructure have the most contact with Mesoamerica makes sense. Once they join, their talents could help the Disparate Alliance rapidly expand.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T15:54:33-07:00","id":1539804,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539723#post1539723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree that people from the Inca Empire are also likely early members. If it's a mythic history, rather than priests, though, why not road-builders? The Inca are justly famous for their road network, and having the mages responsible for supporting the empire's transportation, logistics, and communications infrastructure have the most contact with Mesoamerica makes sense. Once they join, their talents could help the Disparate Alliance rapidly expand.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, how bout make &quot;El Dorado&quot; to be a &quot;real&quot; place?<br /> <br /> These Incan Mages could be than just &quot;simple&quot; road makers, but actually be well versed in Correspondence magic, and &quot;El Dorado&quot; could be their magic &quot;Shangri-La&quot; that the Order of Reason's Conquistadors failed to uncover... Therefore, there could still be Incan Road Makers in there, hidden<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bMaybe they even turned &quot;El Dorado&quot; into the Horizon stronghold of the Disparate Alliance, that could be still using even thou the &quot;Road Makers&quot; have disappeared.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T16:02:11-07:00","id":1539805,"title":"","content":"Of all the South American Natives, probably the ones with the biggest expression in the future are the Tupi-Guarani people. In Paraguay, half the population still speaks it in addition to Spanish. They are also still present in Brazil. They have a polytheistic and animistic religion, and there are groups with their intact culture.<br /> <br /> Other groups are too small to really count.<br /> <br /> In Chile and Peru, the Incan traditions still exist, but it is dwindling, some elders still speak the old language, but that is increasingly rare amongst the younger generations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T19:17:19-07:00","id":1539815,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539713#post1539713\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To Europeans the world would look scary. The Ottoman State looked to be ready to conquer Europe and enslave the entire population. The Barbary Corsairs (called pirates by the Europeans) were conducting slave raids as far north as the British Isles (by the 17th century the Barbary Corsairs had raided Ireland and Iceland and had planned attacks on the Caribbean, North America, and Norway). Before 1650 the numbers of Europeans taken by the Barbary Corsairs in slave raids would have outnumbered African slaves purchased as slaves (the African kingdoms never allowed Europeans to raid their lands for slaves, they sold prisoners of war to Europeans) by at least three to one.<br /> <br /> Europe looked fragile, weak, and doomed. The Order of Hermes would have been aggressively guarding Europeans against threats that would seem obvious to them and obscure to us.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. The 14th century is an inflexion time for Europe. It is, at one hand, a moment of extreme fragility for all of the Christendom and also the point from which they finally emerge out of the &quot;Dark Ages&quot; from the fall of the Roman Empire.<br /> <br /> At one hand, the Germanic Pagans were finally almost all converted to Christianity, but there were still episodes of violence; the principality of Muscovy finally rid itself from the Golden Horde, although remnants of it remained as the Golden Horde, which were diminishing in time until the last few Khanates were conquered by the Russians in the 18th century, the Reconquista at the Iberian Peninsula is reaching it's final moments but the Ottomans begun their conquest of the Balkans, finally ending the last remnants of the Byzantine Empire.<br /> <br /> To the Europeans at the time, they feel their world at the brink, and their entire civilization at the edge of the Abyss. The century begins with a great famine that kills millions, and then by the middle of the century, the Black Death kills ONE THIRD of the people in Europe.<br /> <br /> Imagine that, we all just went nuts with COVID with a lethality of less than 0.1%, just imagine a disease that wiped out 1 out of every 3 people.<br /> <br /> For the Iberians, they live in a state of constant Crusade, against an enemy that wages endless Jihad. But for the first time in 700 years, they are beginning to fight back - and winning.<br /> <br /> At the Balkans however, the situation is much more dire. The Pope in Rome already fears that the Ottomans are an unbeatable foe that will conquer the world. Even thou the Christendom is facing some degree of success in Iberia, in the Balkans things look dire.<br /> <br /> And to make matters worse, England and France, two of the most powerful nations of all of the Catholic world, get themselves involved at the 100 years war.<br /> <br /> A few dates worth mentioning:<br /> <br /> \u2022 1305\u20131314: The Trials of the Knights Templar. The Knights Templar arrested and tried. Jacques de Molay, the last grand master of the Templars, is executed in 1314.<br /> \u2022 1327: Tver Uprising against the Golden Horde.<br /> \u2022 1337: The Hundred Years' War begins when Edward III of England lays claim to the French throne.<br /> \u2022 1343\u20131345: In Saint George's Night Uprising, pagan Estonians launch a last large-scale attempt<br /> \u2022 1346: King Valdemar IV of Denmark sells the Duchy of Estonia to the Teutonic Order.to rid themselves of the non-indigenous Christian religion.<br /> \u2022 1347\u20131351: The Black Death kills around a third of the population of Europe.<br /> \u2022 1348: The 6.9-magnitude 1348 Friuli earthquake centered in Northern Italy was felt across Europe. Contemporaries linked the quake with the Black Death and Great Famine, fueling fears that the Biblical Apocalypse had arrived.<br /> \u2022 1371: The Battle of Maritsa, the Serbs are defeated by the Ottomans, with most of Serb nobility being killed.<br /> \u2022 1378: Battle of the Vozha River between Russians and Mongols.<br /> \u2022 1380: Russian principalities defeat the Golden Horde at the Battle of Kulikovo.<br /> \u2022 1382: Khan Tokhtamysh captures Moscow.<br /> \u2022 1389: Battle of Kosovo between Serbs and Ottoman Turks<br /> \u2022 1391: Anti-Jewish pogroms spread throughout Spain and Portugal, and many thousands of Jews are massacred<br /> \u2022 1396: The Battle of Nicopolis, in which the Ottoman Empire defeats a large Crusader army of knights and infantry from various Christian kingdoms including Hungary, France, the Holy Roman Empire, Burgundy and Wallachia.<br /> <br /> So, the Curia and &quot;The Patriarchs&quot; (yes, I am creating a new group of Mages for the Orthodox church), although having their share of bad blood, also have some common ground, mainly in their (rightfully so) perception that they are under siege and surrounded by enemies on all sides. From the perspective of the Catholics and the Curia, the Orthodoxes are a shield against the Mongol Hordes and against the Ottomans, while from the point of view of the Orthodoxes, the Catholics are a shield against also the Ottomans and the Sarracens and Mamelukes of North Africa.<br /> <br /> Looking from the European point of view, once upon a time there was Rome, that conquered... Everything.<br /> <br /> Then, Rome fell.<br /> <br /> Except, that it didn't.<br /> <br /> Although we today claim that the &quot;official&quot; date that Rome ended was 476, that's... Not entirely correct. It just ceased to be an Empire and lost all its power, but it remained as an independent political entity, now under the rule of the Papacy, and their old Imperial powers remained a bit in the form of said Papacy. The former &quot;Roman Empire&quot; was no longer able to fully enforce its will and dominance over the European now feuds, but they remained some of it in the form of the authority of the Church; therefore, what we have is something a little more like &quot;semi-independence&quot; with various degrees of influence from it. Each feud became it's own autonomous political entity but still under a degree of influence from the Vatican at Rome, which acted as the medieval &quot;UN&quot; of the time.<br /> <br /> Besides that, the thing that we call the &quot;Byzantine&quot; Empire is largely a modern fabrication. They called themselves as ROMANS, and yes, there were TWO &quot;Roman Empires&quot; for a large amount of time.<br /> <br /> The entire Mediterranean was &quot;Mare Nostrum&quot;, even after the Western Roman Empire &quot;fell&quot;, which means that everything in the Middle East and North Africa was CHRISTIAN lands. Egypt was the seat of the Coptic Orthodox church and the entirety of Anatolia and the area of the Levante and Syria were all Greek Orthodox.<br /> <br /> Worse of all, the &quot;Moors&quot; entered the Iberic Peninsula and got as far as France! And they came so, so close to swallowing Europe whole.<br /> <br /> Then came Islam and expelled Christianism from the Middle East and North Africa, and the Patriarchy of Ethiopia got isolated from the rest of Christendom.<br /> <br /> Then, as Christendom began to spread beyond the original borders of the Roman Empire in the north, converting pagans in Germany and England, came the pillaging of Viking pirates from the Norse, slaughtering entire monasteries in Northern Europe.<br /> <br /> Then came the Mongols from the steps, slaughtering millions.<br /> <br /> And finally, from Anatolia comes the Ottomans, who just conquered everybody left and right.<br /> <br /> By the 14th century, the Norse are &quot;tamed&quot; - the pagans are one of the many threats that Christendom has managed to survive to - but that seems the least of their worries, given how absolutely overwhelmed by the Mongols and Ottomans and Sarracens they believe themselves to be.<br /> <br /> And to make it all worse, the Black Death absolutely destroys Europe.<br /> <br /> It really seems as the Judgment Day has been set upon Europe.<br /> <br /> For all of these reasons, I would say that Portugal would be the best place for the birth of the &quot;Proto Order of Reason&quot;. It is the first Kingdom to stablish Absolute Monarchy, moving away entirely from feudalism, it had unified their nation and won their own Reconquista war against the Caliphate of Cordoba, while Castille was still engaged in the final steps of it. For those reasons, Portugal launched the Great Navigations under the rule of the King Henrique the Navigator (you probably know him by the barbaric Germanic language of &quot;English&quot; as Henry the Navigator).<br /> <br /> And the reason is simple: Portugal was surrounded on on side by the Atlantic Ocean, and on the other, by the Realm of Castille and Leon (who would unite, and then also unite with Aragon and form Spain).<br /> <br /> So, Portugal had nowhere to expand but towards a bigger and more powerful realm, and to make matters worse, just because they had won their Reconquista war, it didn't mean they were safe from the Islamic Caliphs from North Africa.<br /> <br /> Thus they had the idea to launch themselves into the sea.<br /> <br /> This later on would devolve into the massive endeavor of finding the way to the Indies by going over Africa, after 1453 when the Ottomans take Constantinople and cut the trade with India to Europe, in order to wage economic war against Christendom, which will culminate with the expedition of Vasco da Gama from Portugal that finally maps the way around Africa and into India, more or less at the same time that Colombo discover the Americas. <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> Thus, the 14th century looks like the end of the world for Europeans, but Europe will emerge far more powerful in the 15th century, and by the 16th century they'll begin to slowly outpass the Muslims as they begin to conquer literally all of the world.<br /> <br /> And the idea was exactly that: the reason why the Spaniards were so aggressive in their colonization is because Spain was the frontier territory between Christendom and the Muslim world, and they had just waged 9 centuries of endless Crusade against 9 centuries of endless Islamic Jihad. To the Spaniards, colonization was not just about new lands and new riches, but it was about surviving in a civilizational conflict of extermination against a relentless enemy that they have been fighting for millenia. Therefore, the project was to turn all of the world Christian, and in so doing, suffocating the Muslims in isolation in their area, and thus managing to survive the onslaught.<br /> <br /> But that's still almost 2 centuries away. At the 14th century, the Curia and the Patriarchs have their hands full trying to deal with internal problems and rivalries between the many feuds in Europe, and the Islamic and Mongolic menaces that threatens to absolutely end their worlds.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, the Order of Hermes is busy in Italy &quot;rediscovering&quot; stuff from the Roman Empire, while Templars are hiding everywhere, but in Portugal they find sanctuary in secrecy and begin to usher in a &quot;new age of reason&quot; for the continent.<br /> <br /> I also propose that the Order of Reason take many similarities with the Curia early on, being more like a loose alliance than a monolithic pyramid, which is why there probably is a &quot;Vatican&quot; for the Order of Reason, but they organize themselves around a crown, thus around &quot;national&quot; political interests, which explains why OoR and Technocrats are constantly fighting one another. Now, I just wonder what would the &quot;Vatican&quot; for the Order of Reason be?<br /> <br /> In the 19th century it is London obviously, then after WW2 it is New York, but what about in the 16th century when they are officially funded, what would that be?<br /> <br /> Hmmm... I guess it should be in Madri, which becomes the center of gravity in Europe at the time under the rules of Catarina de Castille and Frederico de Aragon and then their grandson Carlos Magno, King of Spain and the Emperor of the Holy Roman Empire (and probably the only guy as powerful as the Ottoman Sultan), much to the dismay of the Portuguese Order of Reason that began the whole thing.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T20:18:03-07:00","id":1539818,"title":"","content":"Outside of Europe, besides the Ottomans, the Timurid empire formed, a miscellaneous Islamic empire with Turkic, Mongolic and Persian cultures, forming yet another vast Islamic empire. The Islamic conquest of India proceeds and it is absolutely brutal, 300 million Hindus will perish at the hands of Muslims over a period of merely 3 centuries. In China, the Mongolic rule ends and begins the Ming dynasty.<br /> <br /> In Africa, the kingdom of Mali reaches its peak under the rule of Mansa Musa who was, probably, the wealthiest person ever since ever. When you think of wealth, do not think about Elon Musk or Jeff Bezos, not even think about Crassius of the first Roman triumvirate, but rather think about Mansa Musa. This guy was so wealthy that legend says that he broke the economy of a town due to endless inflation when he decide to just simply distribute gold to everybody. What to him was just pennies, inflated the local economy into oblivion.<br /> <br /> Yes, West Africa was rich and powerful, the Islamic Caliphs and Ottoman Sultan were rich and powerful, India was rich as hell and the Ming Dynasty was as powerful as the old Roman Empire. Europe meanwhile was broke and desperate.<br /> <br /> AND in the Americas, the Mexica founded the city of Tenochtitlan, while the Mississippian mound city of Cahokia was abandoned.<br /> <br /> That was also the time when the Incan Empire was formed, and it was MASSIVE. Therefore, there were many great things happening in the Americas; I particularly liked the idea of the &quot;Road Makers&quot; craft for the Incas, because their Empire is pretty unique in human history, formed without use of the wheel (they knew it, but it was almost useless in the mountains) no draft animals except for llamas or even a written system - it is weird yeah, instead they recorded information using 'knots'. Yeah, it is absolutely fascinating. Yes, they used knots from fibers to record information, not written words. And they also didn't have iron. AND they build a massively extensive Empire on top of mountains. So the &quot;Road Makers&quot; are absolutely central for their Empire, and their Magik prominence would be absolutely linked to the administration of the Empire, very similarly to how the Wu Lung would be closely linked to the Emperor of China. Likewise, the Aztecs Priests-Mages would also be closely linked to the Emperor and Nobility on Tenochtitlan.<br /> <br /> There are many great things that can be arranged for the &quot;Illuminati&quot; scenery in the Americas of the time, as a prelude to the coming War of Ascension, and yes, I am absolutely of the mind that the Disparate Alliance should be kick started by an alliance of Aztecs, Mayan and Incan Mages, after the Spanish conquistadors of the Order of Reason have their way with them.<br /> <br /> Those are two great and advanced civilizations, at the same level of sophistication as the Europeans, only lacking in terms of technology. They were not just groups of tribals. And even the Spaniards knew this. Hernan Cortez for example never wanted to destroy the Aztecs, he wanted to breed a new Empire out of them... Well, he wanted them to be Catholics and &quot;westernized&quot;, which would never work, but he understood how powerful they were and... He wanted to be their Emperor. Yeah. The guy was ambitious. And, if he could have his way, eventually he would be using this new Aztec Empire with European technology to conquer the world.<br /> <br /> But he failed so, who cares.<br /> <br /> That however is a great plot for Cortez to be a secret Order of Reason agent with this crazy dream to turn the Aztecs into the seat of their own little personal empire (and use it to wage war against both the Curia and the Janissaries in the future).<br /> <br /> Then in South America it was Pizarro that conquered the Incas, but he wasn't as insane as Cortez. But again, that's another guy that has &quot;Order of Reason&quot; written all over.<br /> <br /> It is also important to note that the Curia and Order of Reason won't actually begin to collide violently against one another until probably the 18th century. Up until there, they seemingly collaborate... Although it is likely that the OoR was probably just pretending to be allies with the Curia, binding their time to jump on them.<br /> <br /> So yeah, the 14 century was a pivotal century all over the world. And in the Americas, more so than ever.<br /> <br /> In North America there was almost yet another great Empire like the Incas, Aztecs and Maias, but it was abandoned at that same period that the Aztecs and Incas were rising. What caused the fall of the city of Cahokia? That's fuel for the secret &quot;world of darkness&quot;. What kind of demonically or magic cataclysm could have caused this? And what kinds of magical reverberations could still exist at the Mississippi due to it? Maybe something Nefandic is at the bottom of the good old Mississippi, hidden and lurking?<br /> <br /> Then onto the Mayans, they were a huge collection of city states, much like the ancient Greeks, with some rising and falling constantly. The first Mayan cities date from 750BC and by 500BC were already monumental. That's yet another civilization that was as sophisticated as the ancient Greeks. The last Mayan city was only conquered in the 17th century. At the 9th century AD there was a huge collapse similar to the Bronze Age collapse in the Mediterranean that saw the end of the Micenean civilization - the civilization that came before the Greeks in that part, and from which the God Dyonisus came from (remember, Dyonisus is the origin of EVERYTHING, since it is from the cult of this Micenic God, which was NOT a Greek God, that came the Orphic mystery cults, that would culminate into the 1st century Gnosticism, that would form the Order of Hermes, then the Masons, and then the Order of Reason. Yeah, blame the crazy God of wine and orgies for all of that).<br /> <br /> Therefore, the Disparate Alliance forms in the 16th century as an alliance of Aztecs, Mayans and Incan Mages, who quickly learn about the Conquistadors and travel the world in search of allies.<br /> <br /> How does then the Traditions form, or why don't the Traditions just grow out of the Disparate Alliance?<br /> <br /> I think that, given the Hermetic Order love towards exotic mysticism, they would have loved to study Amerindian Magic, and by doing so, a contact between then could be formed, and from that, cooperation, that could see the Alliance evolve into what then would become the Traditions","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T20:31:28-07:00","id":1539820,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Certainly! Here are some suggestions for names of mage groups inspired by the Aztec, Maya, and Incan civilizations, with a focus on their cultural and historical elements:<br /> <br /> ### Aztec-Inspired Mage Groups:<br /> 1. **Order of the Sun\u2019s Jaguar**: Reflects the importance of the sun and jaguar in Aztec culture.<br /> 2. **Circle of the Feathered Serpent**: Inspired by Quetzalcoatl, the feathered serpent deity.<br /> 3. **Guardians of the Sacred Tempest**: Combines the Aztec\u2019s reverence for storms and sacred forces.<br /> 4. **Keepers of the Stone and Blood**: Echoes the Aztec practices of stonework and ritual sacrifice.<br /> <br /> ### Maya-Inspired Mage Groups:<br /> 1. **Lodges of the Starborn Scribes**: Refers to the Maya\u2019s advanced knowledge of astronomy and their scribes.<br /> 2. **Sects of the Celestial Jaguar**: Combines Maya celestial symbolism with jaguar imagery.<br /> 3. **Brotherhood of the Hidden Glyphs**: Reflects the Maya's use of complex glyphic writing.<br /> 4. **Order of the Sacred Calendar**: Alludes to the Maya\u2019s intricate calendrical systems.<br /> <br /> ### Incan-Inspired Mage Groups:<br /> 1. **Clan of the Inca\u2019s Light**: Reflects the divine status of the Inca rulers.<br /> 2. **Keepers of the Sun\u2019s Radiance**: Echoes the Incan worship of Inti, the sun god.<br /> 3. **Order of the Andean Serpent**: Inspired by the Andean serpent symbolism and geography.<br /> 4. **Coven of the Sacred Mountain**: Alludes to the significance of mountains in Incan spirituality, like Machu Picchu.<br /> <br /> These names aim to honor and reflect the historical and cultural significance of each civilization while integrating them into the magical framework of &quot;Mage: The Ascension.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I just couldn't resist...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T20:56:00-07:00","id":1539821,"title":"","content":"The Incan road builders (we can call them Kamayuq Nan, which, if I didn't mangle the translation, loosely translates to the roads office) are definitely well-versed in Correspondence magic, yeah. I imagine that their roads don't just run through normal space, and they may be impossible to find unless you're &quot;supposed&quot; to find them, or ones that can take you more than one place. They would certainly be involved in making any Disparate equivalent to Horizon, if only because it's their job to make the paths that would lead to it.<br /> <br /> The Guarani do sound like interesting candidates for having a magical society present among the Disparates, as does the Musica Confederation, but I don't have any firm ideas on what their magical societies might look like.<br /> <br /> Also, if we're having them construct an equivalent of Horizon, we could help figure out the earliest factions by figuring out who they'd need to build it and what each faction's specialty would be. So far we have:<br /> 1. The <b>Fifth Sun Society</b>, who would be the people actually creating the Umbral space as well as warding it.<br /> 2. <b>Kamayug Nan</b> is responsible for maintaining the roads that travel to it.<br /> <br /> We need someone to create the ecology of the place and handle food production, since it's going to be big enough that they need it. The <b>Verbena</b> are likely choices if the Disparates don't recruit someone more local (I bet we could think of a faction from the Amazon that would work, given the existence of terra preta and the agroforestry there). You also want:<br /> 1. Architects and urban planners. Kamayug Nan can handle some of that, but they're more specialized in transport.<br /> 2. Military forces. Obviously the military will be contributed to by all the factions, since you don't want one group to be in charge of the military, but of the three only the Fifth Sun Society are really fighters and they're more akin to preppers than an organized military force.<br /> 3. Medicine (if the Verbena are recruits, they can handle this too).<br /> 4. Artisans--and eventually some sort of light industry, but that will come later.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T21:30:03-07:00","id":1539823,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539820#post1539820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, I just couldn't resist...</div> </div> </div> </div>Heh. Do it again, but for Africa\u2026","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T21:35:25-07:00","id":1539824,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539611#post1539611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm sorry but, there won't be 5000+ micro groups made of a single Mage (if that much) that are completely irrelevant to the game. There's a reason why &quot;Dreamspeakers&quot; is a thing. You can customize your Mage if you will, but no, there won't be a single unique group for each individual human tribe in History.<br /> <br /> Let's also remind that the number of Mages is miniscule. Official source is &quot;1 Awaken per 1.000.000 sleepers&quot;, which in the 1400's would mean a mere 500 Mages globally. Granted, I firmly believe this number should be summarily ignored, otherwise we wouldn't have an &quot;Ascension War&quot;, but rather a few sages hidden in mountains in half a dozen of sacred grounds, but still, that number can't be so high to the point that we get 5M Mages (1% of the population), which would mean that every single noble and his dog are Awaken.<br /> <br /> This means that many groups actually won't have a single Mage to count within their ranks, so it's not too crazy to think that not all cultures are or even SHOULD be represented. And again, nobody would take it seriously to have &quot;2000 Traditions&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, Animists, Totemists, Fetishists, Politheists. That division sticks, those are obviously umbrella terms for things that share SOME similarities, even if not perfect and if doesn't account for every bit of idiosyncrasies within them, but it is already more than just &quot;Dreamspeakers&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sleepwalkers are far more common than Awakened mages, though, and they too can contribute to the paradigm clashes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T21:44:29-07:00","id":1539825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539821#post1539821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Incan road builders (we can call them Kamayuq Nan, which, if I didn't mangle the translation, loosely translates to the roads office) are definitely well-versed in Correspondence magic, yeah. I imagine that their roads don't just run through normal space, and they may be impossible to find unless you're &quot;supposed&quot; to find them, or ones that can take you more than one place. They would certainly be involved in making any Disparate equivalent to Horizon, if only because it's their job to make the paths that would lead to it.<br /> <br /> The Guarani do sound like interesting candidates for having a magical society present among the Disparates, as does the Musica Confederation, but I don't have any firm ideas on what their magical societies might look like.<br /> <br /> Also, if we're having them construct an equivalent of Horizon, we could help figure out the earliest factions by figuring out who they'd need to build it and what each faction's specialty would be. So far we have:<br /> 1. The <b>Fifth Sun Society</b>, who would be the people actually creating the Umbral space as well as warding it.<br /> 2. <b>Kamayug Nan</b> is responsible for maintaining the roads that travel to it.<br /> <br /> We need someone to create the ecology of the place and handle food production, since it's going to be big enough that they need it. The <b>Verbena</b> are likely choices if the Disparates don't recruit someone more local (I bet we could think of a faction from the Amazon that would work, given the existence of terra preta and the agroforestry there). You also want:<br /> 1. Architects and urban planners. Kamayug Nan can handle some of that, but they're more specialized in transport.<br /> 2. Military forces. Obviously the military will be contributed to by all the factions, since you don't want one group to be in charge of the military, but of the three only the Fifth Sun Society are really fighters and they're more akin to preppers than an organized military force.<br /> 3. Medicine (if the Verbena are recruits, they can handle this too).<br /> 4. Artisans--and eventually some sort of light industry, but that will come later.</div> </div> </div> </div>Including the Guarani definitely sounds interesting. You could probably give them an eschatological or life-denying focus, given the nature of Guarani religion. Having a more individualistic culture would serve as a nice foil to several of the more collectivistic cultures in the alliance. I think it would be hard to figure out exactly what their magical traditions would be like in a historic context, though. Contemporary Guarani religion is difficult to separate from the poor material conditions that many of the people currently experience, conditions which would not be applicable in the setting described.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T22:18:27-07:00","id":1539827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539821#post1539821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Incan road builders (we can call them Kamayuq Nan, which, if I didn't mangle the translation, loosely translates to the roads office) are definitely well-versed in Correspondence magic, yeah. I imagine that their roads don't just run through normal space, and they may be impossible to find unless you're &quot;supposed&quot; to find them, or ones that can take you more than one place. They would certainly be involved in making any Disparate equivalent to Horizon, if only because it's their job to make the paths that would lead to it.<br /> <br /> The Guarani do sound like interesting candidates for having a magical society present among the Disparates, as does the Musica Confederation, but I don't have any firm ideas on what their magical societies might look like.<br /> <br /> Also, if we're having them construct an equivalent of Horizon, we could help figure out the earliest factions by figuring out who they'd need to build it and what each faction's specialty would be. So far we have:<br /> 1. The <b>Fifth Sun Society</b>, who would be the people actually creating the Umbral space as well as warding it.<br /> 2. <b>Kamayug Nan</b> is responsible for maintaining the roads that travel to it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ehh... What is the Fifth Sun Society? <br /> <br /> <br /> [/Quote]We need someone to create the ecology of the place and handle food production, since it's going to be big enough that they need it. The <b>Verbena</b> are likely choices if the Disparates don't recruit someone more local (I bet we could think of a faction from the Amazon that would work, given the existence of terra preta and the agroforestry there). You also want:<br /> 1. Architects and urban planners. Kamayug Nan can handle some of that, but they're more specialized in transport.[/quote]<br /> Oh, those would definitely be the Mayans, hands down.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2. Military forces. Obviously the military will be contributed to by all the factions, since you don't want one group to be in charge of the military, but of the three only the Fifth Sun Society are really fighters and they're more akin to preppers than an organized military force. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I'm missing something here. Are those part of some official group?<br /> Anyway, about military I have only one word: JaguarWarriors (yes, that's a single word, the word is mine and I do what I want with it). Nothing is more badass in the world than that. No Samurai nor knight no ninja nor Templar or Janissary or Viking Jaguar Warriors are the most terrifying thing ever (and the prospect of being brutally sacrificed if you survive just adds to the badassery)[/quote]<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3. Medicine (if the Verbena are recruits, they can handle this too). </div> </div> </div>At this point, we need to discuss of wether or not the Verbena will even be a thing. I never quite liked them, and found it to be quite niche to dead European cultures, some of which died at the hands of the Romans nonetheless, so, so long ago. I feel that there are several other traditions more relevant than they<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. Artisans--and eventually some sort of light industry, but that will come later. </div> </div> </div>Will, initially all of these roles would have to be played by the triple alliance (Incas, Mayans, Aztecs). Then, as other groups start to join, those could be delegated or shared between more appropriate groups.<br /> <br /> Now, ironically we've delved very little into North America, and the only place where there actually happened a few alliances of natives against the European or US powers. Again, Cahokia is a mine field to explore, as is the native alliance that almost sprung an Amerindian nation. That &quot;Amerindian Federation&quot; could be another &quot;Disparate Alliance&quot; of sorts, therefore we could have the Triple Alliance from the South forming in earnest, then joining with the North American Amerindian Federation, then the rest of the world, and with the Hermetic involving, forming the Traditions","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T22:35:18-07:00","id":1539828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Certainly! Here is a comprehensive summary based on all the discussions from the specified point forward, integrating the various elements into a cohesive narrative:<br /> <br /> ### **Reimagining the Ascension War in the 1400s**<br /> <br /> #### **1. Hermetic Schools**<br /> - **Characteristics:**<br /> - Hermetic scholars in the 1400s are deeply engaged in ancient texts and alchemical practices.<br /> - They strive to merge the mystical traditions of the Roman Empire with contemporary knowledge.<br /> - The schools are fragmented, with some focusing on esoteric interpretations of classical philosophy, while others blend mysticism with early scientific inquiries.<br /> <br /> #### **2. Wildlands**<br /> - **Characteristics:**<br /> - Rural Europe is depicted as a region rich with mythologies and folklore.<br /> - The landscape is home to magical creatures and local traditions.<br /> - This environment supports diverse regional magical practices and conflicts, reflecting the varied beliefs of the time.<br /> <br /> #### **3. Religious Mysticism**<br /> - **Christian Europe:**<br /> - Mages align with Christian frameworks, integrating mystical insights with their faith.<br /> - They are often associated with secretive orders within the Church.<br /> <br /> - **Islamic Regions:**<br /> - Islamic mages might be tied to scholarly institutions or mystical brotherhoods.<br /> - They integrate mystical practices with Islamic teachings.<br /> <br /> #### **4. Other Cultures**<br /> - **Aztec and Incan Mages:**<br /> - Aztec and Incan mages possess complex magical systems that interact with European and Asian practices.<br /> - Their magical systems reflect sophisticated traditions that contrast with or complement those of other regions.<br /> <br /> - **African Mages:**<br /> - Mages from Africa are connected to indigenous spiritual traditions.<br /> - These traditions influence or conflict with other magical paradigms.<br /> <br /> #### **5. Countercultures**<br /> - **Gnosticism and Hermeticism:**<br /> - Gnosticism and Hermeticism act as subversive forces in Europe.<br /> - They challenge dominant religious and magical ideologies, rather than forming a cohesive faction.<br /> <br /> #### **6. Major Cultural and Religious Representations**<br /> - **Diverse Traditions:**<br /> - The global diversity of mystical traditions includes Zoroastrianism, Taoism, Confucianism, and various forms of Paganism.<br /> - Each group has distinct goals and methods, reflecting a complex interplay between local and global magical practices.<br /> <br /> ### **Development of Magical Factions and Dynamics**<br /> <br /> #### **1. The Curia**<br /> - **Role and Evolution:**<br /> - The Curia is an influential faction known for its historical and mystical impact.<br /> - It collaborates with the Order of Reason initially but eventually conflicts with it in the 18th century.<br /> <br /> #### **2. Magical Societies in Africa**<br /> - **Mali Empire:**<br /> - Mansa Musa\u2019s Mali Empire is known for immense wealth and influence, impacting local economies significantly.<br /> - The potential for a powerful magical society in West Africa is considered, reflecting the grandeur of the Mali Empire.<br /> <br /> - **Magical Societies:**<br /> - While specific African magical societies are not detailed, their inclusion enriches the magical landscape.<br /> <br /> #### **3. Magical Societies in Asia**<br /> - **Timurid Empire:**<br /> - The Timurid Empire, with its diverse cultural influences, contributes to the broader magical context.<br /> <br /> - **Ming Dynasty:**<br /> - The Ming Dynasty represents a major power in Asia, influencing magical practices and interactions with other factions.<br /> <br /> #### **4. The Order of Reason and Its Evolution into the Technocracy**<br /> - **Formation and Characteristics:**<br /> - The Order of Reason evolves into the Technocracy, focusing on technological control and rationality.<br /> - Agents like Hernan Cortez and Francisco Pizarro are depicted as ambitious figures seeking to harness the power of indigenous civilizations.<br /> <br /> #### **5. The Disparate Alliance and Its Formation**<br /> - **Formation in the Americas:**<br /> - The Disparate Alliance forms in the 16th century as a coalition of Aztec, Maya, and Incan mages.<br /> - It responds to the Spanish conquistadors and the Order of Reason\u2019s encroachment.<br /> <br /> - **Factions Involved:**<br /> - **Fifth Sun Society:** Creates and wards the magical equivalent of Horizon.<br /> - **Kamayuq Nan:** Maintains magical pathways to the equivalent of Horizon.<br /> - **Other Potential Factions:** The Guarani and Musica Confederation might contribute to the magical society, though specifics are less defined.<br /> <br /> #### **6. The Order of Hermes and the Masons**<br /> - **Order of Hermes:**<br /> - Known for fascination with exotic mysticism, the Order of Hermes cooperates with the Disparate Alliance.<br /> - This cooperation influences the evolution of the Disparate Alliance into the Traditions.<br /> <br /> - **Masons:**<br /> - Linked to the Order of Hermes through the development of mystical traditions.<br /> - Their historical evolution is tied to broader mystical knowledge and influence.<br /> <br /> ### **Magical Landscape Overview**<br /> - **Early Interactions and Influences:**<br /> - The Curia and Order of Reason's early cooperation evolves into later conflict.<br /> - The Order of Reason's agents are ambitious in their attempts to control and harness indigenous power.<br /> <br /> - **Formation and Evolution:**<br /> - The Disparate Alliance forms in response to external threats and evolves with the help of the Hermetic Order.<br /> - The Technocracy arises from the Order of Reason's vision of technological control.<br /> <br /> This summary integrates the elements of the magical setting discussed, highlighting key factions, cultural influences, and historical dynamics. </div> </div> </div>Well, I put everything discussed here for ChatGPT... All I want was for this thing to resume it for me...<br /> <br /> Suffice it to say, we don't need to worry about Skynet for now.<br /> <br /> But, it is something we can work with. Tomorrow I'll make our current dumb but future machine God-Overlord give something better, now I'm going to bed","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T23:09:44-07:00","id":1539830,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539823#post1539823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Heh. Do it again, but for Africa\u2026</div> </div> </div> </div>Ay ay Captain!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's a brief overview of the six key African civilizations of the 14th century, including their geographic locations and main achievements:<br /> <br /> 1. **Mali Empire**:<br /> - **Location**: West Africa, centered around the modern-day regions of Mali and parts of Mauritania, Senegal, and Niger.<br /> - **Main Achievements**: The Mali Empire was renowned for its immense wealth, particularly during the reign of Mansa Musa, whose pilgrimage to Mecca demonstrated the empire's opulence. The empire was a major center for trade, education, and culture, with Timbuktu becoming a prominent scholarly and religious hub with notable institutions like the University of Sankore.<br /> <br /> 2. **Songhai Empire**:<br /> - **Location**: West Africa, stretching across present-day Mali, Niger, and Nigeria.<br /> - **Main Achievements**: The Songhai Empire emerged as a dominant power in West Africa after the decline of Mali. It was known for its strong military, sophisticated administrative system, and flourishing trade routes. The empire\u2019s capital, Gao, and the city of Timbuktu continued to be centers of learning and commerce.<br /> <br /> 3. **Hausa City-States**:<br /> - **Location**: Northern Nigeria.<br /> - **Main Achievements**: The Hausa city-states, including Kano, Katsina, and Zaria, were known for their trade networks and Islamic scholarship. They developed a distinct culture marked by advanced urban planning, with impressive city walls and significant contributions to trade and Islamic scholarship.<br /> <br /> 4. **Great Zimbabwe**:<br /> - **Location**: Southeastern Africa, in modern-day Zimbabwe.<br /> - **Main Achievements**: Great Zimbabwe is famous for its extensive stone ruins, including the Great Enclosure and the Great Zimbabwe Ruins, which are among the most impressive ancient structures in Africa. The civilization was a major center of trade and political power, connected to regional trade networks.<br /> <br /> 5. **Swahili City-States**:<br /> - **Location**: East African coast, including parts of modern-day Kenya, Tanzania, and Mozambique.<br /> - **Main Achievements**: The Swahili city-states, such as Kilwa Kisiwani, were key players in Indian Ocean trade, linking Africa with Arabia, Persia, and India. They were known for their prosperous trade in gold, ivory, and other goods, and for their distinctive Swahili culture and architecture.<br /> <br /> 6. **Kingdom of Aksum (Axumite Empire)**:<br /> - **Location**: Northeastern Africa, in modern-day Ethiopia and parts of Eritrea.<br /> - **Main Achievements**: The Aksumite Empire was a major trading empire known for its impressive architecture, including the stelae and the Church of St. Mary of Zion. It was an early adopter of Christianity, and its wealth was derived from trade with the Roman Empire, India, and Arabia. The empire played a crucial role in the spread of Christianity in Africa.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Certainly! Here are the Magical Traditions for a game of *Mage: The Ascension* based on the six key African civilizations of the 14th century: <br /> ### 1. **Mali Empire: The Mali Mystics**<br /> <br /> - **Paradigm**: **Sacred Sovereignty** <br /> Mali Mystics believe in the divine right and spiritual responsibility of the rulers and scholars. Their magic is deeply intertwined with the sacred and regal aspects of their society, reflecting the grandeur and spiritual authority of the Mansa.<br /> <br /> - **Magical Practices**:<br /> - **Divination**: Using sacred texts and oral traditions to foresee events and make decisions, often performed by griots or trained diviners.<br /> - **Transmutation**: Rituals involving gold and other precious materials to invoke blessings and influence prosperity.<br /> - **Protective Charms**: Crafting and using talismans imbued with prayers and royal authority to protect individuals and the realm from harm.<br /> <br /> ### 2. **Songhai Empire: The Songhai Scholars**<br /> <br /> - **Paradigm**: **Esoteric Knowledge** <br /> The Songhai Scholars base their magic on the pursuit of knowledge and the power of intellectual and spiritual enlightenment. Their practices focus on integrating scholarly wisdom with mystical power.<br /> <br /> - **Magical Practices**:<br /> - **Astrology**: Using celestial observations to guide decisions and influence events, reflecting the Songhai's scholarly achievements in astronomy.<br /> - **Ritual Alchemy**: Combining ancient texts and symbols to transform substances and achieve mystical effects, mirroring their advanced understanding of trade and economics.<br /> - **Invocation of Ancestors**: Calling upon the spirits of ancestors to gain guidance and power, deeply rooted in their cultural practices.<br /> <br /> ### 3. **Hausa City-States: The Hausa Guardians**<br /> <br /> - **Paradigm**: **Urban Spiritism** <br /> The Hausa Guardians draw their magical power from the urban environment and the spiritual energy of their city-states. Their magic integrates local folklore and city-specific deities.<br /> <br /> - **Magical Practices**:<br /> - **City-Warding**: Using rituals and spells to protect city walls and important structures from both physical and magical threats.<br /> - **Folk Magic**: Employing traditional practices and charms that align with Hausa mythology to influence daily life and community well-being.<br /> - **Spiritual Architecture**: Incorporating magical symbols and rituals into city planning and construction to enhance spiritual energy and community cohesion.<br /> <br /> ### 4. **Great Zimbabwe: The Zimbabwean Stonekeepers**<br /> <br /> - **Paradigm**: **Sacred Geometry** <br /> The Zimbabwean Stonekeepers focus on the mystical significance of geometric patterns and sacred sites. Their magic emphasizes the connection between physical structures and spiritual power.<br /> <br /> - **Magical Practices**:<br /> - **Stone Magic**: Using the energy of ancient stone structures and monuments, such as the Great Enclosure, to perform rituals and channel power.<br /> - **Geomancy**: Creating and interpreting patterns in the earth to gain insights and influence events, reflecting their knowledge of construction and landscape.<br /> - **Cultural Artifacts**: Crafting and using ritual objects and symbols imbued with historical and spiritual significance.<br /> <br /> ### 5. **Swahili City-States: The Swahili Navigators**<br /> <br /> - **Paradigm**: **Oceanic Mysticism** <br /> The Swahili Navigators draw their magic from the vast and mysterious Indian Ocean and the interconnected trade networks that define their society. Their beliefs center on the harmony between maritime trade and mystical forces.<br /> <br /> - **Magical Practices**:<br /> - **Nautical Magic**: Utilizing rituals and spells related to the sea and trade winds to navigate, protect vessels, and ensure prosperous voyages.<br /> - **Trade Enchantment**: Enchanting goods and trade items to enhance their value and facilitate favorable trade relationships.<br /> - **Spirit Summoning**: Calling upon ocean spirits and deities to gain guidance and influence over maritime activities.<br /> <br /> ### 6. **Kingdom of Aksum: The Aksumite Theurgists**<br /> <br /> - **Paradigm**: **Divine Integration** <br /> The Aksumite Theurgists believe in integrating divine will with earthly affairs, drawing on their Christian faith and the legacy of their ancient empire. Their magic is closely linked with religious rites and divine intervention.<br /> <br /> - **Magical Practices**:<br /> - **Sacred Relics**: Using relics and sacred texts, including the Ark of the Covenant, to perform miracles and gain divine favor.<br /> - **Divine Rituals**: Conducting elaborate rituals and prayers to invoke blessings and divine protection, reflecting their Christian heritage.<br /> - **Holy Geometry**: Employing sacred symbols and geometric patterns in architectural design and ritual practice to align with divine principles.<br /> <br /> These Traditions reflect the unique cultural, religious, and historical contexts of each civilization, providing a rich and diverse range of magical practices for a *Mage: The Ascension* game.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T23:35:20-07:00","id":1539831,"title":"","content":"Since I think we haven't spoken too much about Islam, even thou it is one of the main concerns about both the Curia and the OoR, I decided to ask GPT to help flesh out a Tradition based on Islam, and the result is amazing.<br /> <br /> I'm just surprised that it went straight for Sufism, which is a minor denomination from Islam that is more directed towards what they call &quot;Mystic Islam&quot;, so not really a surprise. The only thing is that this would not be well received by Sunni or Shia Muslims.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">In *Mage: The Ascension*, Islamic magic would be a fascinating blend of mystical traditions, religious beliefs, and historical practices. Here\u2019s a detailed description of how Islamic magic might be represented in the game:<br /> <br /> ### **Islamic Magic: The Sufic Mystics**<br /> <br /> #### **Paradigm: Divine Oneness**<br /> <br /> Islamic magic in *Mage: The Ascension* would center around the concept of **Tawhid**, the Islamic principle of the oneness of God (Allah). This paradigm emphasizes the unity of the divine and the interconnectedness of all creation. Mages in this tradition believe that understanding and aligning with the divine will can influence the material world and the unseen realms.<br /> <br /> - **Core Belief**: The universe is a manifestation of the divine will, and everything is interconnected through God's will. Magic is a way to align with this divine order and bring about harmony and balance.<br /> <br /> #### **Magical Practices:**<br /> <br /> 1. **Invocation of Divine Names**:<br /> - **Practice**: Using the 99 names of Allah (Asmaul Husna) in invocations and prayers to channel specific divine attributes and powers. Each name represents a different aspect of the divine and can be used to invoke particular magical effects.<br /> - **Effect**: This practice can include blessings, protection, healing, or influencing events, depending on the name used.<br /> <br /> 2. **Sufi Mysticism**:<br /> - **Practice**: Engaging in practices from Sufism, such as **Dhikr** (remembrance of God) and **whirling**, to attain spiritual enlightenment and mystical experiences. These practices often involve intense meditation, chanting, and ecstatic states to align with the divine presence.<br /> - **Effect**: These mystical states can grant insight into the unseen, facilitate communion with spiritual entities, or enhance the mage\u2019s spiritual power.<br /> <br /> 3. **Astral and Elemental Magic**:<br /> - **Practice**: Drawing on Islamic cosmology, which divides the universe into the **Material World** and the **Unseen World (Ghayb)**, mages can perform rituals that interact with elemental forces and celestial bodies. Islamic magic includes manipulating the elements (earth, water, air, fire) and celestial influences.<br /> - **Effect**: This can involve elemental control, celestial navigation, or protection against unseen forces.<br /> <br /> 4. **Use of Amulets and Talismans**:<br /> - **Practice**: Crafting and using **taweez** (amulets) inscribed with Quranic verses or specific prayers to ward off evil, provide protection, or bring about desired outcomes.<br /> - **Effect**: These items can offer physical or magical protection, enhance personal power, or influence events according to the inscriptions.<br /> <br /> 5. **Healing and Purification**:<br /> - **Practice**: Utilizing **Ruqyah** (spiritual healing) techniques derived from the Quran and Hadith to cure ailments, purify spaces, or remove curses. This includes recitations, the use of blessed water, and spiritual cleansing rituals.<br /> - **Effect**: This practice can heal physical and spiritual afflictions, purify spaces from negative energies, or remove harmful influences.<br /> <br /> #### **Common Foci:**<br /> <br /> 1. **Sacred Texts**:<br /> - **Focus**: The Quran and Hadith (sayings of the Prophet Muhammad) are central to Islamic magic. The recitation and memorization of specific verses are crucial for invoking divine power and performing rituals.<br /> - **Effect**: Texts are used to channel divine attributes, perform blessings, and ensure that the magic aligns with divine will.<br /> <br /> 2. **Divine Names and Symbols**:<br /> - **Focus**: The 99 names of Allah and other sacred symbols like the **Bismillah** (In the name of Allah) are used as focal points in rituals and invocations.<br /> - **Effect**: These names and symbols enhance the mage\u2019s connection to divine attributes and direct magical energies.<br /> <br /> 3. **Prayer Beads (Misbaha)**:<br /> - **Focus**: Prayer beads used in Dhikr and other spiritual practices to aid in meditation and invocation.<br /> - **Effect**: They help maintain focus during spiritual practices and are used to count recitations or prayers.<br /> <br /> 4. **Amulets and Talismans**:<br /> - **Focus**: Crafted items inscribed with Quranic verses or prayers, often worn or carried for protection and spiritual enhancement.<br /> - **Effect**: Provide ongoing protection, amplify magical effects, or bring about specific outcomes.<br /> <br /> 5. **Sacred Sites**:<br /> - **Focus**: Locations of religious significance, such as mosques or shrines, which are imbued with spiritual energy and used as centers for rituals.<br /> - **Effect**: Enhance the potency of rituals performed within these sacred spaces and provide a strong connection to the divine.<br /> <br /> These elements create a rich tapestry of Islamic magical practices that align with the religious and cultural context of Islam while providing a broad range of magical possibilities for a *Mage: The Ascension* game.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-04T23:42:35-07:00","id":1539832,"title":"","content":"And now, the Magic for both Sunni and Shia Muslims, and I gotta say, I liked that very much, it is better than I could ever think about<b></b>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Certainly! Here\u2019s how Sunni and Shia Islamic magic might be represented in *Mage: The Ascension*, including their paradigms, magical practices, and common foci. <br /> ### **Sunni Islamic Magic: The Followers of the Sunna**<br /> <br /> #### **Paradigm: Divine Unity and Law**<br /> <br /> Sunni Islamic magic is rooted in the belief in **Tawhid**, the oneness of God, and the adherence to the **Sunna** (the practices of the Prophet Muhammad) as well as the **Sharia** (Islamic law). Sunni mages believe that magic is a tool to align with divine will and maintain social order.<br /> <br /> - **Core Belief**: The universe is created and governed by the divine will of Allah, and adherence to the Sunna and Sharia ensures harmony and justice. Magic is a means to uphold these divine principles and bring about lawful and just outcomes.<br /> <br /> #### **Magical Practices:**<br /> <br /> 1. **Recitation of Quranic Verses:**<br /> - **Practice**: Using specific verses from the Quran to invoke divine blessings, protection, or influence over events. These verses are recited during rituals or written on talismans.<br /> - **Effect**: Invokes divine power for various purposes such as healing, protection, and influencing outcomes in accordance with divine will.<br /> <br /> 2. **Adherence to the Sunna:**<br /> - **Practice**: Following the actions and sayings of the Prophet Muhammad as recorded in Hadith to perform rituals and magical practices.<br /> - **Effect**: Ensures that magical practices are in line with divine law and carry the blessing of the Prophet\u2019s guidance.<br /> <br /> 3. **Use of Religious Amulets:**<br /> - **Practice**: Creating and using amulets inscribed with Quranic verses or Hadith for protection and blessings.<br /> - **Effect**: Provides physical and magical protection, enhances personal power, and influences events.<br /> <br /> 4. **Ruqyah (Spiritual Healing):**<br /> - **Practice**: Using Quranic verses and prayers to heal physical and spiritual ailments, and to remove negative influences or curses.<br /> - **Effect**: Cures illnesses, purifies spaces, and removes harmful spiritual entities or influences.<br /> <br /> 5. **Charitable Acts and Fasting:**<br /> - **Practice**: Incorporating acts of charity (Sadaqah) and fasting (Sawm) as part of magical rituals to invoke divine favor and strengthen spiritual power.<br /> - **Effect**: Enhances magical efficacy and aligns the mage\u2019s actions with divine righteousness.<br /> <br /> #### **Common Foci:**<br /> <br /> 1. **Quranic Texts:**<br /> - **Focus**: The Quran, with its verses used in rituals, invocations, and as inscribed text on magical items.<br /> - **Effect**: Channels divine attributes and powers, ensuring rituals align with divine will.<br /> <br /> 2. **Hadith Collections:**<br /> - **Focus**: The sayings and actions of the Prophet Muhammad, used as guides for performing magic in accordance with the Sunna.<br /> - **Effect**: Ensures practices are aligned with prophetic traditions and divine law.<br /> <br /> 3. **Prayer Beads (Misbaha):**<br /> - **Focus**: Beads used in Dhikr (remembrance of God) to assist in meditation and invocation.<br /> - **Effect**: Helps maintain focus and connection during spiritual practices.<br /> <br /> 4. **Religious Symbols:**<br /> - **Focus**: Symbols such as the Bismillah (In the name of Allah) and other Quranic phrases used in rituals and talismans.<br /> - **Effect**: Enhances the efficacy of magical practices and provides protection.<br /> <br /> 5. **Sacred Spaces:**<br /> - **Focus**: Mosques and other religious sites used for performing rituals and seeking divine blessings.<br /> - **Effect**: Amplifies the power of rituals and provides a strong connection to divine energy.<br /> <br /> ### **Shia Islamic Magic: The Followers of the Ahl al-Bayt**<br /> <br /> #### **Paradigm: Divine Guidance and Imamate**<br /> <br /> Shia Islamic magic is centered around the belief in **Tawhid** and the **Imamate**, the leadership of the Prophet Muhammad\u2019s family, especially the Twelve Imams. Shia mages view magic as a means to align with divine will and the guidance of the Imams.<br /> <br /> - **Core Belief**: The universe is governed by the divine will of Allah, and the Imams, as divinely appointed leaders, offer spiritual guidance and authority. Magic is a way to connect with this divine guidance and seek intervention from the Imams.<br /> <br /> #### **Magical Practices:**<br /> <br /> 1. **Invocation of Imam\u2019s Names and Titles:**<br /> - **Practice**: Using the names and titles of the Twelve Imams in invocations and prayers to access their divine guidance and intercession.<br /> - **Effect**: Channels the spiritual authority and power of the Imams for various magical effects, such as healing, protection, and guidance.<br /> <br /> 2. **Shia Mysticism (Irfan):**<br /> - **Practice**: Engaging in practices from Shia mysticism, including meditation on divine attributes and the teachings of the Imams. This includes **Mawlid** (celebrations of the Imams\u2019 birthdays) and **Ziyarat** (pilgrimages to holy sites).<br /> - **Effect**: Provides spiritual insight, enhances magical power, and facilitates communion with divine entities.<br /> <br /> 3. **Use of Talismans and Amulets:**<br /> - **Practice**: Crafting and using talismans inscribed with Quranic verses, names of the Imams, or specific prayers for protection and blessings.<br /> - **Effect**: Offers protection, enhances personal power, and influences events in accordance with divine will.<br /> <br /> 4. **Spiritual Cleansing and Healing:**<br /> - **Practice**: Utilizing rituals and prayers for spiritual cleansing and healing, drawing on the teachings of the Imams and Shia holy texts.<br /> - **Effect**: Purifies individuals and spaces from negative influences and provides healing.<br /> <br /> 5. **Communal Prayers and Feasts:**<br /> - **Practice**: Incorporating communal prayers and feasts, such as those during **Ashura** and **Eid al-Ghadeer**, as part of magical rituals to invoke divine favor and strengthen community bonds.<br /> - **Effect**: Enhances magical efficacy and aligns rituals with communal spiritual practices.<br /> <br /> #### **Common Foci:**<br /> <br /> 1. **Holy Texts:**<br /> - **Focus**: Quranic texts and Shia religious writings, including the sayings of the Imams, used in rituals and invocations.<br /> - **Effect**: Channels divine attributes and the guidance of the Imams.<br /> <br /> 2. **Imam\u2019s Names and Titles:**<br /> - **Focus**: Names and titles of the Twelve Imams used in prayers and rituals.<br /> - **Effect**: Provides access to the spiritual authority and intercession of the Imams.<br /> <br /> 3. **Sacred Sites:**<br /> - **Focus**: Shrines and pilgrimage sites associated with the Imams and Shia saints.<br /> - **Effect**: Amplifies the power of rituals performed at these sites and connects with divine presence.<br /> <br /> 4. **Ritual Objects:**<br /> - **Focus**: Items used in Shia rituals, such as prayer rugs and beads.<br /> - **Effect**: Aids in meditation, invocation, and spiritual practices.<br /> <br /> 5. **Symbolic Artifacts:**<br /> - **Focus**: Artifacts associated with the Imams or significant events in Shia history.<br /> - **Effect**: Enhances magical practices and connects with historical and spiritual significance.<br /> <br /> These interpretations provide a rich basis for incorporating Sunni and Shia Islamic magic into a *Mage: The Ascension* game, each with its own distinct practices and foci reflecting their unique religious traditions.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-05T05:09:24-07:00","id":1539845,"title":"","content":"So, after understanding a bit about how the scenery on the Magic of Islam is, we can firmly say that there's no less than 3 main distinct and often times antagonistic groups of Islamic Mages, the Sunni, Shia and Sufi Mages, each with their own branches of magic and their specific peculiarities.<br /> <br /> We can discuss their role in the history of the Ascension War later on other than just merely Jihad - although I have to admit, their role would probably be limited to Jihad indeed up until at least the 19th century. Even after that thou, I see them as even less likely to form external alliances than either the Curia or the Patriarchs would be, and there's a strong possibility that they would just simply try to engage in Jihad against... Well, everybody else, which includes Jihad against the Curia and the Patriarchs, against the Technocracy and even against one another (in fact, their main fights are probably between Sunni vs Shia even more so than against the Technocracy).<br /> <br /> The exception are the Sufi, they would indeed be willing to reach out to Christians and Jews, and this may surprise you, but even to &quot;pagans&quot;. But the Sufi are the smaller sect of Islam, only about 5% of Muslims (but from 1.4 billion people, that's still around 70 million Sufi Muslims). Sufism is more open to interpretation and not so closed to external influences, so it is possible that the Sufi craft (again, we can name these later) would be willing to join the Traditions or Disparate Alliance or some similar. The Sunni and Shia however, not a chance.<br /> <br /> And now I think it's interesting to understand a bit of China; after that I'll go a bit into the Indian subcontinent<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">China has a rich tapestry of religious beliefs and practices that have evolved over millennia. Here\u2019s an overview of the most common religions in China throughout its history, along with their respective time periods:<br /> 1. **Ancient Chinese Religion (Shamanism and Folk Religion)**<br /> - **Time Period**: Prehistoric times to present<br /> - **Description**: Ancient Chinese religion primarily consisted of shamanistic practices, ancestor worship, and the veneration of nature spirits. This indigenous belief system formed the foundation of later religious traditions and has continued to influence Chinese culture and religious practices.<br /> <br /> 2. **Confucianism**<br /> - **Time Period**: Developed around the 6th century BCE and became a dominant ideology during the Han Dynasty (206 BCE \u2013 220 CE). It has influenced Chinese culture and governance continuously since then.<br /> - **Description**: Founded by Confucius, Confucianism emphasizes moral virtues, social harmony, and respect for hierarchy and tradition. While not a religion in the traditional sense, it has been a guiding philosophy and ethical system in Chinese society.<br /> <br /> 3. **Daoism (Taoism)**<br /> - **Time Period**: Emerged around the 4th century BCE and has remained an influential spiritual and philosophical tradition in China.<br /> - **Description**: Based on the teachings of Laozi and Zhuangzi, Daoism emphasizes living in harmony with the Tao (the Way), natural simplicity, and inner cultivation. It encompasses both philosophical teachings and religious practices, including rituals and alchemy.<br /> <br /> 4. **Buddhism**<br /> - **Time Period**: Introduced to China during the Han Dynasty (around the 1st century CE) and became widely popular during the Tang Dynasty (618\u2013907 CE). It has remained a significant religion in China since then.<br /> - **Description**: Buddhism, particularly Mahayana Buddhism, was adapted to Chinese culture and became a major spiritual force. It brought new religious concepts, such as karma and reincarnation, and established monasteries and temples throughout China.<br /> <br /> 5. **Chinese Folk Religion**<br /> - **Time Period**: Continuous from ancient times to present<br /> - **Description**: A diverse collection of local practices, including ancestor worship, the veneration of local deities, and the use of divination and ritual. Chinese folk religion has often coexisted with other major religious traditions, blending elements from Confucianism, Daoism, and Buddhism.<br /> <br /> 6. **Christianity**<br /> - **Time Period**: First introduced in the 7th century (Nestorian Christianity during the Tang Dynasty), but more significantly during the Ming Dynasty (1368\u20131644) and Qing Dynasty (1644\u20131912) with the arrival of Catholic and Protestant missionaries.<br /> - **Description**: Christianity in China has experienced periods of growth and suppression. While not as widespread as traditional Chinese religions, it has a presence, particularly among certain communities.<br /> <br /> 7. **Islam**<br /> - **Time Period**: Introduced during the Tang Dynasty (around the 7th century) and has been present in China, particularly among ethnic minorities like the Hui and Uighur people.<br /> - **Description**: Islam in China has developed unique cultural expressions and has been practiced continuously, especially in regions like Xinjiang and Ningxia.<br /> <br /> 8. **Manichaeism and Zoroastrianism**<br /> - **Time Period**: Manichaeism and Zoroastrianism were present during the Tang Dynasty (618\u2013907 CE) due to cultural exchanges along the Silk Road.<br /> - **Description**: These religions had limited influence and eventually declined, but they left an imprint on Chinese religious diversity.<br /> <br /> 9. **Neo-Confucianism**<br /> - **Time Period**: Developed during the Song Dynasty (960\u20131279 CE) and became dominant in the Ming (1368\u20131644 CE) and Qing (1644\u20131912 CE) Dynasties.<br /> - **Description**: A revival and reinterpretation of Confucianism incorporating elements from Daoism and Buddhism. Neo-Confucianism emphasized rationalism, morality, and the importance of education.<br /> <br /> These religions and philosophies have often intermingled, with many Chinese people historically practicing a blend of Confucian, Daoist, Buddhist, and folk religious beliefs.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Celestial Bureaucracy is an essential concept in Chinese religious and mythological traditions, particularly within Daoism and Chinese folk religion. Here\u2019s an overview of its place within the broader context of Chinese religious history:<br /> <br /> ### The Concept of the Celestial Bureaucracy<br /> <br /> **Description**:<br /> - The Celestial Bureaucracy is a hierarchical structure of deities and spiritual beings that mirrors the administrative bureaucracy of imperial China. It reflects the organization and values of ancient Chinese society, with a clear hierarchy and division of responsibilities among gods and spirits.<br /> - This system posits that the heavens operate like a government, with the Jade Emperor (Yuhuang Dadi) at the top, overseeing a vast array of gods, goddesses, and lesser spirits who manage various aspects of the cosmos and human affairs.<br /> <br /> ### Integration with Major Chinese Religions<br /> <br /> **Daoism**:<br /> - **Place**: Central to religious Daoism, the Celestial Bureaucracy integrates Daoist cosmology and mythology.<br /> - **Role**: Daoist priests and practitioners often engage with this bureaucracy through rituals, offerings, and prayers to various deities for protection, health, prosperity, and guidance. The Jade Emperor and other high-ranking deities are frequently invoked in Daoist temples and ceremonies.<br /> <br /> **Chinese Folk Religion**:<br /> - **Place**: Deeply embedded in folk religious practices, where it forms the backbone of local and family worship traditions.<br /> - **Role**: Common folk practices include the worship of household gods, city gods (chenghuang), and other local deities who are considered part of the celestial administration. Festivals, offerings, and rituals are performed to honor these deities and seek their blessings.<br /> <br /> **Confucianism**:<br /> - **Place**: While Confucianism primarily focuses on ethics and social philosophy rather than metaphysical beliefs, the concept of the Celestial Bureaucracy aligns with Confucian ideals of order, hierarchy, and duty.<br /> - **Role**: Confucianism does not emphasize the worship of deities, but it acknowledges the importance of rituals and ceremonies, which often intersect with folk religious practices involving the Celestial Bureaucracy.<br /> <br /> **Buddhism**:<br /> - **Place**: Buddhism in China has adapted to and incorporated elements of the Celestial Bureaucracy.<br /> - **Role**: Chinese Buddhist practices sometimes include the veneration of celestial beings who are integrated into the broader structure of Buddhist cosmology. Deities like the Four Heavenly Kings are examples of such syncretism.<br /> <br /> ### Influence and Practices<br /> <br /> **Rituals and Worship**:<br /> - **Temples**: Temples dedicated to various deities within the Celestial Bureaucracy are found throughout China. The Jade Emperor, local gods, and other celestial officials have specific temples where followers can pray, make offerings, and seek divine intervention.<br /> - **Festivals**: Many Chinese festivals, such as the Lunar New Year, the Lantern Festival, and the Ghost Festival, involve rituals and ceremonies that honor the deities of the Celestial Bureaucracy.<br /> <br /> **Everyday Life**:<br /> - **Ancestral Worship**: The Celestial Bureaucracy often intersects with ancestral worship, as ancestors are believed to become part of the spiritual realm and can intercede with higher deities on behalf of their descendants.<br /> - **Divination and Alchemy**: Practices like divination, astrology, and alchemy often invoke the authority of celestial officials, seeking their guidance and approval.<br /> <br /> ### Historical and Cultural Relevance<br /> <br /> **Syncretism**:<br /> - The Celestial Bureaucracy exemplifies the syncretic nature of Chinese religious traditions, blending elements from Daoism, Buddhism, Confucianism, and folk beliefs into a cohesive spiritual system.<br /> <br /> **Cultural Reflection**:<br /> - It reflects the structure and values of Chinese society, emphasizing hierarchy, order, duty, and the interplay between the human and divine realms. This mirroring of earthly and heavenly governance reinforces social norms and the importance of ritual and propriety.<br /> <br /> **Endurance**:<br /> - The concept has endured for millennia, adapting to changing dynasties, social structures, and religious influences while maintaining its core principles. It continues to be a significant aspect of Chinese religious life and cultural heritage.<br /> <br /> The Celestial Bureaucracy remains a vital part of Chinese spirituality, embodying the interconnectedness of the divine and human worlds and providing a framework for understanding the cosmos and one\u2019s place within it.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, I had completely forgotten to say that China also has its ancient Polytheistic (&quot;pagan&quot;) Folk religion, which dates to the prehistoric times. Yes, that's what the fabled &quot;Celestial Bureocracy&quot; is after, it is a Pantheon of Gods, which is China's oldest religion.<br /> <br /> Chinese religiosity however is highly syncretic, so it has adapted to Daoism (Taoism), which is yet another original Chinese religion, Confucianism, which is not a religion exactly but rather a philosophy and moral code, and later on to Buddhism, which was imported from India.<br /> <br /> All of those are present in pretty much every aspects of Chinese religiosity, and in all of those the Celestial Bureocracy is present (oh, the Hermetics would LOVE this concept of &quot;As Above, so Bellow&quot; that the Celestial Bureocracy has - and yes, this is a true concept from real life Hermeticism. It's one of it's core tenets actually, that the material Plane imitates the higher planes. Kinda like the WoD Umbra you know? And people wonder why I say that the WoD has Gnosticism dripping throughout every single page of it. Ok, rant's over)<br /> <br /> Therefore, Chinese Mages would be BOTH Daoists, Confucianists, Buddhists but also Polytheists adherents to the Celestial Bureocracy's Pantheon. That's who the Wu Lung would be.<br /> <br /> By the way, the Akashic Brotherhood would be completely gone. There's no place for them actually. Just the Wu Lung, who would be completely integrated to the Imperial Power structure, and would fall down together with their last Emperor because of that.<br /> <br /> Now, a brief description of how that magic of the Wu Lung (insert new name for them here) would work:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A group of Chinese mages in *Mage: The Ascension* could draw from a rich tapestry of Daoism, Confucianism, Buddhism, and Chinese folk religion, including the concept of the Celestial Bureaucracy. Here's how their paradigm, magical practices, and common foci might be structured:<br /> <br /> ### **Paradigm: Harmony with the Dao and the Celestial Bureaucracy**<br /> <br /> **Core Beliefs**:<br /> 1. **Unity of Heaven, Earth, and Humanity**: The universe operates as a harmonious whole, with the Dao (the Way) being the fundamental principle that underlies all existence. The goal of magic is to align oneself with the Dao and the cosmic order.<br /> 2. **Celestial Bureaucracy**: The universe is governed by a celestial hierarchy, with deities and spirits overseeing various aspects of reality. Mages work in harmony with these beings, drawing on their power and guidance.<br /> 3. **Balance and Harmony**: Maintaining balance and harmony within oneself, society, and the natural world is crucial. Magic is used to restore balance where it is disrupted.<br /> 4. **Moral and Ethical Conduct**: Influenced by Confucianism, the mages adhere to ethical conduct, emphasizing virtues such as respect for elders, filial piety, and righteousness.<br /> <br /> ### **Magical Practices**<br /> <br /> 1. **Daoist Alchemy and Internal Cultivation**:<br /> - **Internal Alchemy**: Practices like Qi Gong, Tai Chi, and meditation are used to cultivate internal energy (Qi). This energy can be directed for various magical purposes, such as healing, protection, or enhancing physical abilities.<br /> - **External Alchemy**: The creation of elixirs and talismans using alchemical processes, often imbued with mystical symbols and rituals.<br /> <br /> 2. **Rituals and Offerings to the Celestial Bureaucracy**:<br /> - **Invocation of Deities**: Rituals to communicate with and seek blessings from gods, goddesses, and ancestral spirits. These may include prayer, chanting, and burning incense.<br /> - **Offerings and Sacrifices**: Food, incense, paper money, and other offerings are made to appease and honor celestial beings and ancestors.<br /> <br /> 3. **Buddhist Mantras and Sutras**:<br /> - **Chanting and Recitation**: Reciting Buddhist sutras and mantras to invoke the power of the Buddha, Bodhisattvas, and protective deities.<br /> - **Meditation**: Buddhist meditation practices to attain spiritual insight and clarity.<br /> <br /> 4. **Feng Shui and Geomancy**:<br /> - **Environmental Harmonization**: Using principles of Feng Shui to arrange spaces in alignment with cosmic forces. This practice is used for creating auspicious environments and warding off negative influences.<br /> <br /> 5. **Confucian Rituals**:<br /> - **Ancestral Worship**: Rituals honoring ancestors, seeking their guidance, and maintaining familial harmony.<br /> - **Rites of Passage and Social Harmony**: Conducting ceremonies for significant life events (weddings, funerals, etc.) and maintaining social order through ritual observances.<br /> <br /> 6. **Folk Magic and Talismans**:<br /> - **Talismans and Amulets**: Creation and use of objects inscribed with magical symbols or writings to protect, heal, or attract good fortune.<br /> - **Folk Healing**: Use of herbal medicine, acupuncture, and other traditional healing methods.<br /> <br /> ### **Common Foci**<br /> <br /> 1. **Incense and Candles**: Used in rituals to purify spaces, invoke deities, and offer respect.<br /> 2. **Talismans and Amulets**: Inscribed papers, metal charms, or jade carvings imbued with protective or healing properties.<br /> 3. **Ritual Implements**: Items like swords, mirrors, and bells used in Daoist and folk rituals.<br /> 4. **Sacred Texts**: Daoist scriptures, Confucian classics, Buddhist sutras, and other religious texts.<br /> 5. **Meditation Cushions and Prayer Beads**: Used in meditation and prayer practices.<br /> 6. **Alchemical Tools**: Cauldrons, herbs, minerals, and other materials for creating alchemical substances.<br /> 7. **Feng Shui Compasses**: Instruments used to determine auspicious alignments and orientations.<br /> <br /> ### **Operational Approach**<br /> <br /> **Community and Temple-Based Practices**:<br /> - These mages may operate from temples or community centers where they serve both spiritual and practical roles in their communities. They could act as priests, healers, spiritual advisors, and protectors, using their magic to address both mundane and supernatural issues.<br /> <br /> **Role of the Master and Disciple**:<br /> - Traditionally, these mages would follow a master-disciple relationship, where knowledge and practices are passed down through direct instruction and apprenticeship. This hierarchy mirrors the structure of the Celestial Bureaucracy and Confucian social order.<br /> <br /> **Interactions with Other Mages and Traditions**:<br /> - While primarily drawing from Chinese traditions, these mages might interact with others in the global magical community, offering their unique insights into balance, harmony, and spiritual cultivation. They could form alliances based on mutual respect for different cultural practices and philosophies.<br /> <br /> This synthesis of Daoism, Confucianism, Buddhism, and Chinese folk religion would provide a rich and diverse foundation for magical practices, emphasizing harmony with the cosmos, ethical conduct, and reverence for the celestial order.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-05T05:28:13-07:00","id":1539848,"title":"","content":"I believe that Manicheism and Zoroastrianism deserve some honorable mentions, despite their status as declining religions; yet, from the 14th century up to now, they may still be background for smaller Crafts.<br /> <br /> Also, you'll notice some keen similarities towards the big &quot;Illuminatis&quot; of our alt history - aka the Gnostics... Yeah, the same Gnostics behind the Order of Hermes and the Masons, the ones that will ultimately turn into the Technocracy (after you Derivated their function about 10 times).<br /> <br /> <i>It all adds up<br /> <br /> <br /> <img width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://i.kym-cdn.com/entries/icons/facebook/000/022/524/pepe_silvia_meme_banner.jpg\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://i.kym-cdn.com/entries/icons/facebook/000/022/524/pepe_silvia_meme_banner.jpg\" data-thumb-url=\"https://i.kym-cdn.com/entries/icons/facebook/000/022/524/pepe_silvia_meme_banner.jpg\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/></i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-05T05:40:45-07:00","id":1539850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">**Manichaeism** and **Zoroastrianism** are both ancient religions with some overlapping concepts, but they have distinct origins, teachings, and theological structures.<br /> <br /> ### **Origins and Founders**<br /> <br /> - **Manichaeism**:<br /> - **Founder**: Mani, a Persian prophet who lived in the 3rd century CE.<br /> - **Geographic Origin**: Mesopotamia (modern-day Iraq and Iran).<br /> - **Time Period**: Founded in the 3rd century CE.<br /> <br /> - **Zoroastrianism**:<br /> - **Founder**: Traditionally attributed to the prophet Zoroaster (also known as Zarathustra).<br /> - **Geographic Origin**: Ancient Persia (modern-day Iran).<br /> - **Time Period**: Likely founded between the 10th and 6th centuries BCE.<br /> <br /> ### **Theological Differences**<br /> <br /> - **Manichaeism**:<br /> - **Dualism**: A strict dualistic belief in the eternal struggle between the forces of Light (good) and Darkness (evil). The material world is seen as the creation of the evil principle.<br /> - **Cosmology**: The universe is a battleground for the conflict between Light and Darkness. The soul, part of the Light, is trapped in the material body, which is part of Darkness.<br /> - **Prophets and Syncretism**: Mani presented himself as the last in a line of prophets, including Zoroaster, Buddha, and Jesus, aiming to synthesize their teachings into a universal religion.<br /> - **Scriptures**: Manichaeism had its own scriptures written by Mani and his followers.<br /> <br /> - **Zoroastrianism**:<br /> - **Dualism**: Also dualistic but less strictly so. It emphasizes the struggle between Ahura Mazda (the supreme god of light and wisdom) and Angra Mainyu (the destructive spirit).<br /> - **Cosmology**: The world is created by Ahura Mazda as inherently good, with evil as an opposing force that corrupts it. Humans have free will to choose between good (asha) and evil (druj).<br /> - **Monotheistic Elements**: Zoroastrianism is often considered the first monotheistic religion, with Ahura Mazda as the supreme deity. However, it also acknowledges other divine beings and spirits.<br /> - **Scriptures**: The primary sacred text is the Avesta, which includes hymns, rituals, and religious teachings attributed to Zoroaster.<br /> <br /> ### **Ethical and Moral Teachings**<br /> <br /> - **Manichaeism**:<br /> - Focuses on the salvation of the soul by rejecting the material world and living a life of asceticism. It teaches that the soul must be liberated from the material world of Darkness through knowledge and spiritual practices.<br /> <br /> - **Zoroastrianism**:<br /> - Emphasizes the moral choice between good and evil, with a focus on good thoughts, good words, and good deeds. It teaches that humans should promote the order of asha (truth and righteousness) and fight against druj (falsehood and chaos).<br /> <br /> ### **Religious Practices**<br /> <br /> - **Manichaeism**:<br /> - **Asceticism**: Encourages a strict ascetic lifestyle, especially among the elect, who are the spiritual elite.<br /> - **Rituals and Scriptures**: Rituals include prayers, fasting, and the study of Manichaean scriptures.<br /> <br /> - **Zoroastrianism**:<br /> - **Rituals**: Includes fire worship (as fire is seen as a symbol of purity and Ahura Mazda), rituals of purification, and recitation of the Gathas (hymns attributed to Zoroaster).<br /> - **Ethical Living**: Emphasizes living an ethical life and participating in community rituals and festivals.<br /> <br /> ### **Historical Influence and Legacy**<br /> <br /> - **Manichaeism**:<br /> - Spread widely across the Roman Empire, Persia, and even into China, but faced persecution and declined over time. Its influence persisted in various dualistic traditions and sects.<br /> <br /> - **Zoroastrianism**:<br /> - Dominant in the Persian empires and influenced later religious and philosophical systems, including Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. Although much reduced in number today, Zoroastrian communities continue to exist, primarily in Iran and India (where they are known as Parsis).<br /> <br /> In summary, while both Manichaeism and Zoroastrianism involve dualistic elements and originate from Persia, they differ significantly in their origins, theological details, and religious practices. Manichaeism's strict dualism and syncretic nature contrast with Zoroastrianism's emphasis on a single supreme deity and a more nuanced understanding of good and evil.</div> </div> </div> </div><i>**Manichaeism**:</i> <i>- **Dualism**: A strict dualistic belief in the eternal struggle between the forces of Light (good) and Darkness (evil). The material world is seen as the creation of the evil principle.</i><br /> <br /> Notice how this is basically Gnosticism 101 (or rather, Gnosticism is Manicheism under a Christian syncretic visage).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ChatGPT</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the 14th century, both Manichaeism and Zoroastrianism had experienced significant changes in influence and presence, with varying degrees of impact in different regions.<br /> <br /> ### **Manichaeism**<br /> <br /> 1. **Decline in Influence**:<br /> - **Persecution and Decline**: Manichaeism faced severe persecution from various political and religious authorities, including the Roman Empire, the Sassanian Empire, and later the Islamic caliphates. By the 14th century, it had largely declined as an organized religion.<br /> - **Surviving Communities**: Small Manichaean communities persisted in Central Asia and parts of China. However, these groups were increasingly marginalized and isolated, with limited influence.<br /> <br /> 2. **Legacy and Influence**:<br /> - Despite its decline, Manichaeism's dualistic ideas influenced later religious and philosophical movements, including some Christian heretical sects in Europe and various Gnostic traditions. However, by the 14th century, these influences were more historical and theoretical rather than direct or widespread.<br /> <br /> ### **Zoroastrianism**<br /> <br /> 1. **Status in Persia**:<br /> - **Islamic Dominance**: Following the Islamic conquest of Persia in the 7th century, Zoroastrianism experienced a significant decline in prominence as Islam became the dominant religion. By the 14th century, most Persians had converted to Islam.<br /> - **Zoroastrian Communities**: Despite the decline, Zoroastrian communities continued to exist, primarily in rural areas and among specific communities that maintained their religious traditions. They faced discrimination and pressure to convert, but they managed to preserve their cultural and religious practices to some extent.<br /> <br /> 2. **Influence and Legacy**:<br /> - **Cultural Influence**: Zoroastrian traditions, especially those related to festivals, ethical teachings, and cosmology, had a lasting influence on Persian culture, even among Muslim populations. Elements of Zoroastrianism continued to be present in Persian literature, folklore, and practices.<br /> - **Diaspora and the Parsis**: A significant group of Zoroastrians migrated to India between the 8th and 10th centuries, where they became known as the Parsis. By the 14th century, the Parsi community was well-established in India, preserving Zoroastrian traditions and contributing to the cultural and economic life of the region.<br /> <br /> ### **Summary**<br /> <br /> In the 14th century, **Manichaeism** was largely in decline, with limited influence and only a few surviving communities. **Zoroastrianism**, while no longer the dominant religion in Persia, continued to exist as a minority faith and had a lasting cultural impact in Persia and among the Parsi community in India. Both religions, though diminished in direct influence, contributed to the religious and cultural landscape of the regions where they had once thrived.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Now, while Manichaeism is perhaps too small to merit its own Craft, it doesn't matter all that much since their importance is in being Syncretically modified in the West until originating the Gnostic Heresy, then the Order of Hermes and the Masons, and later on the Technocracy (which would be seen as a travesty and abomination by the original Manichaeists), but the Zoroastrians however may very well harbor a very organized (even if relatively small compared to others crafts or traditions) that still exists even into the modern age.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-05T07:02:47-07:00","id":1539852,"title":"","content":"Using ChatGPT is a bad idea for things such as this, as it is well known to hallucinate garbage (what you've copied and pasted is riddled with inaccuracies), and what <i>isn't</i> utterly worthless is made by regurgitating generic pap. I'm convinced we can do better without it by just reading Wikipedia.<br /> <br /> As far as Zoroastrianism, the Taftani are clearly supposed to be Zoroastrian--look up asha and druj.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539827#post1539827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ehh... What is the Fifth Sun Society?</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539718#post1539718\">They're the Mesoamerican faction I came up with</a>. They are basically doomsday preppers. The Maya don't appear to have had a version of the Five Suns cosmology, but magical practices were similar enough and they're close enough that I think plenty of them might end up joining it. Despite the name, it's really about dealing with clear threats to the entire region--corrupt mages, certain magic monsters, and possibly major droughts. So, I was thinking about not having multiple factions join from Mesoamerica--sure, the region has enough variability that you could have multiple ones, but OOC it seems better to have the main members of the Disparate Alliance be more geographically spread out, even if they're concentrated in Mesoamerica and South America.<br /> <br /> On the other hand... the Disparate Alliance won't be limited to nine seats, so they could have multiple crafts which are just name-dropped and don't show up again.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539827#post1539827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Will, initially all of these roles would have to be played by the triple alliance (Incas, Mayans, Aztecs). Then, as other groups start to join, those could be delegated or shared between more appropriate groups.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is true, at least if the organization founds its equivalent of Horizon early in its history.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539818#post1539818\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How does then the Traditions form, or why don't the Traditions just grow out of the Disparate Alliance?<br /> <br /> I think that, given the Hermetic Order love towards exotic mysticism, they would have loved to study Amerindian Magic, and by doing so, a contact between then could be formed, and from that, cooperation, that could see the Alliance evolve into what then would become the Traditions</div> </div> </div> </div>Because the Order of Hermes is the seed of the Traditions, and the Order of Hermes are initially part of the Order of Reason--many early scientists were interested in the occult as well and it took a while for them to split. What I suggest happening is that the in the initial stages of the Order of Reason's history, it and the Curia are closely entwined and fighting against the Disparate Alliance... maybe not in a state of open war, precisely, but there's conflict.<br /> <br /> Eventually, though, the Order of Reason turns on the Order of Hermes and tries to purge them. They fail. The Order of Hermes goes to ground. They might apply to join the Disparate Alliance, but the Disparates don't want them, at least not as a group. Why? Because just a couple decades ago they were enemies and there's security concerns--the Order of Hermes might be riddled with spies and Hermetics might defect back to the Order of Reason. Nor does the Disparate Alliance need them. This is the 16th or 17th century; the geopolitical situation is not the same as it is during the 18th or 19th centuries. Membership is out of the question, even if the two might offer each other a little help for pragmatic reasons. But the Hermetics don't remain passive. They seek refuge with potentially friendly mages who aren't members of the Disparates, which is how the Wu Lung eventually get pulled into the Traditions. Any time the Order of Reason or Curia/Patriarchs purge any of their own factions, the Hermetics are there to try and recruit them. And the Hermetics have a <i>much</i> better power base in the imperial core than the Disparate Alliance does, so they're usually the first ones to make the offer. Eventually this gives rise to the Traditions.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-05T08:25:09-07:00","id":1539856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539852#post1539852\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Using ChatGPT is a bad idea for things such as this, as it is well known to hallucinate garbage (what you've copied and pasted is riddled with inaccuracies), and what <i>isn't</i> utterly worthless is made by regurgitating generic pap. I'm convinced we can do better without it by just reading Wikipedia.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh I'm well aware of that, which is why I posted the &quot;resume&quot; I tried to make the cursed thing do, to show just how cursed some of the answers are. The ones I posted are mostly the ones I already selected after heavily directing our still fetal machine-God. I use ChatGPT just to mainly clarify my text, I cannot for the love of me be succinct. Also, ChatGPT sometimes comes with interesting ideas for combining concepts; I used it for creating very interesting spirits for example; I just need to guide it towards what I want, but with ChatGPT I managed to completely overhaul my spirit worlds, both with spirits that fit a place and are INTERESTING, as long as their abilities, modus operandi, desires and so on. All I have to do is beat it with a stick every one of the 100's of times it tries to suggest &quot;Aegis&quot; as a spirit lol.<br /> <br /> But it is useful as a complement. I wouldn't discard it so quickly. For example, I know quite a bit about Islam, so him talking about the 12 Imams for Shia Islam is nothing new to me, but I must confess that my imagination is somewhat lacking and to try to devise a purely Shia Mage that doesn't just 'I pray to Allah, Mohamed and Ali&quot; for everything he does is kinda hard.<br /> <br /> In fact, I'm thinking about using ChatGPT for the Christians too, because again, I probably lack imagination. Christianism is the one I'm most knowledgeable about - I live in the largest catholic country in the world after all (Brazil) - but it's the details that ChatGPT gives that helps me a lot. I don't want my Curia guys to just simply pray some rubbish in Latim and be done with it, I want some more attention to details, and the bot can help in that regard.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As far as Zoroastrianism, the Taftani are clearly supposed to be Zoroastrian--look up asha and druj. </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's what I thought about the first time I saw them, then after some more reading I thought they were just some generic &quot;pre-islamic pagans&quot; of Arabia.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539718#post1539718\">They're the Mesoamerican faction I came up with</a>. They are basically doomsday preppers. The Maya don't appear to have had a version of the Five Suns cosmology, but magical practices were similar enough and they're close enough that I think plenty of them might end up joining it. Despite the name, it's really about dealing with clear threats to the entire region--corrupt mages, certain magic monsters, and possibly major droughts. So, I was thinking about not having multiple factions join from Mesoamerica--sure, the region has enough variability that you could have multiple ones, but OOC it seems better to have the main members of the Disparate Alliance be more geographically spread out, even if they're concentrated in Mesoamerica and South America. </div> </div> </div>Oh, ok, I somehow missed that. Yeah it makes sense, the whole Nahuatl thing was about keeping the Fifth Sun floating... Yeah, I imagined that it was a Nahuatl group, but because I never heard of it before I supposed it was some official Craft that I didn't know.<br /> <br /> I think I would like a Craft for the Nahuatl yes, and the Fifth Sun is perfect for it, but I would also like one for the Maya and another for the Inti religion of the Incas. Those 3 civilizations are pretty unique to deserve that, since we are going that route, and yes, there's no limit to Crafts (although it is probably best not to have 500 of them). Then I think we could have one more craft from South America from the Tupi-Guarani which is the most widespread native group - again, in Paraguay it is still largely used as a language, and even in Brazil it was used as <i>lingua franca </i>not only for the natives but also for the Europeans to talk with the natives (that's right, the Portuguese spoke Tupi-Guarani with the natives instead of Portuguese, even with natives of other tribes) up until the 19th century and it's still present in the northern regions of Brazil and South Venezuela and Colombia.<br /> <br /> And another Craft for North America, but that one is a bit harder, since there's no one native group that was as dominant as the Tupi-Guarani were in South America, so I thought about North America having a federation of natives instead<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the other hand... the Disparate Alliance won't be limited to nine seats, so they could have multiple crafts which are just name-dropped and don't show up again. </div> </div> </div>Exactly. And I'm not sure that the Traditions need to keep that Cabalist number either thou<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because the Order of Hermes is the seed of the Traditions, and the Order of Hermes are initially part of the Order of Reason--many early scientists were interested in the occult as well and it took a while for them to split. What I suggest happening is that the in the initial stages of the Order of Reason's history, it and the Curia are closely entwined and fighting against the Disparate Alliance... maybe not in a state of open war, precisely, but there's conflict. </div> </div> </div>I am of the same mind here. Curia, OoH and OoR initially hold common interests, but later they jump on each other's throats<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Eventually, though, the Order of Reason turns on the Order of Hermes and tries to purge them. They fail. The Order of Hermes goes to ground. They might apply to join the Disparate Alliance, but the Disparates don't want them, at least not as a group. Why? Because just a couple decades ago they were enemies and there's security concerns--the Order of Hermes might be riddled with spies and Hermetics might defect back to the Order of Reason. Nor does the Disparate Alliance need them. This is the 16th or 17th century; the geopolitical situation is not the same as it is during the 18th or 19th centuries. Membership is out of the question, even if the two might offer each other a little help for pragmatic reasons. But the Hermetics don't remain passive. They seek refuge with potentially friendly mages who aren't members of the Disparates, which is how the Wu Lung eventually get pulled into the Traditions. Any time the Order of Reason or Curia/Patriarchs purge any of their own factions, the Hermetics are there to try and recruit them. And the Hermetics have a <i>much</i> better power base in the imperial core than the Disparate Alliance does, so they're usually the first ones to make the offer. Eventually this gives rise to the Traditions. </div> </div> </div>I actually liked that idea. So, the Disparate Alliance have many sources from recruiting, we just need to come up with a few dozen names spread over the globe. But who then do the Hermetics recruit? Sure, the Wu Lung are a given, although I don't see why would they even consider it siding with such &quot;barbarians&quot; before the century of humiliation. The Etherites and VA also must exist for no other reason than that they're so freaking cool and don't really disrupt anything with their existence (as in a secret Theosophical group of heretics hiding amongst the Christendom like the Celestial Chorus does).<br /> <br /> But who else?<br /> <br /> Where do the Indian Bhramins go to? The Disparate Alliance or the Traditions? I would prefer them to go to the Traditions, but I want to hear opinions and we also need to detail how that process go about","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28035,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","user":"Magus Maximus","date":". 08-05-2024, 11:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T09:05:30-07:00","id":1539930,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539856#post1539856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where do the Indian Bhramins go to? The Disparate Alliance or the Traditions? I would prefer them to go to the Traditions, but I want to hear opinions and we also need to detail how that process go about</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm under the impression that colonialism was pretty brutal on India, so the Disparate Alliance comes to as a reasonable historical partner.<br /> <br /> In this case I suggest to consider a different approach with the Traditions in mind. The Traditions, when they're not being Gnostic, are very &quot;Orientalist&quot;. That's part of the &quot;occult resurgence on Europe&quot; that it's, I think, important to their identity. It's also widely known that Orientalism it's not a word that projects &quot;historical accuracy&quot;, to put it midly...<br /> <br /> SO, what if the Bhramins never cared with joining the Traditions, but the Traditions <i>went to them instead?.</i> Interested on the occult prowess of mystical orient the Traditions offered all that they could, learned what they could (filtered trough their Paradigms), and as a result Indian-inspired Practices could've been popularized among the Traditions","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T11:31:02-07:00","id":1539934,"title":"","content":"Similar reasons are also why I imagine the Wu Lung being part of the Traditions earlier than the Qing's decline. Members of the Order of Hermes fled to China to escape the Curia and Order of Reason--it had a reputation as a place with deep occult traditions, they considered it a &quot;civilized country,&quot; and there's no Order of Reason or Curia there to bother them. The initial waves brought rare magical artifacts/tass/texts to the Wu Lung to basically bribe them into supporting their case for asylum, but kept in contact with their colleagues in Europe. Eventually, friendships of a kind form between the Chinese Hermetics and Wu Lung. These personal ties would damage relations between the Wu Lung and Order of Reason (sure, the Order of Hermes may be barbarians, but they're the <i>Qing's</i> barbarians now and they do not appreciate the Order of Reason trying to dictate whom they do and do not accept) and drag the Wu Lung into the Traditions even before the Century of Humiliation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28383,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","user":"Citrakayah","date":". 08-06-2024, 02:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T12:50:18-07:00","id":1539937,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539930#post1539930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm under the impression that colonialism was pretty brutal on India, so the Disparate Alliance comes to as a reasonable historical partner.<br /> <br /> In this case I suggest to consider a different approach with the Traditions in mind. The Traditions, when they're not being Gnostic, are very &quot;Orientalist&quot;. That's part of the &quot;occult resurgence on Europe&quot; that it's, I think, important to their identity. It's also widely known that Orientalism it's not a word that projects &quot;historical accuracy&quot;, to put it midly...<br /> <br /> SO, what if the Bhramins never cared with joining the Traditions, but the Traditions <i>went to them instead?.</i> Interested on the occult prowess of mystical orient the Traditions offered all that they could, learned what they could (filtered trough their Paradigms), and as a result Indian-inspired Practices could've been popularized among the Traditions</div> </div> </div> </div>There was one thing that the Brits gave India. Because of the fascination of the British with Ancient India the British unearthed rare manuscripts and published the Classical literature and languages of India. The first published dictionaries and grammars of Sanskrit and several other Classical languages used in ancient India were authored by the English, Scots, Welsh, and Irish. <br /> <br /> By the 1880s the Classical Literature of India and India's ancient history and knowledge base were far more widely known in India than ever before. Was that worth tens of millions of deaths from war and artificial famine? You decide. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T13:16:25-07:00","id":1539939,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539937#post1539937\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There was one thing that the Brits gave India. Because of the fascination of the British with Ancient India the British unearthed rare manuscripts and published the Classical literature and languages of India. The first published dictionaries and grammars of Sanskrit and several other Classical languages used in ancient India were authored by the English, Scots, Welsh, and Irish.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not clear that ancient Bhramins would appreciate that. But the Traditions as defined in this thread would.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By the 1880s the Classical Literature of India and India's ancient history and knowledge base were far more widely known in India than ever before. Was that worth tens of millions of deaths from war and artificial famine? You decide. </div> </div> </div>No, that's not for me to decide. I don't think so.<br /> <br /> But, personally, if I was proposed &quot;tens of millions of deaths from war and artificial famine&quot; in exchange for the *exelent* literature of my country to get &quot;more widely known&quot;. I would say NO!. NOPE. No, thank you, but no.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T13:33:15-07:00","id":1539941,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539939#post1539939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not clear that ancient Bhramins would appreciate that. But the Traditions as defined in this thread would.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> No, that's not for me to decide. I don't think so.<br /> <br /> But, personally, if I was proposed &quot;tens of millions of deaths from war and artificial famine&quot; in exchange for the *exelent* literature of my country to get &quot;more widely known&quot;. I would say NO!. NOPE. No, thank you, but no.</div> </div> </div> </div>A minor historical note. The founders of India's independence movement did delight in the recovery of India's classical literature. Until the rise of &quot;Culture Studies&quot; and &quot;Post-Colonial Theory&quot; no one connected the two items. Most scholars living in India reject the connection. Most non-Indian scholars of &quot;critical theory&quot; champion the link. <br /> <br /> I'm inclined to give the people of India the final say on their history.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T13:52:32-07:00","id":1539944,"title":"","content":"Then why'd <i>you</i> link them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T14:10:59-07:00","id":1539945,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539944#post1539944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then why'd <i>you</i> link them?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because if I didn't I might get sandbagged. I've been attacked by the Woke Police before.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T14:31:35-07:00","id":1539947,"title":"","content":"Try this idea. Both Mage:tA and Mage:t SC have said that parts of the world were excluded from the world on this side of the Gauntlet. It would seem to me that some of these excluded lands might have Mages that strenuous objections to their lands being relegated to the status of myth. <br /> <br /> Groups of the excluded might ban together to restore their nations to the consensus.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 08-06-2024, 05:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T15:40:21-07:00","id":1539951,"title":"","content":"So, anyway\u2026<br /> <br /> I am by no means as well read on South Asian history, theology, or mysticism as I\u2019d like to be so this will probably just be surface level brushes, but here are some thoughts that come to me when thinking about how irl interactions between European polities and the polities of South Asia might effect the development of the traditions.<br /> <br /> European, particularly British, imperial penetration of India was a major factor in development of 19th century occult philosophy in the European world (here taken to include euro-settler colonies outside of Europe itself). Perhaps most famous is the case of Madame Helena Blavatsky who is considered to be one of the earliest and most popular occultists to introduce concepts from \u201cFurther Asia\u201d into the milieu of Western Esotericism (prior to her, Occult Orientalism was more likely to focus on West Asia/North Africa: Persia, Turkey, Egypt, etc.). As one occult historian I follow put it in an interesting fashion, she was one of the first who could claim some firsthand experience with Indian mysticism and Buddhism, in an era where one couldn\u2019t necessarily double-check her claims via a copy of the Vedas from a local library. Therefore her peculiar idiosyncratic (and dubious understood) interpretations of Buddhism and Indian spirituality are brought into Europe and, with the phenomenal success of Theosophy, become nigh-inseparable from all later western occultism. She\u2019s kinda like Crowley in that, even if you aren\u2019t a Crowleyan, if you\u2019re in western esotericism after Crowley you\u2019re still influenced by his work; same with Blavatsky. <br /> <br /> Mage canon actually has some good examples of this: for one, the Akashic Brotherhood are a direct reference to the theosophical concept of the Akashic record. Blavatsky\u2019s fascination with Tibet is also clearly reflected in the White Wolf\u2019s writing (it\u2019s not just because of how huge Tibet was in the 90s). Plus with Crowley downstream from Blavatsky you have his massive influence over the Order of Hermes depiction and Mage in general (and downstream of Crowley you have Robert Graves and his influence over the conception of the Verbena), the list could go on. <br /> <br /> A project such as this definitely deserves a serious consideration of the myriad pre-colonization Awakened orders of South Asia, which is something I\u2019m not sufficiently familiar with to touch on too much, but there\u2019s also definitely the proliferation of adopted and appropriated materials to consider for the development of western schools of Awakened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T17:17:21-07:00","id":1539960,"title":"","content":"Someone earlier suggested abandoning the Akashayana. Instead of that, consider re-imagining then as a Tibetan Tradition \u2014 which, given the general syncretism of Eastern traditions, ended up expanding into other countries like China, India, Korea, and Japan. When re-envisioning it, try to keep the Practices intact, and only adjust the underlying philosophy. <br /> <br /> And to me, the Practice at the center of the Akashayana, Do, should actually be more about bringing out the full potential of a skill or talent: while they're best known for their martial arts, they're basically the Solar Exalts of Mage in terms of what they do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-06T19:07:05-07:00","id":1539963,"title":"","content":"The Akashayana are associated with Tibet for the same reasons as the Green Lama, Dr. Strange, and Chandru the Magician. An early New Ager back in the 1920s, I think, wrote a book called &quot;Magic and Mystery in Tibet.&quot; It was a breathless new age travel book written by a woman who spoke no Asian languages. On the plus side I don't think she was especially racist.<br /> <br /> Any way, &quot;Magic and Mystery in Tibet&quot; established the memeplex of Tibet as the land of virtuous occult knowledge. Madam Blavatsky and theosophy where there first but &quot;Magic and Mystery in Tibet&quot; made the idea mainstream and central to both pop culture and weirdly enough fashionable intellectuals from the 1920s to the 1990s.<br /> <br /> The author was Alexandra David-n\u00e9el. She was French, recognized as an explorer by the scientists of the period, and lived to almost her hundred and first birthday.<br /> <br /> Her books are available on Amazon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 08-06-2024, 10:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-11T10:28:06-07:00","id":1540175,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539934#post1539934\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Similar reasons are also why I imagine the Wu Lung being part of the Traditions earlier than the Qing's decline. Members of the Order of Hermes fled to China to escape the Curia and Order of Reason--it had a reputation as a place with deep occult traditions, they considered it a &quot;civilized country,&quot; and there's no Order of Reason or Curia there to bother them. The initial waves brought rare magical artifacts/tass/texts to the Wu Lung to basically bribe them into supporting their case for asylum, but kept in contact with their colleagues in Europe. Eventually, friendships of a kind form between the Chinese Hermetics and Wu Lung. These personal ties would damage relations between the Wu Lung and Order of Reason (sure, the Order of Hermes may be barbarians, but they're the <i>Qing's</i> barbarians now and they do not appreciate the Order of Reason trying to dictate whom they do and do not accept) and drag the Wu Lung into the Traditions even before the Century of Humiliation.</div> </div> </div> </div>China is not exactly well known for accepting foreign-isms, particularly not the kind that is the subversive type (such as individualistic free thinkers Europeans who were, worse of all, subversive even in Europe itself).<br /> <br /> I could however see those Hermetic &quot;refugees&quot; in China being behind the Taiping Rebellion, which is one of the craziest moments in history, because it was a rebellion that killed around 20-30 millions of people, roughly between 5% to 10% of the Chinese population of the time and it was caused because one guy declared himself as the Second Coming of Christ. Yeah, that's right. It was a highly syncretic variation (heresy?) of Christianism that took hold in China... See the Gnostic parallels here? - and no, I'm not saying that the Taiping Rebellion has direct links to the European Gnosticism/Hermeticism, just that it has it reassembles it in it syncretism and a loose Christian background, but in game terms it could well mean that this was in fact caused by a &quot;Hermetic infection&quot; in China.<br /> <br /> If that was the case thou, the Wu Lung would absolutely HATE the Hermetics, and this would only actually happen at the beginning of the Century of Humiliation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-11T10:58:13-07:00","id":1540176,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539960#post1539960\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Someone earlier suggested abandoning the Akashayana. Instead of that, consider re-imagining then as a Tibetan Tradition \u2014 which, given the general syncretism of Eastern traditions, ended up expanding into other countries like China, India, Korea, and Japan. When re-envisioning it, try to keep the Practices intact, and only adjust the underlying philosophy.<br /> <br /> And to me, the Practice at the center of the Akashayana, Do, should actually be more about bringing out the full potential of a skill or talent: while they're best known for their martial arts, they're basically the Solar Exalts of Mage in terms of what they do.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539963#post1539963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Akashayana are associated with Tibet for the same reasons as the Green Lama, Dr. Strange, and Chandru the Magician. An early New Ager back in the 1920s, I think, wrote a book called &quot;Magic and Mystery in Tibet.&quot; It was a breathless new age travel book written by a woman who spoke no Asian languages. On the plus side I don't think she was especially racist.<br /> <br /> Any way, &quot;Magic and Mystery in Tibet&quot; established the memeplex of Tibet as the land of virtuous occult knowledge. Madam Blavatsky and theosophy where there first but &quot;Magic and Mystery in Tibet&quot; made the idea mainstream and central to both pop culture and weirdly enough fashionable intellectuals from the 1920s to the 1990s.<br /> <br /> The author was Alexandra David-n\u00e9el. She was French, recognized as an explorer by the scientists of the period, and lived to almost her hundred and first birthday.<br /> <br /> Her books are available on Amazon.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly this. As it is, MtA have 4 major problems: the Order of Hermes, the Celestial Chorus, the Ahl-I-Batin and the Brotherhood of Akasha. Not the only ones, but those are the major offenders. And of those, the OoH are the least problematic - and that's because they ARE the representatives of real world &quot;gnosticism&quot; (under quotes so that, again, it is not mistaken with the 1st century christian heresy, but rather syncretic occultist &quot;movement&quot; throughout history). As for the rest, the Celestial Chorus is basically the &quot;Theosophical Society of the West&quot;, the Ahl-I-Batin are the &quot;Theosophical Society of the Middle East&quot; and the Akashics are the &quot;Theosophical Society of the East&quot;.<br /> <br /> It is... Asinine. Sadly, it is the only term that fits. It is just shallow, and it seems more like an attempt to shoehorn D&amp;D &quot;classes&quot; into &quot;kinds of Mages&quot; with a very 1990's view.<br /> <br /> The Order of Hermes are the &quot;classical Wizards&quot; of D&amp;D<br /> Celestial Chorus are the &quot;Clerics&quot;<br /> Eutanathoi are &quot;Necromancers&quot;<br /> Dreamspeakers are &quot;Shamans&quot;<br /> Verbena are &quot;Druids&quot;<br /> The Cult of Ecstasy are &quot;Divinators&quot;<br /> The Akashics are the &quot;Monks&quot;<br /> <br /> And even the other 2 are &quot;Technomancers&quot;, with the Etherites being the &quot;classic&quot; Technomancers while the VA are more of a 90's thing with movies like... Bah, I forgot the name, but it is a movie upon which the guy enters (physically) inside of a Virtual Reality world that he created, and there's a sort of &quot;civil war&quot; inside where the programs want to defeat the &quot;users&quot; (creators) and invade and conquer the real world. It's an awesome movie by the way, and the most VA thing ever, both in aesthetics and ideas, so much so that the VAs seem like a carbon copy of it, but I can't remember the name of it because my brain has been hijacked with the name &quot;Ultron&quot;, that Marvel garbage that unfortunately took the space in my memory. Sorry, some of you may know it.<br /> <br /> Anyway, that was the basic premise of the game, and it was that shallow of a concept, from which they tried to make the world fit into, rather than going the other way around and try to construct it organically based on what the world truly is.<br /> <br /> The Akashics are an almost cartoonish depiction of Wuxia Kung Fu movies and the most shallow (and erroneous) Theosophical views of the mystical Shangri-La land of the Tibet.<br /> <br /> While Tibet could harbor a Zen Buddhist tibetan Craft, it would only ever be a minor Craft in a &quot;realistic&quot; setting, even minor than Japanese Shintoist Crafts.<br /> <br /> Sorry, but the &quot;mystical Tibet&quot; holds as much real importance as El Dorado or Camelot. It is just a fairy tale about some &quot;Shangri-La&quot; that doesn't exist.<br /> <br /> In fact, and this is something that bothers many of the same woke police, is that Tibet was an awful totalitarian Theocracy and a backward oppressive nation. Not that the Chinese genocide of the Tibetan people and culture is any better thou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-11T11:08:15-07:00","id":1540177,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1539951#post1539951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, anyway\u2026<br /> <br /> I am by no means as well read on South Asian history, theology, or mysticism as I\u2019d like to be so this will probably just be surface level brushes, but here are some thoughts that come to me when thinking about how irl interactions between European polities and the polities of South Asia might effect the development of the traditions.<br /> <br /> European, particularly British, imperial penetration of India was a major factor in development of 19th century occult philosophy in the European world (here taken to include euro-settler colonies outside of Europe itself). Perhaps most famous is the case of Madame Helena Blavatsky who is considered to be one of the earliest and most popular occultists to introduce concepts from \u201cFurther Asia\u201d into the milieu of Western Esotericism (prior to her, Occult Orientalism was more likely to focus on West Asia/North Africa: Persia, Turkey, Egypt, etc.). As one occult historian I follow put it in an interesting fashion, she was one of the first who could claim some firsthand experience with Indian mysticism and Buddhism, in an era where one couldn\u2019t necessarily double-check her claims via a copy of the Vedas from a local library. Therefore her peculiar idiosyncratic (and dubious understood) interpretations of Buddhism and Indian spirituality are brought into Europe and, with the phenomenal success of Theosophy, become nigh-inseparable from all later western occultism. She\u2019s kinda like Crowley in that, even if you aren\u2019t a Crowleyan, if you\u2019re in western esotericism after Crowley you\u2019re still influenced by his work; same with Blavatsky.<br /> <br /> Mage canon actually has some good examples of this: for one, the Akashic Brotherhood are a direct reference to the theosophical concept of the Akashic record. Blavatsky\u2019s fascination with Tibet is also clearly reflected in the White Wolf\u2019s writing (it\u2019s not just because of how huge Tibet was in the 90s). Plus with Crowley downstream from Blavatsky you have his massive influence over the Order of Hermes depiction and Mage in general (and downstream of Crowley you have Robert Graves and his influence over the conception of the Verbena), the list could go on.<br /> <br /> A project such as this definitely deserves a serious consideration of the myriad pre-colonization Awakened orders of South Asia, which is something I\u2019m not sufficiently familiar with to touch on too much, but there\u2019s also definitely the proliferation of adopted and appropriated materials to consider for the development of western schools of Awakened.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is also important to remember that the Theosophical ideas of Madame Blavatsky would also be merged in the weirdest and most lunatic ways with Nietzschean ideas and Social Darwinism to create the awful ideas of Nazism. That was actually the reasoning behind my idea of having WW2 being an &quot;unholy alliance&quot;, where mystics and technocrats united under (secret) Nefandic influence to form Nazi Germany and control Imperial Japan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-11T12:23:36-07:00","id":1540178,"title":"","content":"The problem is still, M:tA is a blast. These reworkings are about something most people aren't into. About the only way to move in the direction you seem to want is to massively change the Council structure. Instead of nine major factions in a unified Council something far more like a merger of the Council and the Disparates. Just assume that the politics are byzantine and bloodthirsty and nothing ever gets done. While this would be realistic, and not just for a certain value of realism, it does not seem gamable. <br /> <br /> A Council of 5 to 12 factions would be more functional. As to the metaphysics, can you even clearly define that word so that others know what you're talking about? Most philosophy teachers can't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-11T12:44:52-07:00","id":1540180,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540175#post1540175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> China is not exactly well known for accepting foreign-isms, particularly not the kind that is the subversive type (such as individualistic free thinkers Europeans who were, worse of all, subversive even in Europe itself).</div> </div> </div> </div>That's the stereotype. <a href=\"https://old.reddit.com/r/AskHistorians/comments/k3v019/why_did_the_ottoman_empire_decline/ge7v60a/\" target=\"_blank\">It's not true, though</a>. At the time the Ming were looking outwards and was relatively welcoming of Europeans and had <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesuit_missions_in_China\" target=\"_blank\">fairly cordial exchanges with the Jesuits</a>, though relations with the Catholic Church would eventually degenerate. Use this as a base. During the 15th and 16th centuries, the Order of Hermes, Curia, and Order of Reason are all active in China. During the same time the Curia and OoR are splitting with the Order of Hermes, their relations with the Ming are increasingly tense. And, as the link I provided discusses, there's every reason to think the Ming and Qing governments--thoroughly intertwined with the Wu Lung--would be <i>very</i> interested in getting some European occultists serve as advisors, especially for anything having to do with the Order of Reason or the Curia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-11T13:58:34-07:00","id":1540182,"title":"","content":"Trying to map Traditions to spellcasters from D&amp;D tends to fall apart rapidly upon any analysis beyond the surface. Not really the most important tangent, I admit, but it's there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540176#post1540176\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Order of Hermes are the &quot;classical Wizards&quot; of D&amp;D</div> </div> </div> </div>That mostly tracks, but still not entirely, since the adoption of Enochian gives them a very heavy religious angle that Wizards only have if the campaign world requires wizards and specialists to worship a god of magic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Celestial Chorus are the &quot;Clerics&quot; </div> </div> </div>They would fit into 4e's Divine power source somewhat snugly, but in other editions, you'd have to go far afield to detail the class or combination of classes that they might be, including Avengers of various sorts, Paladins, Crusaders, and custom priests of which there are about a hundred (no exaggeration, the books even admit this in later 2e). They wouldn't just be Bishop Turpin clones crossed with Van Helsing (the original cleric was custom-made to whoop up on the vampire PC Sir Fang in a fit of adolescent passive-aggressiveness regrettably exhibited by adults).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Eutanathoi are &quot;Necromancers&quot; </div> </div> </div>Not even close. Euthanatoi don't typically go around raising skeletons and trying to, say, cheat mortality or kill death (Voormas is the exception that proves the rule). They're more like priests of a TN death god, which in later editions shakes out to being priests of the Raven Queen, which are a rather far cry from any type of Necromancer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Dreamspeakers are &quot;Shamans&quot; </div> </div> </div>There are probably a lot of even less savory, even more stereotypical kinds of D&amp;D spellcasters that Dreamspeakers would be stuck with that don't usually get PC time, mostly in older editions. Alternately, they could be preservers from Athas, or take the next spot from...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Verbena are &quot;Druids&quot;<br /> </div> </div> </div>Verbena fall out as more of an odd kind of wizard in D&amp;D terms (from 2e on, the Transmuter specialist stands out prominently). In 4e, they even drop the last fig leaf and just have a witch sub-class of the wizard. They're kind of like another side of the coin of the Hermetics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Cult of Ecstasy are &quot;Divinators&quot; </div> </div> </div>The CoE aren't allowed anywhere near the sanitized, family-friendly brand of D&amp;D, but some of them might have something in common with Illusionists, given their approaches (when Sahajiya aren't messing with Time they're messing with Mind, and Illusionists in 1e had a lot of other specialist spells too).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Akashics are the &quot;Monks&quot;<br /> </div> </div> </div>Monks can't really be reasonably described as classic wizards of D&amp;D, as they were only spellcasters for part of one editions (2e), which folded them into the priest class for two or three supplements; they were then separated out in the Scarlet Brotherhood book in '99 to more closely resemble their 1e non-caster version.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 08-11-2024, 06:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-12T08:57:10-07:00","id":1540209,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540180#post1540180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's the stereotype. <a href=\"https://old.reddit.com/r/AskHistorians/comments/k3v019/why_did_the_ottoman_empire_decline/ge7v60a/\" target=\"_blank\">It's not true, though</a>. At the time the Ming were looking outwards and was relatively welcoming of Europeans and had <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesuit_missions_in_China\" target=\"_blank\">fairly cordial exchanges with the Jesuits</a>, though relations with the Catholic Church would eventually degenerate. Use this as a base. During the 15th and 16th centuries, the Order of Hermes, Curia, and Order of Reason are all active in China. During the same time the Curia and OoR are splitting with the Order of Hermes, their relations with the Ming are increasingly tense. And, as the link I provided discusses, there's every reason to think the Ming and Qing governments--thoroughly intertwined with the Wu Lung--would be <i>very</i> interested in getting some European occultists serve as advisors, especially for anything having to do with the Order of Reason or the Curia.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. Just like Japan was initially open, then got absolutely closed, then opened again and then turned insane.<br /> <br /> That might very well be possible, but then who would they blame for the Taiping Rebellion? The Curia? The OoR? The OoH? The answer to that would definitely tell where the Wu Lung will end up landing (and that would be in the exact opposite side of the rebels). I don't really see the Curia being behind such a &quot;heresy&quot;, but I'm agnostic towards it being caused to either Hermetic or Masonic(OoR) influence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-12T09:33:56-07:00","id":1540211,"title":"","content":"It's possible that the Taiping Rebellion just <i>happened</i>, not due to any particular group of mages, and everyone was taken fairly aback by it. The reaction European powers had towards the war--siding with the Qing--would suggest that their affiliated mage societies weren't responsible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-13T13:08:29-07:00","id":1540285,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Citrakayah</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540211#post1540211\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's possible that the Taiping Rebellion just <i>happened</i>, not due to any particular group of mages, and everyone was taken fairly aback by it. The reaction European powers had towards the war--siding with the Qing--would suggest that their affiliated mage societies weren't responsible.</div> </div> </div> </div>Uhh... How about... Nefandi?! Lol. Sure, I'm going crazy here, but crazy is fun hehe","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-13T14:15:54-07:00","id":1540295,"title":"","content":"South America doesn't get much love in this sort of thing. They've multiple native traditions. And every nation in Latin America has a distinctive syncretic tradition of occult practice. Some nations like Brazil and Mexico have multiple such traditions. Even the whole &quot;colossus of the North&quot; rhetoric aimed at the USA started well before even the Mexican American War. And it involves ideas of mystic destiny and the crushing of Protestant America in honor of Holy Mother Church. <br /> <br /> If you're rewriting the Council and all the other major groups, Latin America is to rich and weird an Area to ignore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-14T07:52:46-07:00","id":1540356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540295#post1540295\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">South America doesn't get much love in this sort of thing. They've multiple native traditions. And every nation in Latin America has a distinctive syncretic tradition of occult practice. Some nations like Brazil and Mexico have multiple such traditions. Even the whole &quot;colossus of the North&quot; rhetoric aimed at the USA started well before even the Mexican American War. And it involves ideas of mystic destiny and the crushing of Protestant America in honor of Holy Mother Church.<br /> <br /> If you're rewriting the Council and all the other major groups, Latin America is to rich and weird an Area to ignore.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd also like to see what there is to join the council or disparates early on. Much of what you described doesn't arise until later on in Mage history.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-14T14:49:51-07:00","id":1540387,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540356#post1540356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd also like to see what there is to join the council or disparates early on. Much of what you described doesn't arise until later on in Mage history.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe they could be a faction of their own. La Raza Cosmica (I bet I misspelled that) is seen, even in Mexico, as a book that both denounced racism and strongly promoted racism. If we say that Vasconcelos was expressing ideals and contradictions that had long been part of the occult communities of Latin America (which seems to be either accurate or very close) it makes an interesting mixture of radical egalitarian ideals with reactionary impulses. Perfect for a ambiguous Mage faction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 08-14-2024, 06:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-20T10:02:27-07:00","id":1540787,"title":"","content":"&quot;La Raza Cosmica&quot;. Never heard of it, but considering it's Wikipedia page, I wouldn't make it the foundation of a new faction. Much less one &quot;made for LATAM&quot;. <br /> <br /> It has some racist concepts that <i>seem </i>imported from USA, such as the color theory (AFAIK. Never heard of a &quot;red race&quot; outside weird USA stuff).<br /> <br /> As someone from Argentina it's the first time I've heard anything like that 4 colour races outside of weird &quot;aliens-exist&quot; racists.<br /> Spanish-America never had an Apartheid, and so &quot;autochtonous racism&quot; doesn't manifest as much as a strict &quot;division of primeval races&quot; here, but rather as a gradient whith layers, creating a more complex &quot;tree&quot; of races than what wikipedia adscribes to the theory.<br /> <br /> AND, wierdly - or perhaps not so much considering the color theory - with the (very religious) &quot;manifest destiny&quot; idea, that's <i>much </i>more an USA thing than a LATAM thing. Not sure of every country, but when people here goes &quot;we're the greatest country ever&quot;, it's either ironical, bemoaning past glories, or talking about nationalist politics about natural resources and stuff. Never heard anyone saying we're superior &quot;as a race&quot;.<br /> <br /> <i>I've heard on my region</i> that we're not a race but &quot;people of a country&quot;, with the &quot;crisol de razas&quot; thing (that was also invented in the North, but we adopted as an idea), with the naive connotation that we're better because that makes us not racist - which it's patently false (at most, one could say that it's better than Apartheid). But that idea of an extra, superior, race forming seems to defeat the point, and that's the <i>closest </i>thing I can relate with as something that could've been inspired<br /> <br /> IDK, maybe it's more popular on Mexico or other countries. The guy who wrote it was Mexican after all.<br /> And probably Wikipedia it's butchering the book.<br /> <br /> But unless the page it's *very* wrong, I would advice against using any source that would flatten the native ethnic groups of the continent to a &quot;red race&quot;?.<br /> It doesn't fell very representative.<br /> <br /> Maybe go for a group of Traditionalists if u' re positive that &quot;LATAM occultists&quot; take inspiration (Not those I've known, tho) <hr />But if you ask me:<br /> <br /> LATAM it's very, VERY, Christian. Once I heard that Brazil has like 90% of it's population being christian. May be an exaggeration, not sure about the numbers, really, and in any case it would be the most extreme case. Still, <i>strong Cuira pressence</i> should be an axiom.<br /> <br /> And that would be probably the only thing I would say that goes against &quot;current Mage&quot;. It's absurd to think that the Technocracy would have a larger influence than the Curia-things here, even when everyone uses cars and stuff.<br /> <br /> But other than that I think that the dynamics of the Traditions and the Disparate remain more or less appropiate. There are native religions that were crushed, pursued and had to go into obscurity by colonialism. There are &quot;underdog&quot; religions figthing for their own place against materialism and the mainstream religion. Both go into the Disparate really well. And there are masons, and similar occult orders to make for &quot;Traditions&quot;<br /> <br /> About &quot;is too rich and weird an Area&quot;. I mean, is it really much <i>weirder </i>than the rest?, ANGAM doesn't have natives with cultures that were crushed and forced into obscurity, weird christians, and stuff brought by African slaves?. I guess Mexico and &quot;below&quot; DO have more famous ruins of ancient empires, so there could be something about it being richer.<br /> <br /> Here, rather than to make a faction, it could be a good idea to start thinking about &quot;Camps&quot; for the Factions that go beyond &quot;we want to survive&quot;: For instance, there could be a whole factions of Disparate and/or Traditionalists trying to recover the ancient magicks of the Aztecs and Maya on Mexico.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-21-2024, 12:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-20T23:45:13-07:00","id":1540822,"title":"","content":"I never said Latin America was weirder. I merely pointed out that there's a rich untapped supply of weird available in Latin America.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-21T00:23:30-07:00","id":1540824,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540787#post1540787\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As someone from Argentina...</div> </div> </div> </div>Ola hermano! Cheers from Brazil. Always a pleasure to deal with your... Kind... Our closest kindred spirits, our beloved brothers that we Brazilians love to hate. Nice to know there's one Argentinian here, always a pleasure.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Spanish-America never had an Apartheid, and so &quot;autochtonous racism&quot; doesn't manifest as much as a strict &quot;division of primeval races&quot; here, but rather as a gradient whith layers, creating a more complex &quot;tree&quot; of races than what wikipedia adscribes to the theory. </div> </div> </div>Yeah such is also the case in Brazil too.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> AND, wierdly - or perhaps not so much considering the color theory - with the (very religious) &quot;manifest destiny&quot; idea, that's <i>much </i>more an USA thing than a LATAM thing. Not sure of every country, but when people here goes &quot;we're the greatest country ever&quot;, it's either ironical, bemoaning past glories, or talking about nationalist politcs about natural resources and stuff. Never heard anyone saying we're superior &quot;as a race&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Yup. That also holds very true here in Brazil too. Although lately, it's only ever been used ironically.<br /> <br /> And we Brazilians don't see ourselves as a &quot;race&quot;, just as people from many different races that happens to have been born in these lands.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>I've heard on my region</i> that we're not a race but &quot;people of a country&quot;, with the &quot;crisol de razas&quot; thing (that was also invented in the North, but we adopted as an idea), with the naive connotation that we're better because that makes us not racist - which it's patently false (at most, one could say that it's better than Apartheid). But that idea of an extra, superior, race forming seems to defeat the point, and that's the <i>closest </i>thing I can relate with as something that could've been inspired </div> </div> </div>What does &quot;crisol&quot; means? That's one word in Spanish that I never heard before. Anyway, yeah, you're mostly right.<br /> <br /> There is one exception that I can think of thou. Well, two exceptions.<br /> <br /> The first one would be Solano Lopez and his father which I forgot the name, anyway, Solano Lopez father was the first dictator of Paraguay as soon as it got independent. And Solano Lopez was the crazy dictator that managed the genius achievement of going to war against the two most powerful nations of South America at the same time, Brazil and Argentina (and also Uruguay) and managing to obliterate his own country in the process (losing at least 40% of the entire male population of Paraguay in the process).<br /> <br /> Well, the crazies of Paraguay had indeed have an ideology of a sort of racial superiority of the &quot;mestizo&quot; (mixed).<br /> <br /> But we can ignore this because they were a lunatic dictatorship, and Solano Lopez was basically the South America Hitler of the 19th century. Therefore, he was basically a fascist &quot;before fascism was cool&quot;.<br /> <br /> The second exception would be certain fascist movements of the beginning of the 20th century, at least here in Brazil. In here we had something called &quot;Integralism&quot;, which had several ideas of &quot;indigenism', posited that we Brazilians should speak Tupi-Guarani as a national language rather than Portuguese and that our mixed characteristics made us a &quot;superior race&quot; and other such nonsense.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> LATAM it's very, VERY, Christian. Once I heard that Brazil has like 90% of it's population being christian. May be an exaggeration, not sure about the numbers, really, and in any case it would be the most extreme case. Still, <i>strong Cuira pressence</i> should be an axiom. </div> </div> </div>No, it's no exaggeration. It is true. Yes, Brazilians are - mostly - 90% christians. On the surface at least. The reality is always a bit more complex than simple black and white yes or no answers.<br /> <br /> Most people in Brazil believe in God - and to be specific, that &quot;God&quot; is not Zeus or Odin or Allah. Yeah, the vast majority of Brazilians believe in Jesus Christ. I'm fervently atheist, but I'm the exception of exceptions.<br /> <br /> Brazil is the largest Catholic country in the world. It has been a very very long time ever since the beating heart of catholicism no longer is in Italy, but rather in Brazil. I'd say that the Curia don't even hide in the Vatican any longer, but rather in S\u00e3o Paulo and Rio de Janeiro.<br /> <br /> That been said thou, whenever someone in Brazil declares themselves as &quot;Catholics&quot;, that means almost as someone that agrees &quot;yeah yeah, Jesus is the life and the truth alright, now let me move on with my life&quot;. All those millions of &quot;Catholics&quot; we have are pretty low on religiosity.<br /> <br /> The Evangelicals on the other hand are pretty fanaticals. Despite being a minority, they are a growing minority and a very vocal one.<br /> <br /> And besides those, we have... Hmm... We could call those as &quot;pseudo-christians&quot;. Technically christians, but not really... Because those would be some of the Syncretisms. The religions from African origin for instance - Candombl\u00e9 and Umbanda. And the reason why I call them &quot;pseudo-christians&quot; is because that, if you ask them, they'll say they pray to Jesus Christ, sure, but they also believe in a Pantheon of Spirits (yes, spirits, not &quot;Gods&quot;) - Xango, Eshu, Yemanja and so on. My wife is from Umbanda so I know a lot about that - such an irony that an atheist raised by a communist father would end up marrying such a religious person but here I am.<br /> <br /> And there's also a few other &quot;heresies&quot;, here in Brazil for instance we have &quot;Spiritism&quot;, which believes in Reincarnation and past lives and some such, clearly some eastern Syncretisms, but if you ask, they'll declare themselves as &quot;Christians&quot;, and they believe in Jesus and whatnot, when in practice their belief system reassembles more Gnosticism or something like it (oh yes, Spiritism could be a good background for Hermetics here in Brazil).<br /> <br /> And what's more interesting, particularly for those in Candombl\u00e9, Umbanda and Spiritism, is that those are somewhat... Interchangeable. You can find some people that will &quot;incorporate&quot; the spirits of Eshu and Xango on Monday only to receive the communion of the priest in the Catholic church on Sunday.<br /> <br /> The Evangelicals no, they are much more strict about it. Once one turns evangelical, the person immediately denounces the whole of Spiritism, Candombl\u00e9 and Umbanda as &quot;satanic&quot; (and even Catholicism itself as at least borderline satanic).<br /> <br /> Oh, the Catholic priests wouldn't appreciate the &quot;spiritual incorporations&quot; of their competitors either, but they are not so eager to summon the Devil at all the time thou.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And that would be probably the only thing I would say that goes against &quot;current Mage&quot;. It's absurd to think that the Technocracy would have a larger influence than the Curia-things here, even when everyone uses cars and stuff. </div> </div> </div>Nah. Here in Brazil in particular, if there's a place upon which the Curia would cling to power, that would be here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But other than that I think that the dynamics of the Traditions and the Disparate remain more or less appropiate. There are native religions that were crushed, pursued and had to go into obscurity by colonialism. There are &quot;underdog&quot; religions figthing for their own place against materialism and the mainstream religion. Both go into the Disparate really well. And there are masons, and similar occult orders to make for &quot;Traditions&quot;<br /> <br /> About &quot;is too rich and weird an Area&quot;. I mean, is it really much <i>weirder </i>than the rest?, ANGAM doesn't have natives with cultures that were crushed and forced into obscurity, weird christians, and stuff brought by African slaves?. I guess Mexico and &quot;below&quot; DO have more famous ruins of ancient empires, so there could be something about it being richer.<br /> <br /> Here, rather than to make a faction, it could be a good idea to start thinking about &quot;Camps&quot; for the Factions that go beyond &quot;we want to survive&quot;: For instance, there could be a whole factions of Disparate and/or Traditionalists trying to recover the ancient magicks of the Aztecs and Maya on Mexico. </div> </div> </div>Or the typical northern obsession about the Amazonic forest. I mean, here for us it is just a green hellhole, but Americans and Europeans think that it is some sort of mysterious Shangri-La Wonderland. Which is why there are special kinds of unicorns in WoD, mostly in WtA, just around it<br /> <br /> (Funnily enough, the Amazonic Forest would probably be more interesting to the Technocracy than to any Tradition/Disparate around).<br /> <br /> Brazil in particular is MORE urbanized than any European or North American country. We Brazilians live compressed in a dozen of cities at the coast, while everything else is pretty much devoid of people. There's plenty ground for Traditions/Disparates here, but not much in the sense of finding some mythical Wonderland (and no, the Amazon is NOT it).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-22T09:47:36-07:00","id":1540896,"title":"","content":"It's important to note:<br /> <br /> Here in Brazil the biggest religious minority after Christian churches is the African-Catholic syncretism, but we do have indigenous religions, too.<br /> <br /> The problem with those is that not only are they a minority, in their own territories they've been mostly supplanted by Christian churches and have been persecuted by those.<br /> <br /> Your regular indigenous holy place or person is at constant threat of violence and destruction by other indigenous people that became fervorous Christians.<br /> <br /> About 'Spiritism', it is Kardecism, a religious movement of I think European origin. Technically they can be non-christians, but I'm yet to meet such an exception.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-27T10:12:51-07:00","id":1541068,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540824#post1540824\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What does &quot;crisol&quot; means? That's one word in Spanish that I never heard before. Anyway, yeah, you're mostly right.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's an item: crucible on english , google says it's cadinho on Portuguese. A melting pot.<br /> <br /> In the context would imply that we're a people made by the fusion of several races and cultures. I would say it's what happens naturally when you don't have a strong divide between races and/or ethnic groups<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, it's no exaggeration. It is true. Yes, Brazilians are - mostly - 90% christians. On the surface at least. The reality is always a bit more complex than simple black and white yes or no answers.<br /> <br /> Most people in Brazil believe in God - and to be specific, that &quot;God&quot; is not Zeus or Odin or Allah. Yeah, the vast majority of Brazilians believe in Jesus Christ. I'm fervently atheist, but I'm the exception of exceptions.<br /> <br /> Brazil is the largest Catholic country in the world. It has been a very very long time ever since the beating heart of catholicism no longer is in Italy, but rather in Brazil. I'd say that the Curia don't even hide in the Vatican any longer, but rather in S\u00e3o Paulo and Rio de Janeiro.<br /> <br /> That been said thou, whenever someone in Brazil declares themselves as &quot;Catholics&quot;, that means almost as someone that agrees &quot;yeah yeah, Jesus is the life and the truth alright, now let me move on with my life&quot;. All those millions of &quot;Catholics&quot; we have are pretty low on religiosity.<br /> <br /> The Evangelicals on the other hand are pretty fanaticals. Despite being a minority, they are a growing minority and a very vocal one. </div> </div> </div>Damn, 90% ! . God, if informed by the Consensus, trully is brazilean <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The Evangelics are also quite strong in Argentina, in spite of our long Catholic tradition. They're very *energic*, and seems to atract the youth a lot more than the more elaborate Catholic rituals<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And besides those, we have... Hmm... We could call those as &quot;pseudo-christians&quot;. Technically christians, but not really... Because those would be some of the Syncretisms. The religions from African origin for instance - Candombl\u00e9 and Umbanda. And the reason why I call them &quot;pseudo-christians&quot; is because that, if you ask them, they'll say they pray to Jesus Christ, sure, but they also believe in a Pantheon of Spirits (yes, spirits, not &quot;Gods&quot;) - Xango, Eshu, Yemanja and so on. My wife is from Umbanda so I know a lot about that - such an irony that an atheist raised by a communist father would end up marrying such a religious person but here I am.<br /> <br /> And there's also a few other &quot;heresies&quot;, here in Brazil for instance we have &quot;Spiritism&quot;, which believes in Reincarnation and past lives and some such, clearly some eastern Syncretisms, but if you ask, they'll declare themselves as &quot;Christians&quot;, and they believe in Jesus and whatnot, when in practice their belief system reassembles more Gnosticism or something like it (oh yes, Spiritism could be a good background for Hermetics here in Brazil).<br /> <br /> And what's more interesting, particularly for those in Candombl\u00e9, Umbanda and Spiritism, is that those are somewhat... Interchangeable. You can find some people that will &quot;incorporate&quot; the spirits of Eshu and Xango on Monday only to receive the communion of the priest in the Catholic church on Sunday. </div> </div> </div>Very cool.<br /> <br /> I don't know a lot of howminoritary religions work on Argentina. One curious phenomenon, tough, it's that of the worship &quot;folk saints&quot;. Saints not recognized by the church, but by the people. Such as <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_La_Muerte\" target=\"_blank\">San La Muerte</a>. I'm not sure if this is Santeria or not, (I've heard often called such, but I've never heard the word &quot;Orisha&quot;, or anything Cuban like that, related to it)<br /> <br /> An interesting case, on Buenos Aires, and some other places of the Country, worship of &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauchito_Gil\" target=\"_blank\">El Gauchito Gil</a>&quot; it's common enough that it's regular to find random shrines on the city and the contryside.<br /> <br /> This is one interesting case because it's surge in popuilarity it's recent, and it's a good example of how Religion and Power relate. In the Wikipedia page it references how &quot;devout truck drivers&quot; spread the worship of this &quot;outlaw saint&quot;, I can provide a little more context:<br /> The truck driver's Union it's among the most powerful unions of the country, and even some important figures of the union have expressed some degree of belief on this saint. While not the only outlaw saint to have widespread devotion, this one has very strong support. It's very common to find shrines on roads all over the country, this wasn't so just two decades ago.<br /> <br /> Evangelics are no fun like always and have recently started to demolish some of the shrines. But I don't think there's any chance for them to bring the cult into obscurity any time soon.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or the typical northern obsession about the Amazonic forest. I mean, here for us it is just a green hellhole, but Americans and Europeans think that it is some sort of mysterious Shangri-La Wonderland. Which is why there are special kinds of unicorns in WoD, mostly in WtA, just around it<br /> <br /> (Funnily enough, the Amazonic Forest would probably be more interesting to the Technocracy than to any Tradition/Disparate around). </div> </div> </div>Yeah <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> . Conversely Argentina it's the country of &quot;cowboys&quot;, llamas (only on the North-West), and palm trees (did WW believe Argentina it's a lush tropical coutry?) - canon.<br /> <br /> Truth be told, I think that giving ground to the weird, but cool, legends, it's also a valid way to re-imagine Mage. You could take a page from old Mage and imagine the world it's full of mythical places like El Dorado, or the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_the_Caesars\" target=\"_blank\">City of the Caesars</a><br /> <br /> On the context of this thread the effort seems mainly to bring the Mage-verse closer to real-life religions and cultures, and this has no place there. I think it would require a paradigm-shift to the zanny and unreal to get there more properly. But perhaps you could have camps within the factions that take interest on all the places that don't exist, that exist only in fiction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-27-2024, 01:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-29T15:41:03-07:00","id":1541214,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1540896#post1540896\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> About 'Spiritism', it is Kardecism, a religious movement of I think European origin. Technically they can be non-christians, but I'm yet to meet such an exception.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not as familiar with Kardecism as with Umbanda, but I thought that the &quot;prophets&quot; of Spiritism were all Brazilian","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-29T16:15:27-07:00","id":1541215,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1541068#post1541068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's an item: crucible on english , google says it's cadinho on Portuguese. A melting pot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, I see. Cadinho.... Hmm... I believe I never heard that word before, or I've heard it long long ago, in any case it is old Portuguese or it is Portuguese from Portugal only. We don't use it in Brazil. But yeah, I get it now.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In the context would imply that we're a people made by the fusion of several races and cultures. I would say it's what happens naturally when you don't have a strong divide between races and/or ethnic groups </div> </div> </div>Yeah, now there's some pretty interesting recent genetic studies on Brazilian populations that show a lot of Mitochondrial DNA amongst Brazilians comes from natives, which shows that our natives were NOT exterminated... WE are the natives. It just so happens that our ancestors decided to enter the Portuguese villages, learned Portuguese, got baptized and adopted Portuguese names. Heck, we even have documents proving that. For example, during colonial times, the Baron of Niteroi, a big city close to Rio de Janeiro, was the descendant of a native tribal leader that became a subject of the Portuguese crown in the 16th century. <br /> <br /> As a matter of fact, the natives actually drove the Portuguese into their wars, allying with Portuguese, French and Dutch in order to fight one another.<br /> <br /> Basically every single Brazilian is a degree of mixed European, African and Native American, no matter the tone of skin.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Damn, 90% ! . God, if informed by the Consensus, trully is brazilean <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> </div> </div> </div>Yes, it is something around 90% of Christians, with tiny minorities outside of it, but again, out of those 90% some 70% are Catholics, but these so called &quot;Catholics&quot; are very unreligious. Many Brazilians pay &quot;lip service&quot; to their &quot;Catholic faith&quot;, but despite that they still are culturally influenced by it.<br /> <br /> The 30% Evangelicals are much much more strict in their adherence to Christian ideals.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know a lot of howminoritary religions work on Argentina. One curious phenomenon, tough, it's that of the worship &quot;folk saints&quot;. Saints not recognized by the church, but by the people. Such as <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_La_Muerte\" target=\"_blank\">San La Muerte</a>. I'm not sure if this is Santeria or not, (I've heard often called such, but I've never heard the word &quot;Orisha&quot;, or anything Cuban like that, related to it) </div> </div> </div>\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bIt sounds like Santeria, yes. &quot;Holy Death&quot; thou sounds more like the Mexican syncretic traditions coming from the Nahuatl natives. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> An interesting case, on Buenos Aires, and some other places of the Country, worship of &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauchito_Gil\" target=\"_blank\">El Gauchito Gil</a>&quot; it's common enough that it's regular to find random shrines on the city and the contryside. </div> </div> </div>That's also common in Brazil as it was in Medieval Europe. It seems to be a common feature of Catholicism in rural areas more prone to superstitious beliefs.<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-29T17:39:41-07:00","id":1541218,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1539224-rethinking-the-ascension-war?p=1541214#post1541214\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not as familiar with Kardecism as with Umbanda, but I thought that the &quot;prophets&quot; of Spiritism were all Brazilian</div> </div> </div> </div>My mother is Kardecist, so I know it well. Though I do admit I needed to google it now just to be sure of the exact country, Allan Kardec started it in France.<br /> <br /> I think the word 'cadinho' is still used here, actually, but we don't talk much about crucibles to begin with, do we? But I have a friend that's a jeweler, I did heard him talking about the 'cadinho'.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-31T17:47:44-07:00","id":1541311,"title":"","content":"Here's a nice video about... Well... Almost all religions.<br /> <br /> The channel also has a video on Western Esotericism.<br /> <br /> It shows the &quot;family three&quot; of all religions and their main denominations, it is very well detailed and explained, and it shows their historical contexts and many more.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/nxhSOcyPCVo?si=a2dTkxi9ODD1-Qwo\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/nxhSOcyPCVo?si=a2dTkxi9ODD1-Qwo\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/nxhSOcyPCVo?si=a2dTkxi9ODD1-Qwo\" target=\"_blank\"> https://youtu.be/nxhSOcyPCVo?si=a2dTkxi9ODD1-Qwo </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I think this could be a good starting point for this discussion.<br /> <br /> Oh yes, I just remembered that we pretty much completely forgotten about Buddhism, since there's no single good Tradition to represent such a major religion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://fo